window.PHYSICS_CURRICULUM_11 = [{"id":"unitsmeasurement11","index":0,"name":"Units and Measurement","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 1","emoji":"📏","palette":{"from":"#60a5fa","to":"#1e3a8a"},"lessons":[{"id":"um1","title":"The Seven Pillars","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"You wash up on the shore of Metrologia, a land where every kingdom once measured length, mass and time its own way, and bridges collapsed and trade collapsed with them. The Guild of Measures says only the Seven Base Units, fixed by the whole world in 2018, can save the realm.","teach":[{"title":"Why one system?","body":"Before SI, the CGS (centimetre-gram-second), FPS (foot-pound-second) and MKS (metre-kilogram-second) systems competed. The Systeme International d'Unites (SI) is now the internationally accepted system, developed by the BIPM, because its decimal basis makes conversions simple.","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"Seven base units","body":"SI has seven base units: metre (length), kilogram (mass), second (time), ampere (electric current), kelvin (thermodynamic temperature), mole (amount of substance) and candela (luminous intensity).","visual":"7️⃣"},{"title":"Modern definitions","body":"Since 2018 these units are fixed by natural constants: the metre uses the speed of light c = 299792458 m/s, the second uses the caesium-133 hyperfine transition frequency 9192631770 Hz, and the kilogram uses the Planck constant h = 6.62607015x10^-34 J s.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"Beyond the seven","body":"Two extra dimensionless units are also defined for completeness: the radian (rad) for plane angle and the steradian (sr) for solid angle.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which system of units is now internationally accepted for scientific, technical and commercial work?","options":["The International System of Units (SI)","The CGS system","The FPS (British) system","The MKS system"],"answer":0,"explain":"SI, developed by the BIPM, is the internationally accepted system used worldwide."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many base units does SI have?","options":["Seven","Five","Ten","Three"],"answer":0,"explain":"SI is built on seven base units: metre, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole and candela."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The second is defined by fixing the numerical value of which quantity?","options":["The caesium-133 hyperfine transition frequency","The speed of light","The Planck constant","The Avogadro constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"The second is defined by taking the caesium frequency (unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition of caesium-133) to be 9192631770 Hz."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The metre is defined by fixing the speed of light in vacuum, c, at this value in m/s. What is that value?","answer":299792458,"explain":"c is fixed at exactly 299792458 m/s, and the metre is defined using this fixed value."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI base unit of amount of substance?","options":["mole","kilogram","candela","ampere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mole is the SI unit for amount of substance, containing exactly 6.02214076x10^23 elementary entities."}]},{"id":"um2","title":"Counting What's Certain","icon":"🔢","kind":"lesson","story":"The Guild's chief instrument-reader challenges you: a scroll reports a pendulum's period as 1.62 s, but a rival kingdom insists it should be written 1.620000 s for 'more precision'. You must learn the true rules of significant figures to settle the dispute.","teach":[{"title":"What counts as significant","body":"A measured value's significant figures are all the reliably known digits plus the first uncertain digit. In the period 1.62 s, the 1 and 6 are certain and the 2 is the first uncertain digit, giving three significant figures in total.","visual":"✅"},{"title":"Non-zero and sandwiched zeros","body":"All non-zero digits are significant, and so are zeros trapped between two non-zero digits, no matter where the decimal point sits.","visual":"0️⃣"},{"title":"Leading vs trailing zeros","body":"Zeros to the right of the decimal point but before the first non-zero digit (as in 0.002308) are never significant. Trailing zeros in a number WITH a decimal point (like 3.500) ARE significant; trailing zeros in a number with NO decimal point (like 123000) are not.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Units don't change sig figs","body":"Changing units cannot change the number of significant figures. The length 2.308 cm can be written as 0.02308 m, 23.08 mm, or 23080 micrometre - all four forms have exactly four significant figures.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many significant figures does the length 287.5 cm have?","options":["Four","Three","Five","Two"],"answer":0,"explain":"2, 8 and 7 are certain digits and 5 is the first uncertain digit, giving four significant figures."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the number 0.002308, how many significant figures are there?","options":["Four","Seven","Three","Six"],"answer":0,"explain":"Leading zeros before the first non-zero digit are never significant; only 2, 3, 0, 8 count."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The measurement 4.700 m has how many significant figures?","options":["Four","Two","One","Seven"],"answer":0,"explain":"Trailing zeros after a decimal point are significant, so all of 4, 7, 0, 0 count."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many significant figures are in the number 3.500?","answer":4,"explain":"Trailing zeros in a number with a decimal point are significant, giving four sig figs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"123 m is rewritten as 123000 mm. How many significant figures does 123000 mm have?","options":["Three","Six","Two","Five"],"answer":0,"explain":"Trailing zeros in a number without a decimal point are not significant, so it still has three sig figs, matching 123 m."}]},{"id":"um3","title":"Powers of Ten","icon":"🔟","kind":"lesson","story":"To end the ambiguity of trailing zeros forever, the Guild teaches you their most powerful spell: scientific notation, where every quantity becomes a x 10^b. With it you can compare the vastness of the Earth to the tininess of a hydrogen atom in a single glance.","teach":[{"title":"Scientific notation","body":"Every measurement can be written as a x 10^b, where a is a number between 1 and 10. This removes ambiguity about trailing zeros, since every digit in a is significant. For example 4.700 m = 4.700 x 10^2 cm, still four significant figures.","visual":"✖️"},{"title":"Order of magnitude","body":"Rounding a to 1 (if a is at most 5) or to 10 (if a is greater than 5) gives an approximate value 10^b; the exponent b is called the order of magnitude of the quantity.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Comparing scales","body":"The diameter of the Earth (1.28x10^7 m) has order of magnitude 7, while a hydrogen atom (1.06x10^-10 m) has order of magnitude -10 - so the Earth is 17 orders of magnitude larger than a hydrogen atom.","visual":"🌏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the order of magnitude of the Earth's diameter, 1.28x10^7 m?","options":["7","6","8","107"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rounding 1.28 down to 1 gives 1x10^7, so the order of magnitude is 7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The diameter of a hydrogen atom is about 1.06x10^-10 m. What is its order of magnitude (give the exponent, e.g. -10)?","answer":-10,"explain":"1.06 rounds to 1, so the diameter is of the order 10^-10 m, order of magnitude -10."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The Earth's diameter has order of magnitude 7 and the hydrogen atom's has order of magnitude -10. By how many orders of magnitude is the Earth larger?","answer":17,"explain":"7 - (-10) = 17, so the Earth's diameter is 17 orders of magnitude larger."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Writing 4.700 m as 4.700 x 10^2 cm, how many significant figures does it retain?","options":["Four","Two","Three","Seven"],"answer":0,"explain":"The power of 10 is irrelevant to significant figures; all digits in 4.700 remain significant."}]},{"id":"um4","title":"The Trader's Scale","icon":"➗","kind":"lesson","story":"A trader hands you a block of metal: mass 4.237 g, volume 2.51 cm3. His apprentice divides them on an abacus and proudly announces the density as 1.68804780876 g/cm3. The Guild elder winces - you must teach the trader the real rules of the craft.","teach":[{"title":"Multiplication and division","body":"In multiplication or division, the result keeps as many significant figures as the original number with the fewest. For mass 4.237 g and volume 2.51 cm3, the raw division gives 1.68804780876 g/cm3, but since 2.51 has only 3 sig figs, density must be reported as 1.69 g/cm3.","visual":"✖️"},{"title":"Addition and subtraction","body":"In addition or subtraction, the result keeps as many decimal places as the number with the fewest decimal places. Adding 436.32 g + 227.2 g + 0.301 g gives 663.821 g raw, but 227.2 g has only one decimal place, so the answer rounds to 663.8 g.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Rounding convention","body":"When the digit to be dropped is exactly 5, round so the preceding digit becomes even: 2.745 rounds to 2.74 (the preceding 4 is already even), but 2.735 also rounds to 2.74 (the preceding 3 is odd, so it is raised to 4).","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Worked example: the cube","body":"Each side of a cube measures 7.203 m (4 sig figs). Surface area = 6(7.203)^2 = 311.299254 m^2, rounded to 311.3 m^2; volume = (7.203)^3 = 373.714754 m^3, rounded to 373.7 m^3 - both kept to 4 significant figures.","visual":"🧊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"5.74 g of a substance occupies 1.2 cm3. Expressed to the correct significant figures, what is the density in g/cm3?","answer":4.8,"explain":"1.2 cm3 has only 2 sig figs, so 5.74/1.2 must be rounded to 4.8 g cm-3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Add 436.32 g + 227.2 g + 0.301 g and round to the correct number of decimal places. What is the result in grams?","answer":663.8,"explain":"227.2 g has only one decimal place, so the sum 663.821 g rounds to 663.8 g."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Subtract 0.304 m from 0.307 m, keeping the correct number of decimal places. What is the result in metres?","answer":0.003,"explain":"Both values have three decimal places, so 0.307 - 0.304 = 0.003 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A cube has each side 7.203 m. What is its volume in m3, rounded to 4 significant figures?","answer":373.7,"explain":"(7.203)^3 = 373.714754 m3, which rounds to 373.7 m3 (4 significant figures)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"12.9 g minus 7.06 g, both given to three significant figures, should correctly be reported as:","options":["5.8 g","5.84 g","5.840 g","6 g"],"answer":0,"explain":"For subtraction we keep decimal places (one here, from 12.9), not significant figures, giving 5.8 g."}]},{"id":"um5","title":"The Cartographer's Doubt","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"The royal cartographer measures a rectangular plot with a metre scale: length 16.2 cm, breadth 10.1 cm - but every scale has an uncertainty. You must calculate how that uncertainty combines when the plot's area is computed, and judge which measurement is truly more accurate.","teach":[{"title":"Absolute and relative error","body":"A measured length l = 16.2 +/- 0.1 cm has an absolute error of +/-0.1 cm, which is a relative error of about +/-0.6%. Similarly the breadth b = 10.1 +/- 0.1 cm is about +/-1% relative error.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Combining errors in a product","body":"When quantities are multiplied, their percentage errors add. For the rectangle, l x b = 163.62 cm2 with a combined error of about +/-1.6%, i.e. +/-2.6 cm2, so the area is finally quoted as 164 +/- 3 cm2.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Relative error depends on the value too","body":"The same absolute error of +/-0.01 g gives very different relative errors depending on the measured value: for a mass of 1.02 g it is about +/-1%, but for 9.89 g it is only about +/-0.1%.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Accuracy from repeated readings","body":"Taking a larger number of independent measurements (for example, 100 readings of a rod's diameter rather than 5) gives a more reliable estimate of the true value, since random errors tend to average out.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A length is measured as l = 16.2 +/- 0.1 cm. What is its relative error as a percentage (just the number)?","answer":0.6,"explain":"0.1/16.2 x 100% is approximately 0.6%."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Multiplying l = 16.2 cm (+/-0.6%) by b = 10.1 cm (+/-1%), the product's percentage error is approximately (in %)?","answer":1.6,"explain":"For a product, percentage errors add: 0.6% + 1% = 1.6%."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A mass of 1.02 g is measured accurate to +/-0.01 g, and a mass of 9.89 g is also accurate to +/-0.01 g. Which measurement has the smaller relative error?","options":["The 9.89 g measurement","The 1.02 g measurement","Both have equal relative error","Cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"(0.01/9.89)x100% is about 0.1%, far smaller than (0.01/1.02)x100% which is about 1%."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"l = 16.2 cm and b = 10.1 cm multiply to a raw area of 163.62 cm2, finally reported as 164 +/- 3 cm2. What is the reported uncertainty in cm2?","answer":3,"explain":"The combined error of about 1.6% on 163.62 cm2 works out to roughly 3 cm2, giving 164 +/- 3 cm2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does measuring a rod's diameter 100 times give a more reliable estimate than measuring it only 5 times?","options":["Random errors tend to average out over more independent readings","The instrument becomes more accurate with use","Significant figures increase automatically with more readings","The rod's true diameter changes each time it is measured"],"answer":0,"explain":"Averaging many independent measurements reduces the effect of random error, giving a more reliable estimate."}]},{"id":"um6","title":"The Dimensional Fingerprint","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Guild archive you find quantities described not by numbers but by 'dimensions' - the hidden powers of length [L], mass [M] and time [T] buried inside every physical formula. You must decode the dimensional fingerprint of volume, force and velocity.","teach":[{"title":"What are dimensions","body":"The dimensions of a physical quantity are the powers to which the base quantities (length [L], mass [M], time [T], and others) must be raised to represent it. Square brackets [ ] denote 'the dimensions of' a quantity.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Volume's dimensions","body":"Volume is length x breadth x height, so its dimensions are [L]x[L]x[L] = [L3]. It has zero dimension in mass [M0] and zero dimension in time [T0].","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Force's dimensions","body":"Force equals mass x acceleration, or mass x length/time2. Its dimensions are [M][L]/[T]2 = [M L T-2] - one dimension each in mass and length, and -2 dimensions in time.","visual":"💪"},{"title":"Velocity's dimensions","body":"Velocity, average velocity, speed and change in velocity are all length/time, so they all share the dimensions [L T-1] - dimensions describe the quality of a quantity, not its magnitude.","visual":"🏃"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the dimensions of volume?","options":["[L3]","[L2]","[M L3]","[L T-1]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volume is length x breadth x height, giving [L]x[L]x[L] = [L3]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the dimensions of force?","options":["[M L T-2]","[M L2 T-2]","[M L-1 T-2]","[L T-2]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Force = mass x acceleration = [M][L T-2] = [M L T-2]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Speed and velocity share the same dimensions because both are expressed as:","options":["length divided by time","mass divided by time","length multiplied by time","length divided by mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"Speed, velocity and their variants are all length/time, with dimensions [L T-1]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Volume is said to have zero dimension in which base quantities?","options":["Mass and time","Length and time","Length and mass","None - volume has dimensions in all three"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volume depends only on length, so it has [M0] and [T0]."}]},{"id":"um7","title":"The Scribe's Notation","icon":"📝","kind":"lesson","story":"The archive's master scribe demands you write proper 'dimensional equations' for volume, speed, force and density - the sacred notation that lets any physicist, in any land, verify a formula without ever picking up an instrument.","teach":[{"title":"Dimensional formula","body":"The dimensional formula expresses which base quantities, and to what powers, describe a physical quantity. Volume's dimensional formula is [M0 L3 T0], and speed's is [M0 L T-1].","visual":"📜"},{"title":"More dimensional formulae","body":"Acceleration has dimensional formula [M0 L T-2], and mass density (mass divided by volume) has dimensional formula [M L-3 T0].","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Dimensional equations","body":"An equation obtained by equating a physical quantity to its dimensional formula is called its dimensional equation, for example [F] = [M L T-2] for force.","visual":"🟰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the dimensional formula of speed?","options":["[M0 L T-1]","[M0 L2 T-1]","[M L T-1]","[M0 L T-2]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Speed is length/time, giving dimensional formula [M0 L T-1]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the dimensional formula of mass density (mass/volume)?","options":["[M L-3 T0]","[M L3 T0]","[M0 L-3 T0]","[M L-3 T-1]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Density = mass/volume, so [M]/[L3] = [M L-3 T0]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the dimensional formula of acceleration?","options":["[M0 L T-2]","[M0 L T-1]","[M L T-2]","[M0 L2 T-2]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Acceleration is velocity/time = [L T-1]/[T] = [M0 L T-2]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The dimensional formula [M0 L3 T0] belongs to which physical quantity?","options":["Volume","Force","Speed","Density"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volume has dimensions [L3] with zero mass and zero time dimension, i.e. [M0 L3 T0]."}]},{"id":"um8","title":"Trial of Homogeneity","icon":"🔮","kind":"lesson","story":"The final trial before the Guild crowns you a Master of Measures: use the Principle of Homogeneity to test whether ancient formulas are even physically possible, then derive the period of a swinging pendulum using nothing but dimensions.","teach":[{"title":"Principle of homogeneity","body":"Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added, subtracted or equated. If every term in an equation does not have the same dimensions, the equation must be wrong - though passing this test does not prove an equation exactly correct.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Checking x = x0 + v0 t + (1/2) a t2","body":"Each term has dimensions of length: [x]=[L], [x0]=[L], [v0 t]=[L T-1][T]=[L], and [(1/2) a t2]=[L T-2][T2]=[L]. Since all terms match, the equation is dimensionally consistent.","visual":"✔️"},{"title":"Ruling out kinetic energy formulas","body":"Kinetic energy has dimensions [M L2 T-2]. Testing K=m2v3 gives [M2 L3 T-3] (wrong), K=ma gives [M L T-2] (wrong), and K=(1/2)mv2+ma illegally adds mismatched dimensions - all three are ruled out. Both K=(1/2)mv2 and K=(3/16)mv2 pass the dimensional test, so dimensions alone cannot pick between them.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Deducing the pendulum's period","body":"Assuming T = k l^x g^y m^z and matching dimensions [T] = [L]^x [L T-2]^y [M]^z gives x+y=0, -2y=1, z=0, so x=1/2, y=-1/2, z=0. This yields T = k(sqrt(l/g)); the constant, found separately (not by dimensions), is k=2(pi), giving T = 2(pi)(sqrt(l/g)).","visual":"🕰️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the equation x = x0 + v0 t + (1/2) a t2 to be dimensionally consistent, every term on the right must have the same dimensions as:","options":["[L], the dimension of x","[L T-1], the dimension of velocity","[L T-2], the dimension of acceleration","[T], the dimension of time"],"answer":0,"explain":"x has dimensions of length, so every term on the right must also reduce to [L]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which kinetic energy formula is ruled out because it works out to [M2 L3 T-3] instead of [M L2 T-2]?","options":["K = m2 v3","K = (1/2)mv2","K = (3/16)mv2","K = 2mv2"],"answer":0,"explain":"K = m2v3 has dimensions [M2][L T-1]3 = [M2 L3 T-3], which does not match kinetic energy's [M L2 T-2]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is K = (1/2)mv2 + ma dimensionally invalid?","options":["It adds two quantities of different dimensions, [M L2 T-2] and [M L T-2]","Its exponents are not whole numbers","It has too many significant figures","Mass cannot appear twice in a formula"],"answer":0,"explain":"(1/2)mv2 has dimensions [M L2 T-2] while ma has [M L T-2]; only quantities with identical dimensions can be added."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using T = k l^x g^y m^z and matching dimensions for a simple pendulum's period, what values are found for x and y?","options":["x = 1/2, y = -1/2","x = 1, y = -1","x = -1/2, y = 1/2","x = 2, y = -2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Matching [T] = [L]^x [L T-2]^y [M]^z gives x+y=0 and -2y=1, so x=1/2 and y=-1/2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Dimensional analysis gives the simple pendulum's period as T = k(sqrt(l/g)); the actual dimensionless constant k, found beyond dimensional analysis, equals 2(pi). Using pi = 3.14, what is k rounded to 3 significant figures?","answer":6.28,"explain":"k = 2 x 3.14 = 6.28, which is the constant in T = 2(pi)(sqrt(l/g))."}]},{"id":"um-boss","title":"The Metrologist Archon","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","boss":{"name":"The Metrologist Archon","taunt":"Numbers without units are lies, and units without significant figures are noise. Prove you have mastered both, or your measurements mean nothing!"},"story":"At the summit of the Guild tower, the Metrologist Archon guards the crown of Metrologia. It will test everything you have learned: base units, significant figures, errors, and the deep logic of dimensions. Answer true, or be cast back down to the archive.","teach":[{"title":"Final review","body":"SI's seven base units, the rules for significant figures and arithmetic with them, error and uncertainty combination, and dimensional analysis all work together to make measurement precise, honest and checkable.","visual":"🏆"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"SI defines the kilogram by fixing the numerical value of which constant?","options":["The Planck constant, h","The speed of light, c","The Boltzmann constant, k","Avogadro's number, NA"],"answer":0,"explain":"The kilogram is defined by fixing h = 6.62607015x10^-34 J s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many significant figures does the measurement 0.06900 have?","answer":4,"explain":"Leading zeros are not significant, but trailing zeros after the decimal point are, giving four sig figs (6, 9, 0, 0)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A mass of 4.237 g occupies 2.51 cm3. What is the density in g/cm3, correctly rounded to the right number of significant figures?","answer":1.69,"explain":"2.51 cm3 has 3 sig figs, so 4.237/2.51 = 1.68804... rounds to 1.69 g/cm3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Round 2.745 to three significant figures using the round-to-even convention.","answer":2.74,"explain":"The digit to drop is 5 and the preceding digit 4 is already even, so it is simply dropped, giving 2.74."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the dimensions of force?","options":["[M L T-2]","[M L2 T-2]","[M L-2 T-2]","[M0 L T-2]"],"answer":0,"explain":"Force = mass x acceleration, giving dimensions [M][L T-2] = [M L T-2]."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A formula is found to be dimensionally consistent. What can you conclude?","options":["It might be correct, but this is not guaranteed","It is definitely the exact correct formula","It is definitely wrong","It has the correct significant figures"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dimensional consistency is necessary but not sufficient; a dimensionally correct equation need not be the exact physical relation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using T = k l^x g^y m^z, dimensional analysis of the simple pendulum gives z, the exponent of mass m, as what value?","answer":0,"explain":"Matching dimensions gives z = 0, meaning the pendulum's period does not depend on the mass of the bob."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two masses are each measured with absolute error +/-0.01 g: one is 1.02 g, the other is 9.89 g. Which has the larger relative error?","options":["The 1.02 g measurement","The 9.89 g measurement","Both are equal","Relative error cannot be found from this data"],"answer":0,"explain":"(0.01/1.02)x100% is about 1%, much larger than (0.01/9.89)x100% which is about 0.1%."}]}]},{"id":"motionstraightline11","index":1,"name":"Motion in a Straight Line","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 2","emoji":"🏃","palette":{"from":"#34d399","to":"#065f46"},"lessons":[{"id":"mx1","title":"The Origin Stone","icon":"📍","kind":"lesson","story":"You wake in the Kinematics Wastes beside a carved Origin Stone, the only fixed marker for miles. The old surveyor warns you: every position you report must be measured from this stone, and the ground you wear down with your boots is not the same thing as how far you've actually strayed from it.","teach":[{"title":"Position needs an origin","body":"An object is said to be in motion if its position changes with time. Its position can only be specified with reference to a conveniently chosen origin. For motion in a straight line, positions to the right of the origin are taken as positive and to the left as negative.","visual":"📍"},{"title":"Path length vs. displacement","body":"Path length (distance) is the total length of the actual route travelled, while displacement is the straight-line change in position from start to end. A drunkard who takes 5 steps forward then 3 steps backward (each step 1 m) covers a path length of 8 m in that cycle, but his displacement is only 2 m.","visual":"👣"},{"title":"Distance is never less than displacement","body":"The magnitude of displacement over an interval of time is always less than or equal to the total path length covered by the object in that interval. The two are equal only when the object moves in a single direction without reversing.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"You mark an origin on a straight road. Positions to the right are treated as ___ and to the left as ___.","options":["positive; negative","negative; positive","positive; positive","it does not matter"],"answer":0,"explain":"By convention, once you choose an origin, positions to the right are positive and to the left are negative."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A drunkard takes 5 steps forward then 3 steps backward, each step 1 m long. What total path length (in m) does he cover in one such forward-backward cycle?","answer":8,"explain":"Path length adds up every step travelled: 5 m forward + 3 m backward = 8 m, regardless of direction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same drunkard cycle (5 steps forward, 3 steps back, 1 m each), what is his net displacement (in m) from where he started?","answer":2,"explain":"Displacement only cares about the net change in position: 5 m forward minus 3 m backward = 2 m forward."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Path length covered by an object is always ___ the magnitude of its displacement over the same interval.","options":["greater than or equal to","less than","equal to","unrelated to"],"answer":0,"explain":"Path length can only match or exceed the straight-line displacement; it can never fall short of it."}]},{"id":"mx2","title":"The Merchant's Round Trip","icon":"🛒","kind":"lesson","story":"A weary trader asks you to judge his day's work: he walked to the market and, finding it shut, walked straight back home. Was his 'average speed' truly zero, as his aching legs suggest, or does the ledger of physics tell a different story?","teach":[{"title":"Average velocity","body":"Average velocity is displacement divided by the time interval in which it occurs. A man who walks 2.5 km from home to a market at 5 km/h takes 0.5 h to get there, since time = distance/speed.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"Average speed uses total path length","body":"Average speed is defined as the total path length divided by the time interval, not the displacement. If the same man finds the market closed and walks straight back home at 7.5 km/h, his net displacement for the whole round trip is zero, but he has still covered a real path length of 5 km.","visual":"👟"},{"title":"Why the distinction matters","body":"Average speed of an object is always greater than or equal to the magnitude of its average velocity over a given interval. You would not want to tell a tired man that his average speed for the journey was zero, even though his average velocity for the round trip truly is.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The trader walks 2.5 km to the market at 5 km/h. How many minutes does the outward walk take?","answer":30,"explain":"Time = distance/speed = 2.5 km / 5 km/h = 0.5 h = 30 minutes."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"He returns the same 2.5 km home at 7.5 km/h. How many minutes does the return trip take?","answer":20,"explain":"Time = 2.5 km / 7.5 km/h = 1/3 h = 20 minutes, so the round trip takes 30 + 20 = 50 minutes."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Over the full 50-minute round trip, what is the magnitude of his average velocity in km/h?","answer":0,"explain":"He ends up back at his starting point, so his net displacement — and hence his average velocity — is 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Over the same 50-minute round trip, what is his average speed in km/h (total path length divided by time)?","answer":6,"explain":"Total path length = 2.5 km + 2.5 km = 5 km. Average speed = 5 km / (50/60 h) = 6 km/h."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do physicists define average speed as total path length divided by time, rather than as the magnitude of average velocity?","options":["Because a round trip can have zero average velocity even though real distance was covered","Because average velocity is always larger than average speed","Because path length is easier to measure with a ruler","There is no real reason, the two definitions are identical"],"answer":0,"explain":"A round trip like the trader's has zero displacement but non-zero path length, so speed and velocity must be defined differently."}]},{"id":"mx3","title":"The Vanishing Interval","icon":"⏱️","kind":"lesson","story":"The Timekeeper challenges you: she keeps shrinking the time-window around one instant, t = 4 s, and asks you to name the true velocity the car had at that exact heartbeat, not just its average over some stretch.","teach":[{"title":"Instantaneous velocity is a limit","body":"Instantaneous velocity is the limit of the average velocity as the time interval Δt becomes infinitesimally small: v = lim(Δt→0) Δx/Δt = dx/dt. It is the slope of the tangent to the position-time graph at that instant.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Shrinking the window","body":"For a car whose position is x = 0.08t^3, computing Δx/Δt for smaller and smaller Δt centred at t = 4.0 s (2.0 s, 1.0 s, 0.5 s, 0.1 s, 0.01 s) gives values that approach 3.84 m/s, which is exactly dx/dt at t = 4.0 s.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Speed is the magnitude of velocity","body":"Instantaneous speed is simply the magnitude of the instantaneous velocity, and the two are always equal at any given instant. This differs from average speed and average velocity magnitude, which need not be equal over a finite interval.","visual":"💨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the car with x = 0.08t^3 (x in m, t in s), the limiting value of Δx/Δt centred at t = 4.0 s approaches what velocity, in m/s?","answer":3.84,"explain":"As Δt shrinks from 2.0 s to 0.01 s, the average velocity converges to dx/dt at t = 4.0 s, which is 3.84 m/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An object's position is x = a + bt^2, with a = 8.5 m and b = 2.5 m/s^2. Since v = dx/dt = 2bt, what is its velocity (in m/s) at t = 2.0 s?","answer":10,"explain":"v = 2bt = 2(2.5)(2.0) = 10 m/s, matching the worked example."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same x = a + bt^2 with a = 8.5 m, b = 2.5 m/s^2: what is the average velocity (in m/s) between t = 2.0 s and t = 4.0 s?","answer":15,"explain":"x(4.0) = 8.5 + 2.5(16) = 48.5 m and x(2.0) = 8.5 + 2.5(4) = 18.5 m, so average velocity = (48.5 - 18.5)/(4.0 - 2.0) = 15 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At any given instant, instantaneous speed equals ___.","options":["the magnitude of the instantaneous velocity at that instant","the average speed over the whole journey","always zero","twice the instantaneous velocity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Unlike average speed and average velocity, instantaneous speed and the magnitude of instantaneous velocity are always identical."}]},{"id":"mx4","title":"Galileo's Question","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"Centuries ago, Galileo wrestled with a puzzle: does an object's motion change at a steady rate with distance, or with time? Rolling balls down inclined planes, he found the answer — and now you must apply it to earn passage.","teach":[{"title":"Average acceleration","body":"The average acceleration over a time interval is the change of velocity divided by the time interval: a = (v2 - v1)/(t2 - t1) = Δv/Δt. Its SI unit is m/s^2.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Instantaneous acceleration","body":"Instantaneous acceleration is defined the same way as instantaneous velocity: a = lim(Δt→0) Δv/Δt = dv/dt. It equals the slope of the tangent to the velocity-time graph at that instant.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Zero velocity does not mean zero acceleration","body":"Galileo concluded that acceleration due to gravity is the rate of change of velocity with time, a constant, not with distance. A key subtlety: a particle may be momentarily at rest and still have non-zero acceleration, like a ball thrown upward at its highest point.","visual":"⚽"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For x = a + bt^2 with b = 2.5 m/s^2, velocity is v = 2bt = 5.0t m/s. What is the (constant) acceleration dv/dt, in m/s^2?","answer":5,"explain":"Differentiating v = 5.0t with respect to t gives a = 5.0 m/s^2, constant for all time."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the same object: v = 0 m/s at t = 0 s and v = 10 m/s at t = 2.0 s. What is the average acceleration (in m/s^2) over that interval?","answer":5,"explain":"Average acceleration = Δv/Δt = (10 - 0)/(2.0 - 0) = 5 m/s^2, matching the constant instantaneous value."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If a moving object is slowing down, its acceleration points ___ its velocity.","options":["opposite to","in the same direction as","perpendicular to","acceleration must be zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deceleration happens when acceleration opposes the direction of velocity, reducing its magnitude over time."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ball thrown straight up has zero velocity at its highest point. What is true of its acceleration there?","options":["It is still equal to g, directed downward — not zero","It is exactly zero at that instant","It momentarily reverses to point upward","Acceleration only exists while the ball is moving"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zero velocity at an instant does not imply zero acceleration; gravity keeps acting on the ball even at the peak of its path."}]},{"id":"mx5","title":"Mapping the Motion","icon":"🗺️","kind":"lesson","story":"The Cartographer of Constant Acceleration hands you a velocity-time chart and a challenge: the shaded area beneath the line hides the object's displacement. Learn to read it, and derive the equations that bind position, velocity, acceleration and time together.","teach":[{"title":"Area under the v-t graph is displacement","body":"The area under a velocity-time curve between two instants represents the displacement of the object over that interval. For constant velocity u over time T, the v-t graph is a rectangle of height u and base T, so displacement = u × T.","visual":"🟩"},{"title":"The first kinematic equation","body":"For an object moving with uniform acceleration a, starting at velocity v0, its velocity at time t is v = v0 + at. On a velocity-time graph this is a straight line of slope a.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Displacement and the final equation","body":"Using the area under this v-t line (a triangle plus a rectangle) gives x = v0 t + (1/2)at^2, and eliminating t between the two relations gives v^2 = v0^2 + 2ax. If the particle starts at x0 instead of 0, x is simply replaced by (x - x0) throughout.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The area under a velocity-time graph between two instants equals the object's ___ during that interval.","options":["displacement","acceleration","average speed only","position at t = 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"This holds generally, even though it is easiest to see for the simple rectangle case of constant velocity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using x = a + bt^2 with a = 8.5 m, b = 2.5 m/s^2, what is the position x (in m) at t = 2.0 s?","answer":18.5,"explain":"x(2.0) = 8.5 + 2.5(2.0)^2 = 8.5 + 10 = 18.5 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same object, what is the position x (in m) at t = 4.0 s?","answer":48.5,"explain":"x(4.0) = 8.5 + 2.5(4.0)^2 = 8.5 + 40 = 48.5 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The three kinematic equations v = v0 + at, x = v0t + (1/2)at^2, and v^2 = v0^2 + 2ax connect v0, v, a, t and ___.","options":["x, the displacement","the mass of the object","the origin's location only","air resistance"],"answer":0,"explain":"These five quantities — v0, v, a, t and x — are exactly what the kinematic equations of uniformly accelerated motion relate."}]},{"id":"mx6","title":"Ball Toss and Braking Trial","icon":"🏐","kind":"lesson","story":"Two trials await: first, hurl a ball from a tower and predict exactly where and when it lands. Second, step into the driver's seat and prove you understand why doubling your speed does far more than double your stopping distance.","teach":[{"title":"Ball thrown upward from a building","body":"A ball is thrown vertically upward with v0 = +20 m/s from the top of a 25.0 m building, with a = -g = -10 m/s^2. Using v^2 = v0^2 + 2a(y - y0) with v = 0 at the top, the ball rises (y - y0) = 20 m above its launch point.","visual":"⬆️"},{"title":"Two ways to find the total time","body":"Splitting the trip into up (v = v0 + at1, giving t1 = 2 s) and down (using y = y0 + v0t + (1/2)at^2, giving t2 = 3 s) gives a total of 5 s. The same answer comes directly from one quadratic equation using the full displacement from 25 m to the ground.","visual":"⏳"},{"title":"Stopping distance grows with the square of speed","body":"Using v^2 = v0^2 + 2ax with v = 0, the stopping distance is ds = v0^2/(2a), so it is proportional to the square of the initial velocity — doubling speed quadruples stopping distance for the same deceleration. Real test data for one car: braking distances of 10 m, 20 m, 34 m and 50 m at speeds of 11, 15, 20 and 25 m/s.","visual":"🛑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A ball is thrown upward at 20 m/s from a 25.0 m building, with g = 10 m/s^2. How high (in m) above the launch point does it rise?","answer":20,"explain":"0 = (20)^2 + 2(-10)(y - y0) gives (y - y0) = 20 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same ball, what is the total time (in s) before it hits the ground?","answer":5,"explain":"Time up (2 s) plus time down from the peak (3 s) gives a total of 5 s, confirmed by solving the full quadratic directly."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From the real braking-distance data, what is the stopping distance (in m) for the car travelling at 20 m/s?","answer":34,"explain":"The recorded braking distance at 20 m/s was 34 m, consistent with ds being proportional to v0^2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From the same data, what is the stopping distance (in m) for the car travelling at 25 m/s?","answer":50,"explain":"The recorded braking distance at 25 m/s was 50 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the same deceleration, doubling a vehicle's initial speed increases its stopping distance by a factor of ___.","options":["4","2","8","it stays the same"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since ds = v0^2/(2a), doubling v0 quadruples ds — a factor of 4."}]},{"id":"mx7","title":"The Reflex Trial","icon":"🪄","kind":"lesson","story":"Gravity itself is the final trainer. It drops objects at a constant rate near Earth's surface, sorts falling distances into a strange, elegant ratio, and even tests how fast your own hand can react before a ruler slips through your fingers.","teach":[{"title":"Free fall near Earth's surface","body":"An object released near Earth's surface accelerates downward under gravity at a = -g = -9.8 m/s^2 (taking upward as positive), constant so long as the fall height is small compared to Earth's radius. Starting from rest (v0 = 0) at y = 0: v = -9.8t, y = -4.9t^2, and v^2 = -19.6y.","visual":"🍎"},{"title":"Galileo's law of odd numbers","body":"For a body falling from rest, the distances covered in successive equal time intervals stand in the ratio of the odd numbers beginning with unity: 1 : 3 : 5 : 7 : 9 : 11 ... This was first established by Galileo Galilei from his studies of falling bodies.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Measuring your own reaction time","body":"Dropping a ruler through a friend's fingers and measuring how far it falls before being caught gives the reaction time, since the ruler is in free fall with v0 = 0. In one real trial the ruler fell d = 21.0 cm before being caught, giving a reaction time of about 0.2 s.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the reaction-time experiment, a dropped ruler falls d = 21.0 cm (0.210 m) before being caught, with g = 9.8 m/s^2. Using d = (1/2)g(t_r)^2, what is the reaction time t_r in seconds (to 3 decimal places)?","answer":0.207,"explain":"t_r = sqrt(2d/g) = sqrt(2 × 0.210 / 9.8) = sqrt(0.042857) ≈ 0.207 s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a body falling from rest, the distances it covers in the 1st, 2nd, 3rd... equal time intervals are in the ratio ___","options":["1 : 3 : 5 : 7 ...","1 : 2 : 3 : 4 ...","1 : 4 : 9 : 16 ...","they are always equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is Galileo's law of odd numbers, verified by computing y = -(1/2)gτ^2 at successive equal intervals τ."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using v = -9.8t for an object released from rest, what is the magnitude of its velocity (in m/s) after falling for 2.0 s?","answer":19.6,"explain":"|v| = 9.8 × 2.0 = 19.6 m/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using y = -4.9t^2 for the same free fall, what distance (in m) has it fallen after 2.0 s?","answer":19.6,"explain":"|y| = 4.9 × (2.0)^2 = 4.9 × 4 = 19.6 m."}]},{"id":"mx-boss","title":"The Sentinel of Constant Acceleration","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the edge of the Kinematics Wastes stands the Sentinel, forged from five interlocking quantities — v0, v, a, t and x. It will not step aside until you prove, with real numbers, that you command the equations of uniformly accelerated motion.","boss":{"name":"The Sentinel of Constant Acceleration","taunt":"Five quantities. Three equations. Show me you have truly mastered rectilinear motion — or be sent back to the Origin Stone!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything you've forged","body":"Position, path length and displacement; average and instantaneous velocity and speed; average and instantaneous acceleration; and the three kinematic equations v = v0 + at, x = v0t + (1/2)at^2 and v^2 = v0^2 + 2ax — all built from the same idea: motion described precisely with respect to a chosen origin.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"The equations only bind under constant acceleration","body":"The definitions of instantaneous velocity and acceleration are exact and always correct, but the three kinematic equations only hold for motion in which the magnitude and direction of acceleration stay constant throughout.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A car moving at 126 km/h (= 35 m/s) is brought to rest over 200 m. Using v^2 = v0^2 + 2ax, what is the magnitude of its retardation, in m/s^2 (to 2 decimal places)?","answer":3.06,"explain":"0 = 35^2 + 2a(200) gives a = -1225/400 = -3.0625 m/s^2, a retardation of about 3.06 m/s^2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same car, using v = v0 + at, how long (in s, to 2 decimal places) does it take to stop?","answer":11.43,"explain":"t = v0/|a| = 35/3.0625 ≈ 11.43 s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A player throws a ball upward at 29.4 m/s (g = 9.8 m/s^2). Using v^2 = v0^2 - 2gh with v = 0 at the top, how high (in m) does it rise?","answer":44.1,"explain":"h = v0^2/(2g) = 29.4^2 / 19.6 = 864.36/19.6 = 44.1 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same throw, how long (in s) after leaving the player's hand does the ball return to it?","answer":6,"explain":"Total time = 2v0/g = 2(29.4)/9.8 = 6 s, since the up and down trips are symmetric under constant g."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the highest point of the thrown ball's path, its velocity is zero. What is its acceleration at that instant?","options":["9.8 m/s^2, directed downward — not zero","Exactly zero, since the ball is momentarily at rest","9.8 m/s^2, directed upward","Undefined at the turning point"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zero velocity does not imply zero acceleration; gravity continues to act on the ball even at its peak."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A particle can have zero velocity at an instant yet still have ___ acceleration at that same instant.","options":["non-zero","zero","infinite","negative infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"A particle thrown up has zero velocity at its highest point, but the acceleration due to gravity is still acting on it."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Recall from x = a + bt^2 (a = 8.5 m, b = 2.5 m/s^2): what was the average velocity, in m/s, between t = 2.0 s and t = 4.0 s?","answer":15,"explain":"Average velocity = [x(4.0) - x(2.0)] / (4.0 - 2.0) = (48.5 - 18.5)/2.0 = 15 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The kinematic equations v = v0 + at, x = v0t + (1/2)at^2 and v^2 = v0^2 + 2ax are valid only when ___.","options":["acceleration is constant in both magnitude and direction","the object starts from rest","the motion is two-dimensional","air resistance is present"],"answer":0,"explain":"These equations describe uniformly accelerated rectilinear motion only; for changing acceleration, calculus-based methods are needed instead."}]}]},{"id":"motionplane11","index":2,"name":"Motion in a Plane","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 3","emoji":"🎯","palette":{"from":"#f472b6","to":"#831843"},"lessons":[{"id":"mp1","title":"Scalars, Vectors, and the Cartographer's Tower","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"You wake up in the Cartographer's Tower, where every treasure map only shows straight-line distances until you learn that some quantities need a direction stitched onto them. The Tower Keeper hands you a compass and challenges you to sort the tower's readings into scalars and vectors before the map dissolves.","teach":[{"title":"What Makes a Scalar","body":"A scalar quantity is fully described by a single number and a unit, nothing more. Distance, mass, temperature, and time are scalars; they combine by ordinary algebra, like the temperature difference 35.6 degC minus 24.2 degC equals 11.4 degC.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"What Makes a Vector","body":"A vector quantity has both magnitude and direction and obeys the triangle law (or parallelogram law) of addition. Displacement, velocity, acceleration, and force are vectors, so you can't just add their numbers without accounting for direction.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Position and Displacement Vectors","body":"The position vector OP = r locates a point P relative to a chosen origin O. If an object moves from P to P', the displacement vector PP' depends only on the start and end points, never on the actual path taken.","visual":"📍"},{"title":"Equal Vectors","body":"Two vectors are equal only if they have the same magnitude AND the same direction. Two arrows of identical length pointing different ways are not equal, since shifting one parallel to itself must make both tail and tip coincide.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which pair below is correctly classified?","options":["Mass is a scalar, velocity is a vector","Mass is a vector, velocity is a scalar","Both mass and velocity are scalars","Both mass and velocity are vectors"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mass has magnitude only (scalar); velocity has both magnitude and direction (vector)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The maximum temperature on a day is 35.6 degC and the minimum is 24.2 degC. What is the difference, in degC?","answer":11.4,"explain":"Scalars combine by ordinary algebra: 35.6 - 24.2 = 11.4 degC, exactly as in the NCERT example."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An object moves from P to P' and then back to P. What is its net displacement?","options":["A null vector","Equal to the path length","Equal to r + r'","Undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since the initial and final positions coincide, the displacement vector has zero magnitude - a null vector."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Vectors A' and B' have the same length but point in different directions. Are they equal?","options":["No, direction must also match","Yes, magnitude is all that matters","Yes, if they start at the same point","Only if drawn on the same page"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equality of vectors requires both equal magnitude and equal direction."}]},{"id":"mp2","title":"The Scaling Wand and the Null Rune","icon":"🪄","kind":"lesson","story":"The Tower Keeper hands you a Scaling Wand: point it at any arrow and stretch, shrink, or flip it. But beware the Null Rune - multiply a vector by zero, and it vanishes into a point with no direction at all.","teach":[{"title":"Scaling a Vector","body":"Multiplying vector A by a positive number lambda gives lambda*A - the same direction as A, with magnitude scaled by lambda. For example, 2A points the same way as A but is twice as long.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Flipping a Vector","body":"Multiplying A by a negative number reverses the direction and scales the magnitude by the number's size. So -A points opposite to A with the same length, and -1.5A is 1.5 times as long but reversed.","visual":"↩️"},{"title":"The Null Vector","body":"A + (-A) = 0, the null (zero) vector: its magnitude is zero, so its direction cannot be specified. It also results from multiplying any vector by the scalar zero, and it satisfies A + 0 = A.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"Physical Dimensions in Scaling","body":"The scalar lambda can itself carry physical dimensions. For instance, multiplying a constant velocity vector by a time duration gives a displacement vector - the dimension of lambda*A is the product of the dimensions of lambda and A.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If A points east with magnitude 4, what does -2A represent?","options":["A vector pointing west with magnitude 8","A vector pointing east with magnitude 8","A vector pointing west with magnitude 4","A vector pointing east with magnitude 2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Multiplying by a negative number reverses direction and scales magnitude by the number's absolute value: -2 flips east to west and doubles the length."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the direction of a null vector?","options":["It cannot be specified","Always points toward the origin","Always horizontal","Same as the original vector"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since a null vector has zero magnitude, its direction is undefined."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Multiplying a constant velocity vector by a time duration gives a vector representing:","options":["Displacement","Acceleration","Force","Momentum"],"answer":0,"explain":"The dimension of lambda*A is dimension(lambda) times dimension(A); velocity times time gives displacement."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A vector has magnitude 6. It is multiplied by the scalar 1.5. What is the magnitude of the resulting vector?","answer":9,"explain":"Scaling multiplies magnitude directly: 6 x 1.5 = 9 (direction unchanged since 1.5 > 0)."}]},{"id":"mp3","title":"The Storm Over the Tower","icon":"☔","kind":"lesson","story":"Storm clouds gather over the Tower. Rain falls straight down at furious speed, and now a crosswind joins in - you must tilt your umbrella at exactly the right angle, found only by adding the rain's and wind's velocity vectors head-to-tail.","teach":[{"title":"Head-to-Tail (Triangle) Method","body":"To add A + B graphically, place B's tail at A's head, then draw the resultant R from A's tail to B's head. This works because vectors obey the triangle law of addition.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Addition is Commutative and Associative","body":"A + B = B + A, and (A + B) + C = A + (B + C) - the order in which you add vectors, or the way you group them, never changes the final resultant.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"The Parallelogram Method","body":"Bring both vectors' tails to a common origin O, complete the parallelogram, and the diagonal from O gives the resultant R = A + B - an equivalent way to get the same answer as the triangle method.","visual":"◇"},{"title":"Subtracting Vectors","body":"A - B is defined as A + (-B): reverse B's direction, then add it to A using the same triangle or parallelogram method.","visual":"➖"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Rain falls straight down at 35 m/s while wind blows horizontally at 12 m/s. What method finds the resultant velocity?","options":["Add the two vectors head-to-tail (triangle law)","Simply add 35 + 12","Subtract the smaller from the larger","Multiply the two speeds"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since velocity is a vector, the rain and wind velocities must be combined using the triangle (or parallelogram) law of vector addition, not simple arithmetic."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Rain falls vertically at 35 m/s; wind blows horizontally at 12 m/s. What is the magnitude of the resultant velocity, in m/s (nearest whole number)?","answer":37,"explain":"R = sqrt(35^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(1225+144) = sqrt(1369) = 37 m/s, exactly as in NCERT Example 3.1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the same rain-and-wind problem, at approximately what angle (in degrees) from the vertical should the umbrella be tilted? (tan theta = 12/35)","answer":19,"explain":"tan theta = 12/35 = 0.343, so theta = tan^-1(0.343) is about 19 degrees."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement about vector addition is correct?","options":["A + B = B + A","A + B is always greater than A - B","Vector addition depends on the order of adding","A + B = A x B"],"answer":0,"explain":"Vector addition is commutative: A + B = B + A."}]},{"id":"mp4","title":"The Vector Vault","icon":"🔐","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Vector Vault, every arrow is locked onto a grid of perpendicular rails - the x-axis and y-axis. To unlock the vault, you must resolve each guard vector into its Ax and Ay pieces using nothing but unit vectors i-hat and j-hat.","teach":[{"title":"Resolving Along Two Directions","body":"Any vector A lying in a plane can be written as A = lambda*a + mu*b, a sum of component vectors along two non-parallel vectors a and b in the same plane, where lambda and mu are real numbers.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Unit Vectors i, j, k","body":"A unit vector has magnitude exactly 1 and points in a specific direction, with no dimensions or units of its own - it is used only to specify direction. The unit vectors i-hat, j-hat, k-hat point along the x-, y-, and z-axes and are mutually perpendicular.","visual":"1️⃣"},{"title":"Components Ax and Ay","body":"A vector A in the x-y plane resolves as A = Ax*i-hat + Ay*j-hat, where Ax = A cos theta and Ay = A sin theta, with theta the angle A makes with the x-axis. Ax and Ay are numbers, not vectors - Ax*i-hat is the vector.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Magnitude and Direction from Components","body":"Given Ax and Ay, the magnitude is A = sqrt(Ax^2 + Ay^2) and the direction is theta = tan^-1(Ay/Ax) - this lets you convert freely between magnitude-direction form and component form.","visual":"🧭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the magnitude of a unit vector?","options":["Exactly 1","Depends on the vector it represents","0","Equal to the vector's x-component"],"answer":0,"explain":"By definition a unit vector has magnitude 1 and carries no dimension - it specifies direction only."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the magnitude of the vector i-hat + j-hat (2 decimal places)?","answer":1.41,"explain":"|i+j| = sqrt(1^2+1^2) = sqrt(2) is about 1.41, using Ax=1, Ay=1 in A=sqrt(Ax^2+Ay^2)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The vector i-hat + j-hat makes what angle (in degrees) with the x-axis?","answer":45,"explain":"theta = tan^-1(Ay/Ax) = tan^-1(1/1) = 45 degrees."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a vector A with components Ax = A cos theta and Ay = A sin theta, which of the following is true?","options":["Ax^2 + Ay^2 = A^2","Ax + Ay = A","Ax - Ay = A","Ax x Ay = A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since Ax=Acos(theta) and Ay=Asin(theta), Ax^2+Ay^2 = A^2(cos^2(theta)+sin^2(theta)) = A^2."}]},{"id":"mp5","title":"The Motorboat Crossing","icon":"🚤","kind":"lesson","story":"The Tower Keeper retires the ruler and protractor. 'Numbers are faster than drawings,' she says. You now add vectors purely by combining their components, and put the method to the test navigating a motorboat across a river current.","teach":[{"title":"Adding Components Directly","body":"If A = Ax*i + Ay*j and B = Bx*i + By*j, their sum R = A+B has components Rx = Ax+Bx and Ry = Ay+By - each component of the resultant is just the sum of the corresponding components. This extends to any number of vectors, and to 3D with a k-hat term.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Law of Cosines for Vector Magnitude","body":"For two vectors A and B separated by angle theta, the magnitude of their resultant is R = sqrt(A^2 + B^2 + 2AB cos theta) - derived directly from the geometry of the parallelogram method.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Law of Sines for Direction","body":"The resultant's direction alpha (measured from A) satisfies sin(alpha)/B = sin(theta)/R, letting you find exactly which way R points relative to the original vectors.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If A = Ax*i + Ay*j and B = Bx*i + By*j, what is Rx for R = A + B?","options":["Ax + Bx","Ax x Bx","Ax - Bx","Ax / Bx"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each component of the resultant vector is simply the sum of the corresponding components of A and B."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A motorboat races north at 25 km/h while the water current runs at 10 km/h in a direction 60 degrees east of south (120 degrees from the boat's velocity). What is the resultant speed, in km/h (nearest whole number)?","answer":22,"explain":"Using the Law of cosines: R = sqrt(25^2 + 10^2 + 2x25x10xcos120) = sqrt(625+100-250) = sqrt(475) is about 21.8, rounding to 22 km/h, as in NCERT Example 3.3."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Example 3.2, the formula R^2 = A^2 + B^2 + 2AB cos theta is known as:","options":["The Law of cosines","The Law of sines","Pythagoras' theorem","The parallelogram rule of scalars"],"answer":0,"explain":"This relation for the magnitude of the vector sum is the Law of cosines, derived using the parallelogram method."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two vectors of magnitude 3 and 4 act at a right angle (theta=90 degrees, cos theta=0) to each other. What is the magnitude of their resultant?","answer":5,"explain":"R = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2 + 2x3x4xcos90) = sqrt(9+16+0) = sqrt(25) = 5."}]},{"id":"mp6","title":"The Tracker's Plain","icon":"📡","kind":"lesson","story":"You step out of the Vector Vault onto the Tracker's Plain, where a mysterious signal drifts along a curving path. Its position vector r changes every instant - differentiate it, and you'll unmask its velocity and acceleration.","teach":[{"title":"Position and Displacement in 2D","body":"The position vector r = x*i + y*j locates a particle using its x- and y-coordinates. If it moves from P to P', the displacement is delta-r = r' - r = delta-x*i + delta-y*j.","visual":"📍"},{"title":"Velocity is Tangent to the Path","body":"Average velocity is displacement divided by time interval. As the time interval shrinks to zero, this becomes the instantaneous velocity v = dr/dt, which always points tangent to the path in the direction of motion, never along the path itself.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Velocity and Acceleration in Components","body":"v = vx*i + vy*j where vx = dx/dt, vy = dy/dt, with speed v = sqrt(vx^2+vy^2). Acceleration a = dv/dt = ax*i + ay*j, where ax = dvx/dt, ay = dvy/dt - found by differentiating velocity component by component.","visual":"🚀"},{"title":"2D vs 1D Motion","body":"In one dimension, velocity and acceleration are always along the same line. In two or three dimensions, they can point at any angle between 0 and 180 degrees to each other.","visual":"🔀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which direction does the instantaneous velocity vector of a moving particle point?","options":["Tangent to the path, in the direction of motion","Always toward the origin","Perpendicular to the path","Along the position vector"],"answer":0,"explain":"As the time interval shrinks to zero, the displacement vector becomes tangent to the path, so instantaneous velocity always points tangentially in the direction of motion."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A particle's position is r(t) = 3.0t i + 2.0t^2 j + 5.0 k (metres). What is the y-component of its acceleration, in m/s^2?","answer":4,"explain":"vy = d(2.0t^2)/dt = 4.0t, so ay = dvy/dt = 4.0 m/s^2, constant, since v = 3.0i + 4.0t*j and a = 4.0j."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same particle, what is the magnitude of velocity (in m/s) at t = 1.0 s?","answer":5,"explain":"At t=1s, v = 3.0i + 4.0j, so |v| = sqrt(3^2+4^2) = sqrt(25) = 5.0 m/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At t = 1.0 s, this velocity makes what angle (in degrees, nearest whole number) with the x-axis? (tan theta = 4/3)","answer":53,"explain":"theta = tan^-1(4/3) is about 53 degrees, matching NCERT Example 3.4."}]},{"id":"mp7","title":"The Rogue Force Field","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"A rogue force field grips the Tracker's Plain, pushing every object with the same constant acceleration. You must predict exactly where a drifting particle will be, and how fast, using the plane's kinematic equations.","teach":[{"title":"Velocity Under Constant Acceleration","body":"When acceleration a is constant, v = v0 + at, where v0 is the velocity at t=0. In components: vx = v0x + ax*t and vy = v0y + ay*t - each axis behaves like straight-line motion independently.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Position Under Constant Acceleration","body":"The position follows r = r0 + v0*t + (1/2)*a*t^2, built the same way as the 1D case but now for full position vectors. In components: x = x0 + v0x*t + (1/2)ax*t^2, and similarly for y.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Independence of Perpendicular Motions","body":"Motion in a plane with constant acceleration is really two separate, simultaneous one-dimensional motions along perpendicular x- and y-axes - you can solve each axis on its own and then combine the results.","visual":"✂️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Under constant acceleration a, the position vector obeys which equation?","options":["r = r0 + v0*t + (1/2)a*t^2","r = r0 + a*t","r = v0*t","r = (1/2)a*t^2"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the vector form of the constant-acceleration position equation, analogous to the 1D kinematic equation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A particle starts at the origin at t=0 with velocity 5.0i m/s and constant acceleration (3.0i + 2.0j) m/s^2. At what time t (in seconds) does its x-coordinate equal 84 m? (x = 5.0t + 1.5t^2)","answer":6,"explain":"5.0t + 1.5t^2 = 84 is solved by t = 6 s (as in NCERT Example 3.5)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same particle, what is its y-coordinate (in m) at t = 6 s? (y = 1.0t^2)","answer":36,"explain":"y = 1.0 x 6^2 = 36.0 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the particle's speed (in m/s, nearest whole number) at t = 6 s, given v = (5.0+3.0t)i + 2.0t*j?","answer":26,"explain":"At t=6s, v = 23.0i + 12.0j, so speed = sqrt(23^2+12^2) = sqrt(673) is about 25.9, rounding to 26 m/s."}]},{"id":"mp8","title":"The Cliff of Trajectories","icon":"🪨","kind":"lesson","story":"At the Cliff of Trajectories, a mischievous imp hurls stones and cannonballs off the edge just to watch them fall. To out-predict him, you must split every throw into a horizontal drift and a vertical free-fall happening at the same time.","teach":[{"title":"Two Independent Components","body":"Projectile motion combines a horizontal component with zero acceleration and a vertical component with constant downward acceleration g, occurring simultaneously. Galileo first stated this independence in 1632.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Position and Velocity Equations","body":"With launch angle theta0 and speed v0 from the origin: x = (v0 cos theta0)t, y = (v0 sin theta0)t - (1/2)g*t^2. Velocity components: vx = v0 cos theta0 (constant), vy = v0 sin theta0 - g*t.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"The Path is a Parabola","body":"Eliminating t between the x and y equations gives an equation of the form y = ax + bx^2 - the equation of a parabola.","visual":"🌙"},{"title":"Time of Flight, Max Height, Range","body":"Time to reach max height: tm = v0 sin theta0 / g. Total time of flight: Tf = 2*tm. Max height: hm = (v0 sin theta0)^2 / (2g). Range: R = v0^2 sin(2*theta0) / g, maximized at theta0 = 45 degrees.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who first stated the independence of horizontal and vertical components of projectile motion, and when?","options":["Galileo, in 1632","Newton, in 1687","Huygens, in 1673","Kepler, in 1609"],"answer":0,"explain":"Galileo stated this independence in his Dialogue on the great world systems (1632)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What shape does the path of a projectile trace?","options":["A parabola","A perfect circle","A straight line","An ellipse"],"answer":0,"explain":"Eliminating time from x(t) and y(t) gives an equation of the form y = ax + bx^2, which is a parabola."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A hiker on a 490 m cliff throws a stone horizontally at 15 m/s. Using g = 9.8 m/s^2, how many seconds does it take to hit the ground? (490 = (1/2)x9.8xt^2)","answer":10,"explain":"t^2 = 2x490/9.8 = 100, so t = 10 s, as in NCERT Example 3.7."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same stone, what speed (in m/s, nearest whole number) does it hit the ground with? (vx=15, vy=-9.8x10=-98)","answer":99,"explain":"Speed = sqrt(15^2 + 98^2) = sqrt(9829) is about 99.1, rounding to 99 m/s."}]},{"id":"mp9","title":"The Imp's Duel of Angles","icon":"🏏","kind":"lesson","story":"The imp challenges you to a duel of angles: he claims two throws at different elevations can land in exactly the same spot. Prove him right or wrong by hurling a cricket ball at 28 m/s and tracking exactly where it comes down.","teach":[{"title":"Complementary Angles Give Equal Range","body":"For a fixed launch speed v0, angles of (45 degrees + alpha) and (45 degrees - alpha) give the same range R, since sin(90+2*alpha) = sin(90-2*alpha) = cos(2*alpha). Galileo noted this symmetry in Two New Sciences.","visual":"🎪"},{"title":"Maximum Range at 45 Degrees","body":"Since R = v0^2 sin(2*theta0) / g, the range is largest when sin(2*theta0) = 1, i.e. theta0 = 45 degrees, giving the maximum possible range Rm = v0^2/g for that launch speed.","visual":"🏆"},{"title":"Worked Example: The Cricket Ball","body":"A ball thrown at 28 m/s at 30 degrees above horizontal (g=9.8 m/s^2) reaches max height 10.0 m, stays aloft for about 2.9 s, and lands about 69 m from the thrower.","visual":"🏏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a given launch speed, at what angle above the horizontal is the range of a projectile maximum?","options":["45 degrees","30 degrees","60 degrees","90 degrees"],"answer":0,"explain":"R = v0^2 sin(2*theta0)/g is maximum when sin(2*theta0) = 1, i.e. theta0 = 45 degrees."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A cricket ball is thrown at 28 m/s at 30 degrees above horizontal (g=9.8 m/s^2). What is the maximum height reached, in metres? (h = (v0 sin theta0)^2 / 2g)","answer":10,"explain":"h = (28x0.5)^2/(2x9.8) = 196/19.6 = 10.0 m, matching NCERT Example 3.8."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same throw, how many seconds (to 1 decimal place) does the ball stay in the air before returning to the same height? (Tf = 2*v0*sin(theta0)/g)","answer":2.9,"explain":"Tf = 2x28x0.5/9.8 = 28/9.8 is about 2.9 s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same throw, what horizontal distance (in metres, nearest whole number) does the ball travel before landing? (R = v0^2 sin(2*theta0)/g)","answer":69,"explain":"R = 28^2 x sin(60)/9.8 = 784x0.866/9.8 is about 69 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do launch angles of 30 degrees and 60 degrees (equally spaced around 45 degrees) give the same range for the same speed?","options":["Because sin(60) = sin(120), i.e. sin(2x30) = sin(2x60)","Because both angles give the same maximum height","Because gravity only acts at exactly 45 degrees","Because horizontal velocity is the same at both angles"],"answer":0,"explain":"R depends on sin(2*theta0); since 2x30=60 and 2x60=120, and sin(60)=sin(120), the ranges are equal - this is Galileo's complementary angle result."}]},{"id":"mp10","title":"The Ring of Ceaseless Turning","icon":"🎡","kind":"lesson","story":"The final trial before the Boss Chamber is the Ring of Ceaseless Turning - a track where speed never changes but direction always does. Only by understanding centripetal acceleration can you explain why something moving at constant speed is still accelerating.","teach":[{"title":"Constant Speed, Changing Direction","body":"In uniform circular motion, speed stays constant but direction continuously changes, so the object is always accelerating. This acceleration is directed toward the centre of the circle - called centripetal acceleration.","visual":"🎡"},{"title":"Magnitude of Centripetal Acceleration","body":"The centripetal acceleration has magnitude ac = v^2/R, where v is the constant speed and R is the radius. Though its magnitude is constant, its direction keeps changing, so ac itself is not a constant vector.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Angular Speed and Its Relations","body":"Angular speed omega is the rate of change of angular displacement. It relates to linear speed by v = omega*R, and centripetal acceleration can also be written ac = omega^2*R.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Period, Frequency, and Full Revolutions","body":"Time period T is the time for one revolution; frequency nu = 1/T. Since distance per revolution is 2*pi*R: v = 2*pi*R/T = 2*pi*R*nu, and ac = 4*pi^2*nu^2*R.","visual":"⏱️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In uniform circular motion, in which direction does the acceleration point?","options":["Toward the centre of the circle","Along the direction of velocity","Away from the centre","Tangent to the circle"],"answer":0,"explain":"Centripetal acceleration always points toward the centre of the circular path."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An insect moves steadily around a circular groove of radius 12 cm, completing 7 revolutions in 100 s. What is its angular speed in rad/s (2 decimal places)? (omega = 2*pi*7/100)","answer":0.44,"explain":"omega = 2*pi*7/100 is about 0.44 rad/s, as in NCERT Example 3.9."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same insect, what is its linear speed in cm/s (1 decimal place)? (v = omega*R)","answer":5.3,"explain":"v = 0.44 x 12 = 5.28, rounding to 5.3 cm/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same insect, what is the magnitude of its acceleration in cm/s^2 (1 decimal place)? (a = omega^2 * R)","answer":2.3,"explain":"a = (0.44)^2 x 12 = 0.1936x12 is about 2.3 cm/s^2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Is the acceleration vector in uniform circular motion constant?","options":["No - its magnitude is constant but its direction continuously changes","Yes - both magnitude and direction are constant","No - both magnitude and direction change","Yes, because speed is constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"The magnitude v^2/R stays fixed, but since the direction (always toward the centre) keeps changing as the object moves around the circle, the acceleration vector itself is not constant."}]},{"id":"mp-boss","title":"The Vector Sentinel","icon":"🛡️","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Tower stands the Vector Sentinel, an armored guardian built from every arrow, rain cloud, cannonball, and spinning ring you've mastered. Defeat it by proving you can wield scalars, vectors, and the full mathematics of motion in a plane, all at once.","teach":[{"title":"Everything Comes Together","body":"This chapter built from scalars and vectors, through graphical and analytical addition, resolution into components, and finally into motion in a plane - constant acceleration, projectile motion, and uniform circular motion all rest on the same vector foundation.","visual":"🛡️"}],"boss":{"name":"The Vector Sentinel","taunt":"Magnitude without direction is nothing! Show me you understand BOTH, traveler, or be swept off this parabola forever!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is a vector quantity?","options":["Displacement","Mass","Temperature","Time"],"answer":0,"explain":"Displacement has both magnitude and direction, unlike the purely scalar mass, temperature, and time."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Rain falls vertically at 35 m/s and wind blows horizontally at 12 m/s. What is the resultant speed, in m/s?","answer":37,"explain":"R = sqrt(35^2+12^2) = sqrt(1369) = 37 m/s (Example 3.1)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A motorboat heads north at 25 km/h while a 10 km/h current acts at 120 degrees to it. What is the resultant speed in km/h (nearest whole number)?","answer":22,"explain":"R = sqrt(25^2+10^2+2x25x10xcos120) = sqrt(475) is about 22 km/h (Example 3.3)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A cricket ball thrown at 28 m/s at 30 degrees above horizontal reaches what maximum height, in metres?","answer":10,"explain":"hm = (v0 sin theta0)^2 / 2g = 14^2/19.6 = 10.0 m (Example 3.8)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"That same cricket ball lands how far (in metres, nearest whole number) from the thrower?","answer":69,"explain":"R = v0^2 sin(2*theta0)/g = 784xsin(60)/9.8 is about 69 m (Example 3.8)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An insect in a 12 cm radius groove completing 7 revolutions in 100 s has what centripetal acceleration, in cm/s^2 (1 decimal place)?","answer":2.3,"explain":"a = omega^2*R = (0.44)^2x12 is about 2.3 cm/s^2 (Example 3.9)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the acceleration in uniform circular motion NOT a constant vector, even though speed is constant?","options":["Its direction keeps changing even though its magnitude stays the same","Its magnitude keeps changing","Both magnitude and direction change","It actually is constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"Centripetal acceleration always points toward the centre; as the object moves around the circle this direction continuously changes, even though |ac|=v^2/R stays fixed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A stone thrown horizontally off a 490 m cliff at 15 m/s takes 10 s to land. What is true about its horizontal velocity during the fall?","options":["It stays constant at 15 m/s throughout","It increases due to gravity","It decreases to zero","It becomes negative"],"answer":0,"explain":"Horizontal and vertical motions are independent; with no horizontal acceleration, vx = 15 m/s stays constant while only vy changes under gravity (Example 3.7)."}]}]},{"id":"lawsofmotion11","index":3,"name":"Laws of Motion","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 4","emoji":"🚀","palette":{"from":"#fb923c","to":"#7c2d12"},"lessons":[{"id":"lm1","title":"Aristotle's Fallacy","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into a crumbling Greek lecture hall where the ghost of Aristotle insists: 'An external force is required to keep a body in motion!' He points to a toy cart that stops the instant you let go of its string. Something about his argument feels wrong -- can you find the flaw before he traps you in his logic forever?","teach":[{"title":"Aristotle's Claim","body":"Aristotle (384-322 B.C.) held that a body needs a continuous external force to keep moving -- for example, he believed an arrow keeps flying because air behind it keeps pushing it. This matches everyday experience: a toy car stops the instant you stop pulling its string.","visual":"🏹"},{"title":"The Hidden Force","body":"The real reason the toy car stops is friction from the floor opposing its motion. As long as the car moves at constant velocity, the child's pulling force exactly cancels the floor's friction -- the net external force is zero, not absent.","visual":"🛞"},{"title":"Where Aristotle Went Wrong","body":"Aristotle mistook the force needed to overcome friction for a force needed to sustain motion itself. To find the true law of motion, one must imagine an idealised world with no friction -- exactly what Galileo did next.","visual":"❌"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Aristotle's view of motion, what is required to keep a body moving?","options":["A continuous external force acting on it","Nothing -- motion continues forever on its own","Only an initial push, nothing after that","A force only if the body is heavy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aristotle believed motion needed a sustained force, illustrated by his claim that air continuously pushes a flying arrow."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a toy car pulled by a string come to rest the moment the string is released?","options":["Friction between the car and the floor opposes its motion","The string was the only thing giving it mass","Air pressure pushes it backward","Its inertia disappears instantly"],"answer":0,"explain":"Friction from the floor decelerates the car once the applied pulling force is removed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the actual flaw in Aristotle's argument, according to the chapter?","options":["He failed to recognise that friction, not the absence of force, is what needs overcoming","He didn't believe forces existed at all","He thought gravity was the only force in nature","He assumed all bodies were frictionless"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aristotle mistook the force needed to counter friction for a force needed to sustain motion itself."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When a toy car moves in uniform motion after being pulled, what is true about the net external force on it?","options":["It is zero -- the pulling force cancels friction","It equals the full pulling force","It is negative","It keeps increasing"],"answer":0,"explain":"For uniform (constant velocity) motion, the applied force and friction sum to zero net force."}]},{"id":"lm2","title":"Galileo's Law of Inertia","icon":"🎢","kind":"lesson","story":"Aristotle's ghost fades, replaced by Galileo's workshop full of polished ramps. He challenges you to release a ball down one incline and watch it climb the opposite one. 'Tell me,' he says, 'what happens if I make the far ramp flatter... and flatter still?'","teach":[{"title":"Objects on Inclines","body":"Galileo observed that objects moving down an inclined plane accelerate, while those moving up retard (decelerate). Motion on a horizontal plane is the in-between case -- Galileo reasoned a frictionless horizontal plane should produce neither acceleration nor retardation, just constant velocity.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"The Double Incline","body":"A ball released from rest on one inclined plane rolls down and climbs the other, reaching nearly (never more than) its original height. If friction were absent, it would reach exactly the same height every time.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Flattening the Ramp","body":"As the second plane's slope is decreased, the ball travels farther to reach the same height. In the limiting case of a horizontal plane, the ball would need to travel an infinite distance to regain that height -- meaning, ideally, its motion would never cease.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"What Is Inertia?","body":"Galileo concluded that the state of rest and the state of uniform motion are equivalent -- both have zero net force. This property of a body to resist any change in its state of rest or uniform motion is called inertia.","visual":"🪨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"On Galileo's inclined plane, what happens to an object moving down the slope?","options":["It accelerates","It retards (slows down)","It moves at constant velocity","It stops immediately"],"answer":0,"explain":"Objects moving down an incline speed up (accelerate), per Galileo's observation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the double inclined-plane experiment (frictionless, ideal case), how does the ball's final height on the second ramp compare to its initial release height?","options":["It is the same as the initial height","It is always greater","It is always less, no matter what","It becomes zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the ideal frictionless case, the ball would climb back to exactly the same height it was released from."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the second ramp is made perfectly horizontal (zero slope) and there's no friction, what does Galileo conclude about the ball's motion?","options":["It would travel forever, never stopping, moving at constant velocity","It would stop immediately","It would accelerate infinitely","It would reverse direction on its own"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the horizontal, frictionless limiting case, the ball's motion never ceases -- this is the seed of the law of inertia."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does 'inertia' mean, as defined in the chapter?","options":["The property of a body to resist any change in its state of rest or uniform motion","The tendency of a body to speed up over time","The force that stops all motion","The weight of an object"],"answer":0,"explain":"Inertia means 'resistance to change' -- a body keeps its state of rest or uniform velocity unless compelled otherwise by external force."}]},{"id":"lm3","title":"Newton's First Law: Adrift in Space","icon":"🧑‍🚀","kind":"lesson","story":"Galileo's insight becomes law under Isaac Newton's careful hand. Suddenly you're an astronaut, accidentally cut loose from your spaceship as it accelerates through the void at 100 m/s². No stars nearby to pull on you, no ship pushing you anymore. What happens to you now?","teach":[{"title":"The First Law","body":"Newton formalised Galileo's law of inertia as his First Law: 'Every body continues to be in its state of rest or of uniform motion in a straight line unless compelled by some external force to act otherwise.' In short: if the net external force on a body is zero, its acceleration is zero.","visual":"🌌"},{"title":"Reading the Law Both Ways","body":"Sometimes we know the net force is zero and conclude acceleration is zero (like a drifting astronaut with no forces on him). Other times we observe a body is unaccelerated and conclude the net force on it must be zero -- even though individual forces like gravity and friction are still acting and simply cancel out.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"The Book on the Table","body":"A book at rest on a table feels its weight W downward and the table's normal force R upward. The correct logic is: since the book is observed at rest, the first law tells us net force is zero, so R must equal W -- not the other way around.","visual":"📖"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An astronaut is accidentally separated from his spaceship, which was accelerating through interstellar space at 100 m/s². With no nearby stars or the ship acting on him, what is the astronaut's own acceleration (in m/s²) the instant after separation?","answer":0,"explain":"With zero net external force acting on the astronaut, Newton's First Law says his acceleration must be zero -- he simply drifts at constant velocity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which is the correct application of Newton's First Law to a book resting on a table?","options":["Since the book is observed at rest, the net force must be zero, so the normal force R equals the weight W","Since R equals W, the forces cancel and therefore the book is at rest","The book is at rest because it has no weight","R and W are an action-reaction pair, so the book is at rest"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter stresses that the correct reasoning starts from the observed state of rest and concludes the net force (and hence R = W) -- not the reverse."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A spaceship is far out in interstellar space with all its rockets turned off and no nearby objects exerting force on it. According to the First Law, if it is already moving, what will it do?","options":["Continue moving with uniform (constant) velocity","Gradually slow down and stop","Accelerate on its own","Reverse direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"With zero net external force, the First Law demands zero acceleration, so the ship keeps its uniform velocity forever."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In simple terms, Newton's First Law states that if the net external force on a body is zero, its acceleration is...","options":["Zero","Equal to its velocity","Equal to g","Undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the direct restatement of the First Law given in the chapter."}]},{"id":"lm4","title":"Momentum & the Second Law","icon":"🎳","kind":"lesson","story":"Newton hands you a rifle and a block of wood. 'Fire,' he says, 'and tell me how hard the wood must push back to stop your bullet.' You realize mass and velocity together -- momentum -- hold the key to every force calculation ahead.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Momentum?","body":"Momentum p of a body is defined as the product of its mass m and velocity v: p = mv. It's a vector quantity, and experience shows it's the key quantity that determines how motion responds to force -- a truck needs a much bigger force than a car to reach the same speed in the same time.","visual":"🚚"},{"title":"The Second Law","body":"Newton's Second Law states: the rate of change of momentum of a body is directly proportional to the applied force, and takes place in the direction the force acts. Written as F = dp/dt, and for constant mass this becomes F = ma.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Defining the Newton","body":"Choosing the proportionality constant k = 1 in SI units gives F = ma directly. The unit of force, the newton (N), is defined as the force that gives a 1 kg mass an acceleration of 1 m/s²: 1 N = 1 kg·m/s².","visual":"⚙️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A bullet of mass 0.04 kg moving at 90 m/s enters a wooden block and stops after travelling 0.6 m inside it. Using retardation a = u²/(2s), the average resistive force on the bullet works out to how many newtons?","answer":270,"explain":"a = 90²/(2×0.6) = 6750 m/s²; F = ma = 0.04 × 6750 = 270 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of force, defined as the force needed to give a 1 kg mass an acceleration of 1 m/s²?","options":["The newton (N)","The joule (J)","The pascal (Pa)","The watt (W)"],"answer":0,"explain":"1 N = 1 kg·m/s², as defined via Newton's Second Law with k = 1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A constant retarding force of 50 N is applied to a body of mass 20 kg moving initially at 15 m/s. Using a = F/m for the retardation, how many seconds does it take the body to stop?","answer":6,"explain":"a = F/m = 50/20 = 2.5 m/s² (retarding); t = u/a = 15/2.5 = 6 s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newton's Second Law says the rate of change of momentum of a body is directly proportional to the applied force and takes place...","options":["In the direction in which the force acts","Opposite to the direction of the force","Perpendicular to the force","Only along the body's original velocity direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the exact statement of the Second Law from the chapter."}]},{"id":"lm5","title":"Impulse: The Cricketer's Secret","icon":"🏏","kind":"lesson","story":"A fast ball rockets toward you. Catch it stiff-armed and it'll sting for days -- but a seasoned cricketer draws the hands back as the ball arrives. Newton wants you to understand why stretching out the time of contact is the trick to surviving the catch.","teach":[{"title":"Defining Impulse","body":"When a large force acts for a very short time to produce a measurable change in momentum, the product of force and time -- impulse -- is what we can measure, even when the force and exact duration are hard to know separately: Impulse = Force × time = change in momentum.","visual":"💥"},{"title":"The Cricketer's Trick","body":"A seasoned cricketer draws the hands backward while catching a fast ball, allowing a longer time for the ball to stop. Since the same change in momentum spread over a longer time needs a smaller force, this technique avoids hurting the hands.","visual":"🤝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A batsman hits a ball straight back toward the bowler without changing its speed of 12 m/s. If the ball's mass is 0.15 kg, what is the magnitude of the impulse imparted to the ball, in N·s? (Change in momentum = m×12 − m×(−12))","answer":3.6,"explain":"Change in momentum = 0.15×12 − (−0.15×12) = 1.8 + 1.8 = 3.6 N·s, which equals the impulse."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a seasoned cricketer draw the hands backward while catching a fast-moving ball?","options":["It increases the time of contact, reducing the force needed for the same change in momentum","It increases the ball's momentum","It reduces the mass of the ball","It has no physical effect, it's just habit"],"answer":0,"explain":"The same change in momentum over a longer time interval requires a smaller applied force -- that's why it hurts less."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two billiard balls, each of mass 0.05 kg, move in opposite directions at 6 m/s, collide, and rebound with the same speed. What is the magnitude of the impulse (in N·s) imparted to each ball by the other? (Use change in momentum = m×6 − m×(−6))","answer":0.6,"explain":"Change in momentum = 0.05×6 − (−0.05×6) = 0.3 + 0.3 = 0.6 N·s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Newtonian mechanics, how is an 'impulsive force' different from any other force?","options":["It is simply a force that is large and acts for a very short time -- not a fundamentally different category","It obeys different laws than ordinary force","It cannot be measured","It always points opposite to velocity"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states Newtonian mechanics makes no fundamental distinction -- an impulsive force is just a large force acting briefly."}]},{"id":"lm6","title":"Newton's Third Law: Billiards at the Wall","icon":"🎱","kind":"lesson","story":"Two identical billiard balls streak toward a rigid wall -- one straight on, one at a slanted angle -- and rebound with unchanged speed. Newton asks you a trick question: in which case does the wall feel a slanted push, and in which a straight one? Your instinct is about to betray you.","teach":[{"title":"The Third Law","body":"Newton's Third Law: 'To every action, there is always an equal and opposite reaction.' More clearly stated: forces always occur in pairs -- the force on body A by body B is equal and opposite to the force on body B by A (FAB = −FBA).","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"No Cause and Effect","body":"Action and reaction act at the same instant -- there is no cause-effect relationship, and either force can be called 'action' with the other as 'reaction'. Crucially, action and reaction always act on different bodies, so they never cancel each other out for either body alone.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"The Billiard Ball Surprise","body":"When two identical balls strike a wall at different angles (say straight-on and at 30°) and rebound at the same speed, the force on the wall is normal (perpendicular) to the wall in BOTH cases -- not along the ball's incoming direction. This is found using the Second Law on the ball's momentum change, then applying the Third Law.","visual":"🎱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Newton's Third Law, the force on body A due to body B (FAB) is related to the force on body B due to A (FBA) by:","options":["FAB = −FBA (equal in magnitude, opposite in direction)","FAB = FBA (identical, same direction)","FAB = 2×FBA","There is no fixed relationship"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the vector statement of the third law: mutual forces between two bodies are always equal and opposite."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Action and reaction forces in Newton's Third Law always act on:","options":["Different bodies -- so they can never cancel out for a single body","The same body -- so they always cancel out","Whichever body is heavier","No specific body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because they act on different bodies, action and reaction forces are never added together to find the net force on one body."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Example 4.5, two identical billiard balls hit a wall at different angles and rebound with unchanged speed. What is the direction of the force on the wall due to each ball?","options":["Normal (perpendicular) to the wall, in both cases","Along the ball's incoming direction, in both cases","Parallel to the wall","It cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"Even though the balls approach at different angles, analysis of the momentum change shows the force on the wall is normal to it in both cases -- a common misconception is that it follows the ball's approach angle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Is there a cause-and-effect relationship between 'action' and 'reaction' in Newton's Third Law -- does action happen first and cause reaction?","options":["No -- action and reaction occur simultaneously, and either can be labelled which","Yes -- action always happens a fraction of a second before reaction","Yes, but only for contact forces","No -- reaction happens before action"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter explicitly states there is no cause-effect relation implied; the two forces act at the same instant."}]},{"id":"lm7","title":"Conservation of Momentum: The Recoiling Gun","icon":"🔫","kind":"lesson","story":"You fire a gun. Newton points out that as the bullet rockets forward, something else happens to the gun in your hands -- an equal and opposite response that no shooter ever forgets. Time to prove momentum never truly disappears, it just moves around.","teach":[{"title":"Gun and Bullet","body":"When a gun fires a bullet, the force on the bullet by the gun (F) and the force on the gun by the bullet (−F) act for the same time interval Δt (Third Law). Since both start at rest, the bullet's final momentum pb and the gun's recoil momentum pg satisfy pb + pg = 0 -- total momentum stays zero.","visual":"💨"},{"title":"The Law of Conservation of Momentum","body":"In an isolated system (no external force), mutual forces between pairs of particles change individual momenta, but because each pair's forces are equal and opposite, these changes cancel out in pairs. The total momentum of an isolated system of interacting particles is conserved.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Collisions","body":"For two colliding bodies A and B, the total momentum before collision (pA + pB) equals the total momentum after (p'A + p'B) -- this holds true whether the collision is elastic or inelastic. Only in elastic collisions is kinetic energy also conserved.","visual":"💫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A shell of mass 0.020 kg is fired by a gun of mass 100 kg, with a muzzle speed of 80 m/s. Using conservation of momentum (initial total momentum = 0), what is the recoil speed of the gun, in m/s?","answer":0.016,"explain":"m_shell × v_shell = m_gun × v_recoil → 0.020×80 = 100×v_recoil → v_recoil = 1.6/100 = 0.016 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The law of conservation of momentum states that the total momentum of an isolated system of interacting particles is:","options":["Conserved (constant), as long as there is no external force","Always increasing","Always zero","Only conserved if the particles never touch"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the direct statement of the law, following from the second and third laws of motion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For two colliding bodies, momentum conservation (pA + pB = p'A + p'B) holds true for:","options":["Both elastic and inelastic collisions","Only elastic collisions","Only inelastic collisions","Only collisions with no friction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Momentum conservation applies regardless of collision type; kinetic energy conservation is the extra condition unique to elastic collisions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What additionally must be conserved for a collision to be classified as elastic?","options":["Total kinetic energy of the system","Total mass of the system","The direction of motion","The shape of each body"],"answer":0,"explain":"In elastic collisions, total initial kinetic energy equals total final kinetic energy, in addition to momentum being conserved."}]},{"id":"lm8","title":"Equilibrium: The Pulled Rope","icon":"🪢","kind":"lesson","story":"A 6 kg weight hangs from a 2 m rope fixed to the ceiling. You yank the rope's midpoint sideways with a steady 50 N horizontal force. The rope tilts and settles at an angle -- find that angle, and you'll understand what it truly means for a particle to be in equilibrium.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Equilibrium?","body":"A particle is in equilibrium when the net external force on it is zero -- by the First Law, this means it's either at rest or moving uniformly. For two forces to balance, they must be equal and opposite: F1 = −F2.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Three or More Forces","body":"For three concurrent forces in equilibrium, their vector sum must be zero: F1 + F2 + F3 = 0. They can be represented as the three sides of a closed triangle, with arrows taken in the same sense -- and this generalises to any number of forces forming a closed polygon.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Solving the Rope Problem","body":"For a 6 kg mass hung by a rope, with a 50 N horizontal pull applied at the midpoint P: the lower segment's tension T2 must support the weight, so T2 = 6×10 = 60 N. At P, the upper tension T1 balances both T2 vertically and the 50 N pull horizontally: T1 cos θ = 60 N and T1 sin θ = 50 N.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 6 kg mass hangs in equilibrium from the lower segment of a rope (take g = 10 m/s²). What is the tension T2 in that lower segment, in newtons?","answer":60,"explain":"By the First Law, since the weight is at rest, T2 must equal the weight: T2 = 6 × 10 = 60 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a particle in equilibrium under three concurrent forces F1, F2, and F3, what condition must hold?","options":["F1 + F2 + F3 = 0 (their vector sum is zero)","F1 = F2 = F3 in magnitude","Only two of the three forces need to balance","F1 × F2 × F3 = 0"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equilibrium under any number of concurrent forces requires their vector sum to vanish."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At point P in the hanging-rope example, T1 cos θ = 60 N and T1 sin θ = 50 N. What is the value of tan θ (to 2 decimal places)?","answer":0.83,"explain":"tan θ = (T1 sin θ)/(T1 cos θ) = 50/60 ≈ 0.83, giving θ ≈ 40°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A particle in equilibrium under several concurrent forces F1, F2, ... Fn can be represented geometrically by:","options":["The sides of a closed polygon with arrows directed in the same sense","A single straight line","Two parallel lines","A circle whose radius equals the largest force"],"answer":0,"explain":"This generalises the triangle-of-forces rule (for 3 forces) to any number of concurrent forces in equilibrium."}]},{"id":"lm9","title":"Friction: Grip or Slip","icon":"🛹","kind":"lesson","story":"Newton leads you onto a tilting ramp workshop -- a box on a truck floor, a crate on a steepening incline, a trolley dragged by a hanging weight. Every scenario hinges on one quiet, self-adjusting force that decides whether things grip... or slip.","teach":[{"title":"Contact Forces","body":"Besides gravity, mechanics deals mainly with contact forces: the normal reaction (perpendicular to the contact surfaces) and friction (parallel to the surfaces, opposing relative motion). Tension in a string and spring force (F = −kx) are two other common forces.","visual":"🤝"},{"title":"Static Friction","body":"Static friction fs is self-adjusting -- it only appears once an applied force exists, and grows to match it (opposing 'impending motion') up to a maximum limit: fs ≤ μsN, where μs is the coefficient of static friction and N is the normal force.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Kinetic Friction","body":"Once the applied force exceeds the maximum static friction, the body slides, and kinetic friction fk = μkN takes over -- it's roughly independent of contact area and velocity, and experiments show μk is always less than μs.","visual":"🛷"},{"title":"Rolling Friction","body":"A body rolling without slipping suffers, in principle, no friction at all -- but in practice, some rolling friction remains, though it is far smaller (even by 2-3 orders of magnitude) than static or kinetic friction. This is why the wheel, ball bearings, and lubricants are such powerful tools for reducing friction.","visual":"🛞"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A box rests on the floor of an accelerating train. The coefficient of static friction between box and floor is 0.15 (take g = 10 m/s²). What is the maximum acceleration (in m/s²) the train can have before the box starts to slide?","answer":1.5,"explain":"a_max = μs g = 0.15 × 10 = 1.5 m/s², from ma ≤ μs N = μs mg."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 4 kg block on a horizontal plane just begins to slide when the plane is tilted to θ = 15°. What is the coefficient of static friction μs between block and surface (to 2 decimal places), given tan θmax = μs?","answer":0.27,"explain":"μs = tan 15° ≈ 0.27; note this result doesn't depend on the block's mass."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 3 kg block hangs via a string over a pulley, connected to a 20 kg trolley on a surface with μk = 0.04 (take g = 10 m/s²). Solving 30 − T = 3a and T − μk(20×10) = 20a together, the acceleration works out to about 0.96 m/s². What is the tension T in the string, in newtons (to 1 decimal place)?","answer":27.1,"explain":"Substituting a ≈ 0.96 m/s² back into 30 − T = 3a gives T = 30 − 3(0.96) ≈ 27.1 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is true about kinetic friction (fk = μkN) compared to static friction?","options":["The coefficient μk is generally less than μs","μk is always greater than μs","μk and μs are always exactly equal","Kinetic friction increases with velocity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Experiments show the coefficient of kinetic friction is smaller than the coefficient of static friction, and kinetic friction is nearly independent of velocity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is rolling friction (as with wheels or ball bearings) so useful for reducing power loss in machines?","options":["It is much smaller than static or sliding friction, even by 2-3 orders of magnitude","It is exactly zero in all real situations","It increases the normal force","It only works on smooth ice"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rolling friction is dramatically smaller than static/kinetic friction, which is why wheels and ball bearings were such important inventions."}]},{"id":"lm10","title":"Circular Motion: Racing the Banked Curve","icon":"🏎️","kind":"lesson","story":"The final stretch before the boss fight: a racetrack banked at a steep angle, and a cyclist screaming into a sharp unbanked turn. Newton wants to know -- will you find the centripetal force in time, or will you go sliding off the track?","teach":[{"title":"Centripetal Force","body":"A body moving in a circle of radius R with uniform speed v has acceleration v²/R directed toward the centre. By the Second Law, the centripetal force fc = mv²/R must point toward the centre too -- provided by tension (a string), gravity (planets), or friction (a car on a road), depending on the situation.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Car on a Level Road","body":"For a car turning on a flat road, friction alone supplies the centripetal force. Since f ≤ μsN and N = mg, the maximum safe turning speed is vmax = √(μsRg) -- notably independent of the car's mass.","visual":"🛣️"},{"title":"Car on a Banked Road","body":"Banking the road lets the normal force also contribute to the centripetal force, reducing reliance on friction. At the optimum speed v0 = √(Rg tan θ), no friction is needed at all -- this minimises tyre wear, and the maximum possible speed on a banked road is always greater than on a flat one.","visual":"🛞"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A cyclist at 18 km/h (= 5 m/s) takes a turn of radius 3 m on a level road, with μs = 0.1 (take g = 9.8 m/s²). What is the value of μsRg (in m²/s²)?","answer":2.94,"explain":"μsRg = 0.1 × 3 × 9.8 = 2.94 m²/s²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The cyclist's v² works out to 25 m²/s² (since v = 5 m/s), compared to μsRg = 2.94 m²/s². Since the no-slip condition is v² ≤ μsRg, what happens to the cyclist?","options":["The cyclist will slip while taking the turn, since v² far exceeds μsRg","The cyclist is perfectly safe","The bicycle will speed up automatically","Friction becomes negative"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because v² (25) is much greater than μsRg (2.94), the required centripetal force exceeds what friction can supply, so the cyclist slips."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A racetrack of radius 300 m is banked at 15° (take g = 9.8 m/s²). What is the optimum speed v0 = √(Rg tan θ), in m/s (to 1 decimal place), at which no friction is needed to avoid tyre wear?","answer":28.1,"explain":"v0 = √(300 × 9.8 × tan 15°) = √(300 × 9.8 × 0.268) ≈ 28.1 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a car turning on a level (unbanked) road, what provides the centripetal force?","options":["Friction between the tyres and the road","The car's engine directly","Air resistance","The normal reaction alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"On a flat road, the only horizontal force available is friction, so it alone supplies the centripetal force."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The maximum safe turning speed on a level road, vmax = √(μsRg), is notably independent of:","options":["The mass of the car","The radius of the turn","The coefficient of static friction","The value of g"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mass cancels out of the equation ma = μsmg, so vmax depends only on μs, R, and g -- not on the car's mass."}]},{"id":"lm-boss","title":"The Newton Trial","icon":"🍎","kind":"boss","story":"The ghost of Aristotle, the ramps of Galileo, and the recoiling gun all converge in one final proving ground. Newton himself appears, apple in hand, ready to test whether you truly understand force, momentum, and motion -- or whether you'll be sent tumbling by your own miscalculations.","boss":{"name":"Sir Isaac's Proving Ground","taunt":"Every force has its reaction, apprentice. Show me you understand mine, or be swept off this track by your own inertia."},"teach":[{"title":"The Three Laws, Together","body":"First Law: no net force means no acceleration. Second Law: F = ma (or dp/dt) when there is a net force. Third Law: every force on a body comes paired with an equal, opposite force on another body. Together with conservation of momentum, these three laws form the foundation of mechanics.","visual":"🍏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In simple terms, Newton's First Law says: if the net external force on a body is zero, its acceleration is...","options":["Zero","Constant but nonzero","Equal to g","Impossible to determine"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zero net force means zero acceleration -- the core statement of the First Law."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A constant force acts on a 3.0 kg body, changing its speed from 2.0 m/s to 3.5 m/s in 25 s (direction unchanged). What is the magnitude of the force, in newtons (to 2 decimal places)?","answer":0.18,"explain":"a = (3.5−2.0)/25 = 0.06 m/s²; F = ma = 3.0 × 0.06 = 0.18 N."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 5 kg body is acted on by two perpendicular forces, 8 N and 6 N. What is the magnitude of its acceleration, in m/s²?","answer":2,"explain":"Net force = √(8²+6²) = √100 = 10 N; a = F/m = 10/5 = 2 m/s²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A rocket with lift-off mass 20,000 kg blasts upward with an initial acceleration of 5.0 m/s² (take g = 10 m/s²). Using F − mg = ma, what is the initial thrust, in newtons?","answer":300000,"explain":"F = m(g+a) = 20000 × (10+5) = 300000 N."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two masses, 8 kg and 12 kg, are connected over a frictionless pulley by a light inextensible string (take g = 10 m/s²). Using a = (m2−m1)g/(m1+m2), what is the magnitude of their acceleration, in m/s²?","answer":2,"explain":"a = (12−8)×10/(8+12) = 40/20 = 2 m/s²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Action and reaction forces (Newton's Third Law) always act on:","options":["Different bodies, so they never cancel for a single body's motion","The same body, cancelling each other exactly","Whichever object has more mass","No object in particular"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because action and reaction act on different bodies, they can never be summed to give a 'net zero' for either body alone."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which relationship between the coefficients of friction is experimentally true?","options":["μk (kinetic) is less than μs (static)","μk is always greater than μs","μk equals μs exactly","Neither depends on the surfaces in contact"],"answer":0,"explain":"Experiments consistently show the coefficient of kinetic friction is smaller than the coefficient of static friction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The law of conservation of momentum applies to:","options":["An isolated system of particles with no net external force","Any system, even with large external forces","Only systems with a single particle","Only elastic collisions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Momentum is conserved specifically for isolated systems free of net external force -- this follows from Newton's second and third laws."}]}]},{"id":"workenergypower11","index":4,"name":"Work, Energy and Power","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 5","emoji":"⚡","palette":{"from":"#facc15","to":"#713f12"},"lessons":[{"id":"wk1","title":"The Vector Temple","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"The gates of the Study Atlas won't budge until you learn to multiply vectors the physicist's way. An old inscription hints that force and displacement, combined correctly, unlock the path to true 'work'.","teach":[{"title":"The Scalar (Dot) Product","body":"The scalar product of two vectors A and B is written A.B and defined as A.B = AB cosθ, where θ is the angle between them. Since A, B and cosθ are all scalars, A.B is a scalar with no direction of its own.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Unit Vectors","body":"For the unit vectors i, j, k along the x, y, z axes: i.i = j.j = k.k = 1, while i.j = j.k = k.i = 0, because perpendicular vectors have θ = 90° so cosθ = 0.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Component Form","body":"For A = Ax i + Ay j + Az k and B = Bx i + By j + Bz k, the dot product works out to A.B = AxBx + AyBy + AzBz — just multiply matching components and add.","visual":"✖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The scalar product of two vectors A and B is defined as:","options":["A.B = AB cosθ","A.B = AB sinθ","A.B = A + B","A.B = AB tanθ"],"answer":0,"explain":"A.B = AB cosθ, where θ is the angle between the vectors."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For unit vectors i, j, k, what is i.j equal to?","options":["0","1","-1","i+j"],"answer":0,"explain":"i and j are perpendicular, so cos 90° = 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Force F = 3i + 4j - 5k units acts while displacement is d = 5i + 4j + 3k units. Find F.d (in the same units).","answer":16,"explain":"F.d = 3(5) + 4(4) + (-5)(3) = 15 + 16 - 15 = 16 unit."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same force F = 3i + 4j - 5k units, what is F.F (i.e. |F|²)?","answer":50,"explain":"F.F = 3² + 4² + (-5)² = 9 + 16 + 25 = 50 unit."}]},{"id":"wk2","title":"Raindrops and Skidding Wheels","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"A storm cloud looms over the Atlas and a lone raindrop plummets toward the ground, while a cyclist skids to a halt on the path below. Both scenes hide the same secret law: the work-energy theorem.","teach":[{"title":"The Work-Energy Theorem","body":"The work-energy theorem states that the change in kinetic energy of a body equals the work done on it by the net force: Kf - Ki = W. This follows directly from v² - u² = 2as combined with Newton's second law.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Work by a Constant Force","body":"Work done by a force is the product of the component of the force along the displacement and the magnitude of the displacement: W = (F cosθ) d = F.d.","visual":"🏹"},{"title":"When Work Is Zero","body":"No work is done if the displacement is zero (a weightlifter holding a mass steady), if the force is zero, or if force and displacement are mutually perpendicular (like gravity on a satellite in circular orbit).","visual":"🚫"},{"title":"Sign of Work","body":"Work can be positive or negative. If θ is between 0° and 90°, cosθ is positive and work is positive; if θ is between 90° and 180° (like friction opposing motion), cosθ is negative and work is negative.","visual":"➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1.00 g raindrop falls from a height of 1.00 km. Taking g = 10 m/s², what is the work done by gravity Wg (in J)? [Wg = mgh]","answer":10,"explain":"Wg = mgh = 10⁻³ kg × 10 m/s² × 10³ m = 10.0 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The same raindrop hits the ground at 50.0 m/s, starting from rest. What is its change in kinetic energy ΔK (in J)? [ΔK = ½mv²]","answer":1.25,"explain":"ΔK = ½ × 10⁻³ kg × (50.0 m/s)² = 1.25 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the work-energy theorem ΔK = Wg + Wr, find the work Wr (in J) done by the unknown resistive force on the raindrop.","answer":-8.75,"explain":"Wr = ΔK - Wg = 1.25 - 10 = -8.75 J (negative, since resistance opposes the fall)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A cyclist skids to a stop in 10 m; the road exerts 200 N directly opposing the motion. What is the work done by the road on the cycle?","options":["-2000 J","2000 J","0 J","-200 J"],"answer":0,"explain":"Wr = Fd cos180° = 200 × 10 × (-1) = -2000 J; this negative work brings the cycle to a halt."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"By Newton's third law an equal 200 N force acts on the road from the cycle, but the road itself does not move. What is the work done by the cycle on the road (in J)?","answer":0,"explain":"Work requires displacement of the point of application; the road undergoes no displacement, so the work done on it is zero."}]},{"id":"wk3","title":"The Ballistics Range","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"A police officer at the Atlas's training range fires a test bullet through soft plywood. To predict how fast it emerges, you'll need to master kinetic energy — the energy a body carries by virtue of its motion.","teach":[{"title":"Kinetic Energy","body":"If an object of mass m has velocity v, its kinetic energy is K = ½mv.v = ½mv². Kinetic energy is a scalar quantity, and it measures the work an object can do by virtue of its motion.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Everyday Kinetic Energy","body":"The kinetic energy of a fast flowing stream has long been used to grind corn, and sailing ships harness the kinetic energy of the wind — motion itself is a store of usable energy.","visual":"🏃"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A police officer fires a 50.0 g bullet at 200 m/s. What is its initial kinetic energy (in J)? [K = ½mv²]","answer":1000,"explain":"K = ½ × 0.05 kg × (200 m/s)² = 1000 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The bullet emerges from 2.00 cm of plywood with only 10% of its initial kinetic energy. What is its final kinetic energy (in J)?","answer":100,"explain":"Final KE = 0.1 × 1000 J = 100 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the emergent speed of the bullet (in m/s), given its final KE is 100 J and mass is 0.05 kg?","answer":63.2,"explain":"vf = √(2 × 100 J / 0.05 kg) = √4000 ≈ 63.2 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Even though the bullet loses 90% of its kinetic energy passing through the plywood, its speed is reduced by only about:","options":["68%","90%","10%","50%"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since KE depends on v², a 90% drop in KE corresponds to speed falling to about 32% of its original value — a reduction of roughly 68%, not 90%."}]},{"id":"wk4","title":"The Weary Traveller's Trunk","icon":"📈","kind":"lesson","story":"A woman must drag her trunk 20 m across a rough railway platform, but her strength fades along the way. Her changing push is your first real variable force — and the area under its graph holds the answer.","teach":[{"title":"Work by a Variable Force","body":"For a small displacement Δx, a varying force F(x) can be treated as roughly constant, so ΔW = F(x)Δx. Adding up all these small rectangles and shrinking Δx to zero gives the work done as the area under the F(x)-versus-x curve, i.e. W = ∫F(x)dx.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Reading a Force-Displacement Graph","body":"When the applied force changes with distance — for instance a tired worker's push weakening steadily — the work done is simply the total shaded area between the force curve and the displacement axis, treating area below the axis as negative.","visual":"🧍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A woman pushes a trunk with 100 N over the first 10 m, then her force falls linearly to 50 N by the 20 m mark. What is the work done by her force over the full 20 m (in J)? [rectangle 100×10 + trapezium ½(100+50)×10]","answer":1750,"explain":"WF = (100 × 10) + ½(100+50)(10) = 1000 + 750 = 1750 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Throughout the 20 m, a constant 50 N frictional force opposes her motion. What is the work done by friction (in J)?","answer":-1000,"explain":"Wf = (-50 N) × 20 m = -1000 J (negative, since friction opposes displacement)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the net work done on the trunk over the full 20 m?","options":["750 J","1750 J","-1000 J","2750 J"],"answer":0,"explain":"Net work = WF + Wf = 1750 J + (-1000 J) = 750 J."}]},{"id":"wk5","title":"The Rough Patch","icon":"🧮","kind":"lesson","story":"A sliding block enters a mysterious rough patch of floor where the retarding force grows stronger the closer it gets to the centre. Only the calculus version of the work-energy theorem can predict how far its energy will drain.","teach":[{"title":"WE Theorem for a Variable Force","body":"Starting from dK/dt = Fv = F(dx/dt), we get dK = Fdx. Integrating from the initial to final position gives Kf - Ki = ∫F dx = W, proving the work-energy theorem holds even for a varying force.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"What the WE Theorem Does (and Doesn't) Tell You","body":"The WE theorem is an integral, scalar form of Newton's second law. It doesn't carry explicit information about time (it's integrated over the path) or about direction (it's scalar, not vector).","visual":"⏱️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1 kg block moving at vi = 2 m/s enters a rough patch from x=0.1 m to x=2.01 m, where the retarding force is Fr = -k/x with k = 0.5 J. Using Kf = Ki - k·ln(2.01/0.1), find the final kinetic energy (in J). [ln(20.1) ≈ 3.0]","answer":0.5,"explain":"Kf = ½(1)(2²) - 0.5×ln(20.1) = 2 - 0.5(3.0) = 2 - 1.5 = 0.5 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Given the final kinetic energy of 0.5 J for the 1 kg block, what is its final speed vf (in m/s)?","answer":1,"explain":"vf = √(2Kf/m) = √(2×0.5/1) = √1 = 1 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The work-energy theorem is best described as:","options":["an integral, scalar form of Newton's second law","a vector law independent of Newton's laws","valid only for constant forces","a replacement for the law of conservation of momentum"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is derived directly from Newton's second law by integrating force over displacement, and it is a scalar relation."}]},{"id":"wk6","title":"Stored Power of the Fault Lines","icon":"🏹","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Atlas, a stretched bowstring hums with unreleased power, and cracks in the earth's crust sit like compressed springs waiting to snap. Both are guarding the concept of potential energy.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Potential Energy?","body":"Potential energy is 'stored' energy by virtue of the position or configuration of a body. A stretched bow-string or a compressed fault line holds potential energy that, when released, turns into kinetic energy.","visual":"🏹"},{"title":"Gravitational Potential Energy","body":"Near the earth's surface, gravitational potential energy as a function of height is V(h) = mgh. The gravitational force equals the negative derivative of V with respect to h: F = -dV/dh = -mg.","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"Conservative Forces","body":"Potential energy can only be defined for a conservative force, where F(x) = -dV/dx. The work done by such a force depends only on the initial and final positions, and is zero over any closed path.","visual":"🔗"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The gravitational potential energy of an object at height h near the earth's surface is:","options":["V(h) = mgh","V(h) = mgh²","V(h) = ½mgh","V(h) = mg/h"],"answer":0,"explain":"V(h) = mgh is the negative of the work done by gravity in raising the object to height h."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A force is called conservative if:","options":["the work it does depends only on the initial and final positions","it always increases an object's kinetic energy","it acts only on objects in free fall","it is always directed downward"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equivalently, the work done by a conservative force over any closed path is zero."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A ball is dropped from rest at height h = 5 m (g = 10 m/s²). Using v² = 2gh, what speed (in m/s) does it hit the ground with?","answer":10,"explain":"v² = 2 × 10 × 5 = 100, so v = 10 m/s."}]},{"id":"wk7","title":"The Vertical Circle","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"A bob on a light string is swung fast enough to loop a full vertical circle, its string slackening right at the top. Only the conservation of mechanical energy can tell you how fast it must start.","teach":[{"title":"Conservation of Mechanical Energy","body":"If only conservative forces act on a body, its total mechanical energy E = K + V is constant: Ki + V(xi) = Kf + V(xf). Individually K and V can change from point to point, but their sum stays fixed.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"A Ball Falling from Height H","body":"For a ball dropped from height H, EH = mgH at the top and E0 = ½mvf² at the ground. Equating them gives vf = √(2gH) — energy is purely potential at the top and purely kinetic at the bottom.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"The Pendulum's Vertical Loop","body":"For a bob swung with horizontal speed v0 at the bottom of a vertical circular loop of radius L, completing the loop with the string just going slack at the top requires v0² = 5gL, and the speed at the top is vC = √(gL).","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A bob on a string of length L = 2 m (g = 10 m/s²) is swung so it just completes a vertical circular loop (string slack at the top). Using v0² = 5gL, find v0² (in m²/s²).","answer":100,"explain":"v0² = 5 × 10 × 2 = 100 m²/s²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In this vertical-loop problem, what is the ratio of kinetic energies at the side point B to the top point C, KB/KC?","answer":3,"explain":"Working through the energies at B and C gives KB/KC = 3 : 1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the topmost point C of the vertical loop, the string just becomes slack because:","options":["the tension in the string becomes zero there","the bob's speed becomes zero there","gravity stops acting there","the string length changes there"],"answer":0,"explain":"At minimum speed for completing the loop, gravity alone supplies the centripetal force and tension TC = 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A ball is dropped from a cliff of height H = 20 m (g = 10 m/s²). Using EH = E0, what is its speed (in m/s) on reaching the ground?","answer":20,"explain":"vf = √(2gH) = √(2×10×20) = √400 = 20 m/s."}]},{"id":"wk8","title":"The Crash-Test Spring","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"Engineers at the Atlas garage fire a test car into a mounted spring to study crash safety. First on a smooth road, then on one with friction — you must predict exactly how far that spring will compress.","teach":[{"title":"Hooke's Law","body":"An ideal spring obeys Hooke's law: the spring force Fs = -kx, where x is the displacement from equilibrium and k is the spring constant (in N/m). A spring is 'stiff' if k is large and 'soft' if k is small.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Potential Energy of a Spring","body":"Taking the potential energy to be zero at the equilibrium position, the spring's potential energy for an extension or compression x is V(x) = ½kx². This can be checked: -dV/dx = -kx, the spring force.","visual":"🔩"},{"title":"Energy Conservation with a Spring","body":"When a moving mass compresses a spring on a frictionless surface, its kinetic energy converts entirely into the spring's potential energy at maximum compression: ½mv² = ½kxm².","visual":"🚗"},{"title":"Adding Friction","body":"When friction also acts, mechanical energy is no longer conserved, so the work-energy theorem must be used instead: ½mv² = ½kxm² + μmg·xm. The resulting compression is smaller than in the frictionless case.","visual":"🔥"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1000 kg car moving at 18.0 km/h (= 5 m/s) collides with a spring of k = 5.25×10³ N/m on a smooth road. What is the car's kinetic energy (in J)?","answer":12500,"explain":"K = ½ × 1000 × 5² = ½ × 1000 × 25 = 12500 J = 1.25×10⁴ J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At maximum compression, all 1.25×10⁴ J of kinetic energy becomes spring PE (k = 5.25×10³ N/m). What is the maximum compression xm (in m)?","answer":2,"explain":"½kxm² = 1.25×10⁴ J → xm = √(2×1.25×10⁴ / 5.25×10³) = 2.00 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Now redo the crash with a coefficient of friction μ = 0.5 (μmg = 0.5×1000×10 = 5×10³ N). Solving ½mv² = ½kxm² + μmg·xm gives what maximum compression (in m)?","answer":1.35,"explain":"Solving the resulting quadratic in xm gives xm = 1.35 m, less than the frictionless result of 2.00 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Compared to the frictionless case, the maximum compression of the spring when friction is present is:","options":["smaller, because some kinetic energy is lost to friction","larger, because friction adds energy to the spring","exactly the same","impossible to determine without more information"],"answer":0,"explain":"Friction dissipates part of the car's kinetic energy as heat, so less energy remains to compress the spring, giving 1.35 m instead of 2.00 m."}]},{"id":"wk9","title":"The Elevator Motor","icon":"🔌","kind":"lesson","story":"A packed elevator in the Atlas tower must rise steadily against friction, and the motor's rating is on the line. Time to calculate power — the rate at which work gets done.","teach":[{"title":"Defining Power","body":"Power is the time rate at which work is done or energy is transferred. Average power is Pav = W/t, while instantaneous power is the limit of average power as the time interval approaches zero: P = dW/dt.","visual":"🔌"},{"title":"Power in Terms of Force and Velocity","body":"Since dW = F.dr, instantaneous power can be written P = F.v, where v is the instantaneous velocity when the force is F.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Units of Power","body":"Power's SI unit is the watt (W), equal to 1 J/s, named after James Watt. Another common unit is horsepower: 1 hp = 746 W. Note kWh, seen on electricity bills, is a unit of energy, not power.","visual":"💡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An elevator of mass 1800 kg (with passengers) moves up at a constant 2 m/s against a friction force of 4000 N (g = 10 m/s²). What downward force must the motor overcome (in N)? [F = mg + friction]","answer":22000,"explain":"F = (1800 × 10) + 4000 = 18000 + 4000 = 22000 N."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using P = F.v with F = 22000 N and v = 2 m/s, what is the minimum power the motor must deliver (in W)?","answer":44000,"explain":"P = 22000 × 2 = 44000 W."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The SI unit of power is the:","options":["watt (1 J/s)","joule","newton","kilowatt-hour"],"answer":0,"explain":"The watt, named after James Watt, equals one joule per second; a kilowatt-hour is a unit of energy, not power."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many watts make up 1 horsepower?","answer":746,"explain":"1 hp = 746 W, still used to rate automobile and motorbike engines."}]},{"id":"wk10","title":"Billiards and the Neutron Moderator","icon":"🎱","kind":"lesson","story":"From a billiard table to the heart of a nuclear reactor, colliding bodies obey the same two laws: momentum is always conserved, and kinetic energy survives only if the collision is elastic.","teach":[{"title":"Momentum Is Always Conserved","body":"In any collision, the mutual impulsive forces obey Newton's third law (F12 = -F21), so the total linear momentum of the system is conserved — true whether the collision is elastic or not.","visual":"🎱"},{"title":"Elastic vs Inelastic","body":"In an elastic collision, kinetic energy is conserved after the collision. In a completely inelastic collision the bodies stick together and move with a common velocity; in general some kinetic energy is lost to heat and sound.","visual":"💥"},{"title":"Elastic Collisions in 1D","body":"For a 1D elastic collision of mass m1 (speed v1i) with stationary m2: v1f = [(m1-m2)/(m1+m2)]v1i and v2f = [2m1/(m1+m2)]v1i. If the masses are equal, the first mass stops and the second moves off with v1i.","visual":"🏓"},{"title":"Slowing Down Neutrons","body":"A fast neutron loses the most kinetic energy in an elastic collision with a light nucleus of similar mass (like deuterium or carbon) — this is exactly why reactors use a 'moderator' made of such light nuclei.","visual":"☢️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In any collision — elastic or inelastic — which quantity is always conserved?","options":["total linear momentum","total kinetic energy","total mechanical energy of each body separately","the velocity of each body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Momentum conservation follows from Newton's third law at every instant; kinetic energy is conserved only if the collision is elastic."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A fast neutron elastically collides head-on with a deuterium nucleus (mass ≈ 2× the neutron's mass). About what percentage of the neutron's kinetic energy is transferred to the deuterium?","answer":90,"explain":"f2 = 4m1m2/(m1+m2)² = 8/9 ≈ 89%, so 'almost 90%' of the neutron's energy is transferred (as the text states)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For an elastic head-on collision of a neutron with a carbon nucleus, the fraction of kinetic energy the neutron RETAINS is about:","options":["71.6%","8.3%","50%","28.4%"],"answer":0,"explain":"For carbon, f1 = 71.6% (retained by the neutron) and f2 = 28.4% (transferred to carbon)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two equal-mass billiard balls collide elastically; the cue ball deflects and the target ball sinks in a pocket at θ2 = 37° from the cue's original path. What angle θ1 (in degrees) does the cue ball take?","answer":53,"explain":"For equal masses in a glancing elastic collision, θ1 + θ2 = 90°, so θ1 = 90° - 37° = 53°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When two equal masses undergo a glancing elastic collision, with one initially at rest, after the collision the two masses move:","options":["at right angles to each other","parallel to each other","back along the same line","with the same speed as before, unchanged in direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is a general result for equal-mass elastic glancing collisions, confirmed by the 53°+37°=90° billiard example."}]},{"id":"wk-boss","title":"The Perpetual Motion Charlatan","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the Atlas's summit, a smooth-talking inventor claims his new machine creates energy from nothing, running forever without fuel. To unmask the fraud, you must wield everything you've learned about work, energy, power and collisions.","boss":{"name":"The Perpetual Motion Charlatan","taunt":"Behold! My machine needs no fuel, obeys no laws, and never stops! ...Unless, of course, YOU can prove otherwise."},"teach":[{"title":"Why He's Lying","body":"Every device studied this chapter — falling raindrops, sliding blocks, springs, elevators, colliding billiard balls — obeys the work-energy theorem and conservation of energy. Energy only ever converts between forms (or is lost to friction as heat); it is never created from nothing, so no machine can run forever without an energy input.","visual":"👑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The work-energy theorem states that the change in kinetic energy of a body equals:","options":["the work done on it by the net force","the total potential energy of the body","the power delivered to the body","the momentum transferred to the body"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kf - Ki = Wnet, the core statement of the work-energy theorem."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1.00 g raindrop falls 1.00 km (g = 10 m/s²). What is the work done by gravity on it (in J)?","answer":10,"explain":"Wg = mgh = 10⁻³ × 10 × 10³ = 10.0 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 50.0 g bullet fired at 200 m/s emerges from plywood with only 10% of its initial 1000 J kinetic energy. What is its emergent speed (in m/s)?","answer":63.2,"explain":"vf = √(2×100/0.05) ≈ 63.2 m/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A woman's push on a trunk does 1750 J of work over 20 m, while friction does -1000 J. What is the net work done on the trunk (in J)?","answer":750,"explain":"Net work = 1750 + (-1000) = 750 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1000 kg car at 5 m/s (KE = 1.25×10⁴ J) compresses a spring of k = 5.25×10³ N/m on a frictionless road. What is the maximum compression (in m)?","answer":2,"explain":"½kxm² = 1.25×10⁴ J gives xm = 2.00 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An 1800 kg elevator rises at 2 m/s against 4000 N of friction (g = 10 m/s²). What minimum power (in W) must its motor deliver?","answer":44000,"explain":"F = mg + friction = 22000 N; P = Fv = 22000 × 2 = 44000 W."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two equal-mass billiard balls collide elastically, one initially at rest. If the target ball goes off at 37°, the cue ball goes off at:","options":["53°","37°","90°","0°"],"answer":0,"explain":"For equal masses in a glancing elastic collision, the two final paths are at right angles: 53° + 37° = 90°."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"So could the Charlatan's machine really run forever with no energy input?","options":["No — that would violate conservation of energy, proven in every example this chapter","Yes, if the machine is built from a strong enough spring","Yes, as long as no collisions occur inside it","No, but only because friction is never truly zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every case studied — gravity, springs, collisions — conserves total energy (or loses it to friction/heat); none ever creates energy from nothing."}]}]},{"id":"systemsparticles11","index":5,"name":"Systems of Particles and Rotational Motion","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 6","emoji":"🌀","palette":{"from":"#a78bfa","to":"#4c1d95"},"lessons":[{"id":"sy1","title":"The Point That Speaks for the Whole","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"You've been handed a lopsided asteroid fragment and told to predict where it will drift. Tracking every atom is impossible — you need the one point that moves as if it carried all the mass.","teach":[{"title":"Centre of mass of two particles","body":"For two particles of masses m1 and m2 at positions x1 and x2 on a line, the centre of mass is the mass-weighted mean X = (m1x1 + m2x2)/(m1 + m2). If the masses are equal, the centre of mass lies exactly midway between them.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Centre of mass of n particles","body":"For n particles along a line, X = (Σ mi xi)/M, where M = Σ mi is the total mass. In three dimensions this generalises to coordinates (X, Y, Z), or compactly as a vector R = (Σ mi ri)/M.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Continuous bodies","body":"A rigid body is treated as a continuous distribution of mass, so the sums become integrals: X = (1/M)∫x dm, and similarly for Y and Z. By symmetry, homogeneous rings, discs, spheres and rods have their centre of mass at their geometric centre.","visual":"∫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two particles of equal mass m are placed on the x-axis. Where does their centre of mass lie?","options":["Exactly midway between them","At the position of the heavier one","At the origin always","Outside the line joining them"],"answer":0,"explain":"For equal masses, X = (mx1+mx2)/2m = (x1+x2)/2, the midpoint."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.1: Three particles of masses 100 g, 150 g and 200 g sit at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of side 0.5 m, at coordinates O(0,0), A(0.5,0) and B(0.25, 0.25√3). What is the X-coordinate of the centre of mass, in metres (to 3 decimal places)?","answer":0.278,"explain":"X = [100(0)+150(0.5)+200(0.25)]/450 = 125/450 ≈ 0.278 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A uniform L-shaped lamina of mass 3 kg is built from three 1 kg squares with centres of mass at (1/2,1/2), (3/2,1/2) and (1/2,3/2). What is the Y-coordinate of the centre of mass?","options":["5/6 m","1/2 m","3/2 m","1 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"Y = [1(1/2)+1(1/2)+1(3/2)]/3 = (5/2)/3 = 5/6 m, by symmetry with X."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a homogeneous thin rod, why does the centre of mass coincide with its geometric centre?","options":["Reflection symmetry: every mass element dm at +x is matched by an equal dm at −x, so ∫x dm = 0 about the centre","Because the rod has no width","Because mass is concentrated at the ends","Because gravity always acts at the midpoint"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text derives this directly from the reflection-symmetry argument for ∫x dm = 0."}]},{"id":"sy2","title":"The Explosion That Doesn't Change Course","icon":"💥","kind":"lesson","story":"A signal flare explodes mid-arc into glowing fragments. Chaos everywhere — yet you know exactly where the 'average' of all those fragments will land. Prove it.","teach":[{"title":"Velocity and acceleration of the CM","body":"Differentiating MR = Σ mi ri with respect to time gives MV = Σ mi vi, and differentiating again gives MA = Σ mi ai = F1 + F2 + ... + Fn, the vector sum of all forces on all particles.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"Internal forces cancel","body":"By Newton's third law, internal forces between particles occur in equal and opposite pairs, so their contribution to the total force sums to zero. Only external forces survive: M A = F_ext.","visual":"⇄"},{"title":"The centre of mass moves like a free particle","body":"The centre of mass of a system moves as if all the mass were concentrated there and all external forces acted there. This is why a projectile's fragments, after an internal explosion, have a centre of mass that keeps following the same parabolic path as gravity alone dictates.","visual":"🚀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A projectile explodes in mid-air due to internal forces. What happens to the path of the centre of mass of the fragments?","options":["It continues along the same parabolic trajectory as before the explosion","It stops instantly","It flies off in a straight line tangent to the old path","It falls straight down"],"answer":0,"explain":"The explosion forces are internal and contribute nothing to the total external force, which is still just gravity, so the CM keeps its original parabolic path."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To determine the motion of the centre of mass of a system of particles, what do you need to know?","options":["Only the external forces on the system","Only the internal forces","Both internal and external forces equally","Nothing — it's always at rest"],"answer":0,"explain":"Eq. 6.11, M A = F_ext, shows only external forces matter for the CM's motion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A radium nucleus at rest decays into a radon nucleus and an alpha particle, with no external forces acting. How do the two product particles move relative to each other, viewed from the centre-of-mass frame?","options":["Back to back, in opposite directions, with the centre of mass remaining at rest","In the same direction at the same speed","The alpha particle stays at rest","Randomly with no fixed relation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since total momentum is conserved and was zero, the two fragments must move back to back so that momentum stays zero and the CM stays at rest."}]},{"id":"sy3","title":"The Law That Never Breaks","icon":"🔙","kind":"lesson","story":"Two spacecraft drift apart after an internal thruster burn. No outside force touches them. You need to know: does their combined momentum change at all?","teach":[{"title":"Total linear momentum","body":"The linear momentum of a system of n particles is the vector sum P = p1 + p2 + ... + pn = m1v1 + m2v2 + ... + mnvn. Comparing with the centre-of-mass velocity, P = MV: the total momentum equals the total mass times the velocity of the centre of mass.","visual":"➡️"},{"title":"Newton's second law for a system","body":"Differentiating P = MV and comparing with M A = F_ext gives dP/dt = F_ext — Newton's second law extended to a system of particles.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Conservation of linear momentum","body":"If the total external force on a system is zero, dP/dt = 0, so P is constant: the total linear momentum is conserved, and equivalently the centre of mass moves with constant velocity, like a free particle.","visual":"🔒"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does Eq. P = MV tell you about a system of particles?","options":["The total momentum equals the total mass times the velocity of the centre of mass","Momentum is only defined for single particles","P is always zero for a system","M is the momentum divided by acceleration"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is exactly Eq. (6.15) derived by comparing the momentum sum with the centre-of-mass velocity expression."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the total external force acting on a system of particles is zero, what is conserved?","options":["The total linear momentum of the system","The kinetic energy of each particle","The position of every particle","The internal forces"],"answer":0,"explain":"dP/dt = F_ext = 0 implies P = constant — the law of conservation of linear momentum for a system."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A child stands at one end of a long trolley moving uniformly with speed V on a frictionless floor. The child then runs about on the trolley in any manner. What happens to the speed of the centre of mass of the (trolley + child) system?","options":["It remains V, unchanged","It increases","It decreases to zero","It becomes unpredictable"],"answer":0,"explain":"The child's motion involves only internal forces on the trolley+child system; with no external horizontal force, the CM velocity stays at V."}]},{"id":"sy4","title":"The Product That Points Sideways","icon":"➕","kind":"lesson","story":"You need a tool that captures not just 'how much' but 'which way it twists.' A simple multiplication won't do — you need a product that itself points perpendicular to both inputs.","teach":[{"title":"Defining the vector product","body":"The vector product of a and b is a vector c = a × b with magnitude ab sinθ (θ the angle between them), directed perpendicular to the plane of a and b, given by the right-hand screw rule: curl the fingers from a to b, and the thumb points along c.","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"Key properties","body":"The vector product is not commutative: a × b = −(b × a). It does not change sign under reflection (unlike a and b individually), and it is distributive over vector addition: a × (b + c) = a×b + a×c. Also a × a = 0.","visual":"⚠️"},{"title":"Component form","body":"Using î×î=ĵ×ĵ=k̂×k̂=0 and î×ĵ=k̂, ĵ×k̂=î, k̂×î=ĵ, the cross product can be written via a determinant with rows (î,ĵ,k̂), (ax,ay,az), (bx,by,bz). Moment of force and angular momentum are both defined as vector products.","visual":"🔢"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does a × b relate to b × a?","options":["a × b = −(b × a)","a × b = b × a","a × b = 0 always","They have different magnitudes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vector product is anti-commutative: the two have equal magnitude but opposite direction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.4: For a = 3î−4ĵ+5k̂ and b = −2î+ĵ+3k̂, what is the coefficient of î (i-component) in a × b, computed as (ay bz − az by)?","answer":-17,"explain":"i-component = ay bz − az by = (−4)(3) − (5)(1) = −12−5 = −17 (the source's worked value for a×b's i-component)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the magnitude of î × ĵ?","options":["1, since sin 90° = 1 and both are unit vectors","0","√2","Undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"î and ĵ are unit vectors perpendicular to each other, so |î×ĵ| = (1)(1)sin90° = 1, and î×ĵ = k̂."}]},{"id":"sy5","title":"Every Point, One Spin","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"A potter's wheel spins beneath your hands. The rim races past while the centre barely moves — yet every point on the wheel shares one single number that describes the spin. Find it.","teach":[{"title":"Angular velocity","body":"As a rigid body rotates about a fixed axis, every particle moves in a circle. The instantaneous angular velocity ω = dθ/dt is the same for every particle of the body, so we call it the angular velocity of the whole body. Linear speed relates to it by v = ωr, where r is the perpendicular distance from the axis.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Angular velocity as a vector","body":"ω is actually a vector, lying along the fixed axis of rotation, pointing in the direction a right-handed screw would advance if turned with the body. Its magnitude is dθ/dt.","visual":"🧭"},{"title":"v = ω × r","body":"The linear velocity of a particle at position r (measured from an origin on the axis) is v = ω × r. Since ω×OC = 0 (OC lies along ω), only the perpendicular component CP matters, giving a vector of magnitude ωr⊥ directed tangent to the circle — exactly matching the particle's actual velocity.","visual":"→"},{"title":"Angular acceleration","body":"Angular acceleration α = dω/dt is the rotational analogue of linear acceleration. For a fixed axis, both ω and α keep a fixed direction, so the vector equation reduces to the scalar α = dω/dt.","visual":"📈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is angular velocity ω said to belong to the 'whole body' rather than to one particle?","options":["Because every particle of a rigid body rotating about a fixed axis has the same ω, even though their linear speeds v = ωr differ","Because all particles have the same linear velocity","Because ω is measured only at the axis","Because ω is zero for rigid bodies"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states v=ωr applies to all particles with the same ω, so ω characterises the whole body's rotation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the direction of the linear velocity vector v = ω × r for a particle in fixed-axis rotation?","options":["Perpendicular to both ω and r, tangent to the circle described by the particle","Along the axis of rotation","Along r, pointing outward","Opposite to ω"],"answer":0,"explain":"As a cross product, v=ω×r is perpendicular to both ω and r, i.e. tangential to the particle's circular path, matching the known direction of v."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A particle lies exactly on the fixed axis of rotation. What is its linear velocity?","options":["Zero, since its perpendicular distance r from the axis is zero","Equal to ω","Maximum, since it is closest to the axis","Undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"v = ωr, and for a particle on the axis r = 0, so v = 0 — it stays stationary while the body rotates."}]},{"id":"sy6","title":"Push Here, Not There","icon":"🚪","kind":"lesson","story":"You're trying to force open a jammed door. Push near the hinge and nothing happens; push at the outer edge and it swings wide. The door is teaching you about torque.","teach":[{"title":"Moment of force (torque)","body":"Torque is the rotational analogue of force. For a force F acting at a point with position vector r from the origin, the torque is τ = r × F, with magnitude τ = rF sinθ. It vanishes if r=0, F=0, or if the line of action of F passes through the origin (θ = 0° or 180°).","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Why placement matters","body":"τ = r⊥F, where r⊥ = r sinθ is the perpendicular distance of the line of action of F from the origin — called the moment arm. A force applied right at the hinge (r⊥ = 0) produces no rotation at all, however large; a force at right angles to the door's outer edge is most effective.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Units and dimensions","body":"Torque has dimensions ML²T⁻², the same as work or energy, but it is a vector while work is a scalar. Its SI unit is the newton metre (N m).","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.5: Find the k̂-component of the torque τ = r × F about the origin, for r = î−ĵ+k̂ and F = 7î+3ĵ−5k̂, using the determinant expansion (component = rx*Fy − ry*Fx).","answer":10,"explain":"k̂-component = rx Fy − ry Fx = (1)(3) − (−1)(7) = 3+7 = 10."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A force is applied exactly at the hinge line of a door (r = 0 relative to the hinge). What torque does it produce about the hinge, no matter how large the force?","options":["Zero","Maximum possible","Equal to F","Depends on F's direction only"],"answer":0,"explain":"τ = r×F, and with r=0 the torque is zero regardless of the force's magnitude."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the SI units of torque, and why do they match those of work even though the two quantities are physically different?","options":["Newton metre (N m); torque is a vector (r×F) while work is a scalar (F·d), but both have dimensions ML²T⁻²","Watt; torque is power","Joule per second; torque is a rate","Newton; torque is just a force"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text explicitly notes torque has the same dimensions as work/energy (ML²T⁻²) and the same SI unit N m, despite being a vector."}]},{"id":"sy7","title":"The Spin That Won't Quit","icon":"🔭","kind":"lesson","story":"A skater pulls her arms in mid-spin and suddenly whirls faster, with no one pushing her. Somewhere in that motion is a conserved quantity as fundamental to rotation as momentum is to a straight line.","teach":[{"title":"Angular momentum of a particle","body":"The angular momentum of a particle of momentum p at position r from the origin is l = r × p, with magnitude l = rp sinθ = r⊥p. It is zero if p=0, r=0, or the line of p passes through the origin.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Torque is the rate of change of angular momentum","body":"Differentiating l = r×p with respect to time, using dp/dt = F and the fact that v×v = 0, gives dl/dt = τ, the rotational analogue of F = dp/dt.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"System of particles: torque and angular momentum","body":"For a system, L = Σ li and τ = Σ τi. Assuming Newton's third law and that internal forces act along the line joining particles, the internal torques cancel (τ_int = 0), so dL/dt = τ_ext.","visual":"👥"},{"title":"Conservation of angular momentum","body":"If the total external torque on a system is zero, dL/dt = 0, so L is constant — the law of conservation of angular momentum, the rotational analogue of conservation of linear momentum.","visual":"🔒"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the rotational analogue of F = dp/dt for a single particle?","options":["τ = dl/dt","F = dl/dt","τ = dp/dt","l = dτ/dt"],"answer":0,"explain":"Differentiating l=r×p with the product rule (and v×v=0) gives dl/dt = r×F = τ."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do internal forces contribute zero net torque to a system of particles?","options":["Because Newton's third law makes internal forces equal and opposite and directed along the line joining the particles, so each action-reaction pair produces zero net torque","Because internal forces are always zero","Because internal forces only affect linear momentum, not angular momentum","Because internal torques cancel only for rigid bodies"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text derives τ_int = 0 from Newton's third law plus the assumption that internal forces act along the line joining each pair of particles."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Example 6.6: A single particle moves with constant velocity v past an arbitrary fixed point O. What happens to its angular momentum about O over time?","options":["It remains constant in both magnitude and direction, since the perpendicular distance from O to the line of v never changes","It increases steadily","It oscillates","It becomes zero after passing closest to O"],"answer":0,"explain":"l = mvr sinθ = mv·(OM), and OM (the perpendicular distance from O to the velocity line) stays fixed since the direction of v never changes; there's also no external torque on a free particle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the total external torque on a system of particles is zero, what can you conclude?","options":["The total angular momentum L of the system is conserved (constant)","The total linear momentum must also be zero","Every particle stops rotating","The moment of inertia becomes zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"dL/dt = τ_ext = 0 implies L = constant, the law of conservation of angular momentum."}]},{"id":"sy8","title":"Balanced on a Knife's Edge","icon":"🤷","kind":"lesson","story":"A loaded metal bar rests on two knife-edges, and a ladder leans against a frictionless wall. Nothing moves. Your job: find the hidden forces that hold it all in perfect balance.","teach":[{"title":"Conditions for mechanical equilibrium","body":"A rigid body is in mechanical equilibrium if both its linear and angular momentum are unchanging: the total force ΣFi = 0 (translational equilibrium) AND the total torque Στi = 0 (rotational equilibrium). A body can satisfy one without the other — a couple has zero net force but nonzero net torque.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Principle of moments (the lever)","body":"For a lever in equilibrium with load F1 at distance d1 (load arm) and effort F2 at distance d2 (effort arm) from the fulcrum, d1F1 = d2F2, i.e. load arm × load = effort arm × effort. The mechanical advantage is M.A. = F1/F2 = d2/d1.","visual":"🏖️"},{"title":"Centre of gravity","body":"The centre of gravity (CG) is the point where the total gravitational torque on the body is zero: Σ(ri × mi g) = 0. In uniform gravity, the CG coincides with the centre of mass, since g factors out of the sum, leaving Σ mi ri = 0 — the defining condition of the centre of mass.","visual":"📍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.8: A 70 cm uniform metal bar of mass 4.00 kg rests on knife-edges 10 cm from each end (so K1G = K2G = 25 cm). A 6.00 kg load hangs 5 cm from G (on the K1 side, PG = 5 cm). Using −R1(25)+W1(5)+R2(25)=0 in cm and R1+R2 = 10.00g, with g=9.8, find R1 in newtons (to 2 decimal places).","answer":54.88,"explain":"From the source: R1 = 54.88 N and R2 = 43.12 N, solving the translational and rotational equilibrium equations together."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.9: A 3 m ladder of weight W=196.0 N leans against a frictionless wall, its foot 1 m from the wall (so BC = 2√2 m). Using the rotational equilibrium equation 2√2 F1 − (1/2)W = 0, find F1 in newtons (to 1 decimal place).","answer":34.6,"explain":"F1 = W/(4√2) = 196.0/(4×1.414) ≈ 34.6 N, matching the source's worked answer."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the principle of moments for a lever, what does d1F1 = d2F2 express?","options":["Load arm × load = effort arm × effort","Load = effort always","The fulcrum reaction equals the load","Force is independent of distance from the fulcrum"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is Eq. (6.32a), the principle of moments, derived from the rotational equilibrium condition taken about the fulcrum."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Under what condition does the centre of gravity of a body coincide with its centre of mass?","options":["When the gravitational field g does not vary from one part of the body to another (uniform gravity)","Always, for any body","Only for spherical bodies","Only when the body is in free fall"],"answer":0,"explain":"The source notes g cancels out of the torque sum only when it's the same at every particle; if g varies across an extended body, CG and CM differ."}]},{"id":"sy9","title":"Rotational Mass","icon":"🪗","kind":"lesson","story":"A flywheel resists speeding up or slowing down, smoothing out every jerk in an engine's power. It isn't heavier than a plain disc of the same mass — its mass is just arranged differently. That arrangement has a name.","teach":[{"title":"Defining moment of inertia","body":"For a body rotating about a fixed axis with angular velocity ω, each particle has kinetic energy ½mivi² = ½miri²ω². Summing over all particles and factoring out ω² gives K = ½Iω², where the moment of inertia I = Σ miri² (ri is each particle's perpendicular distance from the axis).","visual":"📊"},{"title":"I is the rotational analogue of mass","body":"Comparing K=½Iω² with K=½mv² for translation, moment of inertia plays the role mass plays in linear motion — it measures resistance to a change in rotational motion. Unlike mass, I depends on how mass is distributed relative to the chosen axis, not just how much mass there is.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Standard results","body":"For a thin ring of mass M, radius R, about its central axis: I = MR². For a rod of length l with two point masses M/2 at each end rotating about the centre: I = Ml²/4. Table 6.1 gives more: thin ring about diameter I=MR²/2; rod about perpendicular axis at midpoint I=ML²/12; disc about central axis I=MR²/2; disc about diameter I=MR²/4; hollow cylinder about its axis I=MR²; solid cylinder about its axis I=MR²/2; solid sphere about a diameter I=2MR²/5.","visual":"📋"},{"title":"Radius of gyration","body":"Since I = Mk² for every regular body, k (with dimensions of length) is called the radius of gyration — the distance from the axis at which a point mass equal to the body's total mass would have the same moment of inertia as the body.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the moment of inertia of a thin ring of mass M and radius R about an axis through its centre, perpendicular to its plane?","options":["MR²","MR²/2","MR²/4","2MR²/5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every particle of the ring is at distance R from the axis, so I = Σmiri² = MR² (Table 6.1, row 1)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the moment of inertia of a solid sphere of mass M and radius R about a diameter?","options":["2MR²/5","MR²","MR²/2","MR²/12"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 6.1 gives I = 2MR²/5 for a solid sphere about its diameter."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A rigid massless rod of length l = 2 m has two point masses of M/2 each at its ends, rotating about a perpendicular axis through the centre (M total = 8 kg). Using I = Ml²/4, what is I in kg·m²?","answer":8,"explain":"I = Ml²/4 = 8×4/4 = 8 kg·m²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a flywheel with a large moment of inertia help an engine run smoothly?","options":["Its large I resists sudden increases or decreases in rotational speed, smoothing out jerky changes","It has less mass than other wheels","It converts rotational energy to heat instantly","It has zero angular momentum"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text explains a flywheel's large moment of inertia resists sudden speed changes, giving a smooth, gradual change in speed."}]},{"id":"sy10","title":"Torque Sets the Pace","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"A flywheel sits still until a cord, pulled steadily, sets it spinning. You need to predict exactly how fast it will spin, how much work the pull does, and how that work becomes motion.","teach":[{"title":"Rotational kinematics equations","body":"For uniform angular acceleration α: ω = ω₀ + αt, θ = θ₀ + ω₀t + ½αt², and ω² = ω₀² + 2α(θ−θ₀) — exact analogues of the linear kinematics equations with x→θ, v→ω, a→α.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Work and power in rotation","body":"The work done by a torque τ through angular displacement dθ is dW = τ dθ (the rotational analogue of dW = F dx). Dividing by dt gives instantaneous power P = τω (analogous to P = Fv).","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Newton's second law for rotation","body":"Equating the rate of work done, τω, to the rate of increase of kinetic energy d(½Iω²)/dt = Iωα gives τ = Iα — the rotational analogue of F = ma. Torque produces angular acceleration in proportion to itself and inversely to the moment of inertia.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.11: A motor wheel's angular speed increases from 1200 rpm to 3120 rpm in 16 s, with uniform angular acceleration. In rad/s², using α=(ω−ω₀)/t with ω₀=40π rad/s and ω=104π rad/s, what is α (as a multiple of π, i.e. just the coefficient)?","answer":4,"explain":"α = (104π−40π)/16 = 64π/16 = 4π rad/s², so the coefficient is 4."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.12: A cord wound round a flywheel of mass 20 kg and radius 0.20 m is pulled with a steady force of 25 N. Torque τ = F×R. What is τ in N·m?","answer":5.0,"explain":"τ = 25 N × 0.20 m = 5.0 N·m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Continuing Example 6.12: I = MR²/2 = 20×0.2²/2 = 0.4 kg·m², and τ = 5.0 N·m. Using τ = Iα, what is the angular acceleration α in s⁻²?","answer":12.5,"explain":"α = τ/I = 5.0/0.4 = 12.5 s⁻², matching the source's worked answer."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Continuing Example 6.12: when 2 m of cord unwinds, the work done by the 25 N pull is W = F × distance. What is W in joules?","answer":50,"explain":"W = 25 N × 2 m = 50 J, and the source confirms this equals the kinetic energy gained (no friction loss)."}]},{"id":"sy11","title":"Arms In, Spin Faster","icon":"🤸","kind":"lesson","story":"A skater on ice pulls her arms close and instantly spins faster, with no push from anywhere. The rink is quiet, no external torque acts on her — yet her body finds a way to speed up. This is the chapter's final trick before the boss chamber.","teach":[{"title":"Angular momentum about a fixed axis","body":"For a rigid body symmetric about its fixed rotation axis, the angular momentum component along the axis is Lz = Iω (a vector along k̂), and for such symmetric bodies the total L equals Lz. For asymmetric bodies, L does not lie exactly along the axis.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"dL/dt = τ for fixed-axis rotation","body":"Differentiating Lz = Iω and using dL/dt = τ (with only the axial component of torque relevant) gives d(Iω)/dt = τ, which reduces to τ = Iα when I is constant — consistent with the work-energy derivation.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Conservation of angular momentum: Iw = constant","body":"If the external torque about the fixed axis is zero, Lz = Iω = constant. This is why a skater or a child on a swivel chair spins faster when pulling their arms in (decreasing I) and slower when stretching out (increasing I) — Iω stays fixed.","visual":"⛸️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A child on a frictionless swivel chair spins with arms outstretched, then pulls the arms in. What happens to the angular speed?","options":["It increases, since I decreases while Iω stays constant (zero external torque)","It decreases","It stays exactly the same","It becomes zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"With no external torque about the rotation axis, Iw is conserved; pulling arms in reduces I, so w must increase."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a rigid body symmetric about its fixed axis of rotation, how does the total angular momentum L relate to its axial component Lz?","options":["L = Lz; the perpendicular component L⊥ is zero for such symmetric bodies","L is always zero","L is always perpendicular to Lz","L and Lz are unrelated"],"answer":0,"explain":"For symmetric bodies, diametrically opposite particles' perpendicular contributions cancel, leaving L⊥=0, so L=Lz=Iω."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the condition for Lz = Iω to remain constant over time?","options":["The external torque about the fixed axis must be zero","The moment of inertia must be zero","The angular velocity must be zero","The body must not be rotating"],"answer":0,"explain":"From d(Iω)/dt = τ, if τ=0 then Iω is constant — the conservation law for fixed-axis rotation."}]},{"id":"sy-boss","title":"The Keeper of Spin and Balance","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the centre of the rotating chamber floats the Keeper of Spin and Balance — part flywheel, part lever, part skater mid-pirouette. It will only let you pass if you can master centres of mass, torques, and moments of inertia all at once.","boss":{"name":"The Keeper of Spin and Balance","taunt":"Every particle in me spins with the same ω — can your mind keep up with my moment of inertia?"},"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 6.1 recap: Masses 100 g, 150 g, 200 g sit at O(0,0), A(0.5,0), B(0.25,0.25√3) m. The Y-coordinate of the centre of mass works out to 200(0.25√3)/450. Using √3≈1.732, what is Y in metres (2 decimal places)?","answer":0.19,"explain":"Y = 200(0.4330)/450 ≈ 86.6/450 ≈ 0.192 ≈ 0.19 m, matching the source's 1/(3√3) ≈ 0.19 m result."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When the sum of external forces on a rigid body is zero but the sum of external torques is not, what state is the body in?","options":["Rotational equilibrium is violated while translational equilibrium holds (a pure couple)","Both translational and rotational equilibrium hold","Neither equilibrium holds","The body cannot move at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"A couple has zero net force but nonzero net torque — translational equilibrium without rotational equilibrium."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the moment of inertia formula for a solid cylinder of mass M and radius R about its own axis?","options":["MR²/2","MR²","MR²/4","2MR²/5"],"answer":0,"explain":"Table 6.1 gives I = MR²/2 for a solid cylinder about its axis (same as a disc about its central axis)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Exercise 6.11: A solid cylinder of mass 20 kg and radius 0.25 m rotates about its axis with ω = 100 rad/s. Using I = MR²/2, what is I in kg·m²?","answer":0.625,"explain":"I = (20)(0.25)²/2 = 20×0.0625/2 = 0.625 kg·m²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Continuing Exercise 6.11: with I = 0.625 kg·m² and ω = 100 rad/s, what is the rotational kinetic energy K = ½Iω², in joules?","answer":3125,"explain":"K = 0.5 × 0.625 × 100² = 0.5×0.625×10000 = 3125 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two forces of equal magnitude but opposite direction act perpendicular to a light rod, one at each end, both tending to rotate it the same way (a couple). What is true of this system?","options":["Total force is zero (translational equilibrium) but total torque is nonzero (not in rotational equilibrium)","Total force and total torque are both zero","Total force is nonzero but total torque is zero","The rod cannot experience any rotation"],"answer":0,"explain":"As described for Fig. 6.20(b) in the source, this is a couple: translational equilibrium holds, rotational equilibrium does not."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Torques of equal magnitude are applied to a hollow cylinder and a solid sphere of the same mass and radius, each free to rotate about its symmetry axis. Which acquires the greater angular speed after a given time?","options":["The solid sphere, since I_sphere = 2MR²/5 is smaller than I_cylinder = MR², giving larger α = τ/I for the same τ","The hollow cylinder, since it has more inertia","Both reach the same angular speed","Neither will rotate"],"answer":0,"explain":"With the same τ, smaller I means larger α=τ/I; the solid sphere's I (2MR²/5) is less than the hollow cylinder's I (MR²), so it speeds up faster."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A metre stick balances on a knife edge at its 50 cm mark. Two 5 g coins stacked at the 12.0 cm mark shift the balance point to 45.0 cm. What principle do you use to find the stick's mass?","options":["Rotational equilibrium (principle of moments): the torque from the coins about the new balance point equals the torque from the stick's own weight about that point","Conservation of linear momentum","Only translational equilibrium, ignoring torques","The centre of mass must be at 12.0 cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is Exercise 6.16: you balance moments about the new pivot (45.0 cm) between the coins' weight and the stick's weight acting at its original centre (50 cm mark)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Final check — Example 6.12 finale: the flywheel (I=0.4 kg·m²) starts from rest and gains kinetic energy equal to the 50 J of work done by the pull (no friction). Using K=½Iω²=50, what is ω² in (rad/s)²?","answer":250,"explain":"ω² = 2K/I = 100/0.4 = 250 (rad/s)², matching the source's Example 6.12(c) result."}]}]},{"id":"gravitation11","index":6,"name":"Gravitation","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 7","emoji":"🪐","palette":{"from":"#818cf8","to":"#312e81"},"lessons":[{"id":"gr1","title":"Tycho's Ledger, Kepler's Laws","icon":"🔭","kind":"lesson","story":"A dusty ledger of decades of naked-eye planet sightings lands in your hands - the life's work of a nobleman who never trusted the old circular-orbit dogma. Before the Church's censors burn it, you must find the hidden pattern that Kepler once found: the true shape of the heavens.","teach":[{"title":"Law of Orbits","body":"All planets move in elliptical orbits with the Sun at one focus of the ellipse, not at the centre of a circle as the old Copernican model assumed. A circle is just the special case where both foci merge into one.","visual":"🥚"},{"title":"Law of Areas","body":"The line joining a planet to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal intervals of time. Because the swept area stays constant while the distance to the Sun changes, a planet moves faster when it is closer to the Sun and slower when it is farther away.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"Law of Periods","body":"The square of a planet's orbital period is proportional to the cube of its semi-major axis: T² ∝ a³, with the very same constant of proportionality for every planet going around the Sun.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Kepler's first law, the Sun sits at...","options":["one focus of a planet's elliptical orbit","the exact centre of a circular orbit","the midpoint between two planets","the aphelion of every orbit"],"answer":0,"explain":"The law of orbits places the Sun at one focus of the ellipse, a deviation from the purely circular Copernican model."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By Kepler's law of areas, a planet moves ___ when it is nearer the Sun (perihelion).","options":["faster","slower","at constant speed","not at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Equal areas in equal times means the planet must cover more orbital arc, i.e. move faster, when closer to the Sun."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Kepler's law of periods says that for planets orbiting the Sun...","options":["T²/a³ is the same constant for every planet","T is the same for every planet","a is the same for every planet","T² is proportional to a, not a³"],"answer":0,"explain":"T² = k a³ with the same k for all planets around the Sun is exactly Kepler's third law."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Kepler derived his three laws by analysing decades of naked-eye observations recorded by...","options":["Tycho Brahe","Galileo Galilei","Nicolaus Copernicus","Henry Cavendish"],"answer":0,"explain":"Tycho Brahe compiled the observational data; his assistant Johannes Kepler extracted the three laws from it."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Earth's orbit is nearly circular: its semi-minor to semi-major axis ratio is b/a = 0.99986. By what percentage is b smaller than a? (2 decimal places)","answer":0.014,"explain":"(1 - 0.99986) x 100 = 0.014%, showing Earth's orbit is very close to a circle."}]},{"id":"gr2","title":"The Force Behind the Ellipse","icon":"🍎","kind":"lesson","story":"Patterns are not proof - you need the force law that produces them. Channel Newton's leap from Kepler's geometry to a universal law of attraction, then put it to the test on Mars's two tiny moons, Phobos and Deimos, to weigh a planet you have never touched.","teach":[{"title":"Newton's Universal Law of Gravitation","body":"Any two point masses attract each other with a force F = Gm₁m₂/r², directed along the line joining them. The force falls off with the square of the separation and acts between every pair of masses in the universe.","visual":"🧲"},{"title":"Kepler's Third Law from Newton's Law","body":"Setting the gravitational force equal to the centripetal force needed for a circular orbit reproduces Kepler's third law, T² = kR³, where k = 4π²/(GM) depends only on the mass M of the body being orbited.","visual":"🪐"},{"title":"Superposition Principle","body":"When several masses act on one point mass, the net gravitational force is simply the vector sum of the individual forces from each mass - exactly the principle used to add up forces from an extended body.","visual":"➕"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newton's law of gravitation says the force between two point masses is proportional to...","options":["the product of the masses, and inversely proportional to the square of the distance","the sum of the masses","the distance between them","the square of the product of the masses"],"answer":0,"explain":"F = Gm₁m₂/r²: proportional to the product of the masses, inversely proportional to r²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two 1 kg masses are placed 1 m apart. Using F = Gm₁m₂/r² with G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg², what is the force between them, in units of 10⁻¹¹ N?","answer":6.67,"explain":"F = (6.67x10⁻¹¹)(1)(1)/1² = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Phobos orbits Mars with a period of 7 h 39 min and an orbital radius of 9.4x10³ km. Using T² = 4π²R³/(GM), NCERT finds Mars's mass to be 6.48x10²³ kg. Give this mass in units of 10²³ kg.","answer":6.48,"explain":"Solving M = 4π²R³/(GT²) with the given Phobos data gives the mass of Mars, 6.48x10²³ kg."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Mars's orbital radius around the Sun is 1.52 times Earth's. Using Kepler's third law, T_Mars = (1.52)^1.5 x 365 days. What is the length of the Martian year in days (nearest whole number)?","answer":684,"explain":"(1.52)^1.5 x 365 ≈ 684 days, the length of the Martian year."}]},{"id":"gr3","title":"The Hollow World, The Silent Scale","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep inside a hollow asteroid shell, you feel nothing pulling you toward its walls - is gravity broken, or is something more elegant going on? Above ground, an eccentric nobleman's torsion balance waits, twisting ever so slightly under the pull of lead spheres, ready to reveal the one constant that ties all gravity together.","teach":[{"title":"Shell Theorem: Outside","body":"A uniform hollow spherical shell attracts a point mass outside it exactly as if the shell's entire mass were concentrated at its centre - the sideways components of the pull from every part of the shell cancel, leaving only the net force along the line to the centre.","visual":"🌐"},{"title":"Shell Theorem: Inside","body":"A uniform hollow spherical shell exerts zero net gravitational force on a point mass located anywhere inside it. Pulls from every region of the shell cancel each other out completely.","visual":"🕳️"},{"title":"Cavendish's Experiment","body":"Henry Cavendish first measured the gravitational constant G in 1798 with a torsion balance: two small lead spheres on a suspended bar were attracted by two large lead spheres, twisting the support wire by a measurable angle. The currently accepted value is G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg².","visual":"🪨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A hollow uniform spherical shell exerts what gravitational force on a point mass placed inside it?","options":["zero","the same as if all mass were at the centre","a force that depends on how deep inside it is","an infinite force"],"answer":0,"explain":"Forces from all regions of the shell cancel exactly, so the net force inside is zero."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a point mass outside a uniform spherical shell, the shell's gravity acts as if...","options":["all of its mass were concentrated at the centre","only the near side of the shell existed","the shell were flattened into a disc","there were no force at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the shell theorem for an external point: the whole shell behaves like a point mass at its centre."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The gravitational constant G was first experimentally measured by...","options":["Henry Cavendish","Johannes Kepler","Isaac Newton","Tycho Brahe"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cavendish measured G in 1798 using a torsion-balance apparatus."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the currently accepted value of G, in units of 10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²?","answer":6.67,"explain":"G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg² is the value refined since Cavendish's original measurement."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Unlike a metal shell blocking electric fields, a spherical shell cannot shield a mass inside it from the gravitational pull of...","options":["bodies outside the shell","the shell itself","nothing at all - shielding works fine","other masses also inside the shell"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gravitational shielding is not possible: outside masses still exert their pull on something inside a shell."}]},{"id":"gr4","title":"Weighing the Unweighable Planet","icon":"🌍","kind":"lesson","story":"Skeptical scholars demand proof of the Earth's mass - a planet no scale could ever hold. You must weigh it twice, by two completely different routes, and show the numbers agree.","teach":[{"title":"g at Earth's Surface","body":"Treating the Earth as a uniform sphere made of concentric shells, its whole mass acts as if concentrated at the centre for anyone standing on or above the surface, giving g = GM_E/R_E².","visual":"🌎"},{"title":"Weighing the Earth: Method 1","body":"Rearranging g = GM_E/R_E² gives M_E = gR_E²/G. Using g = 9.81 m/s², R_E = 6.37x10⁶ m, and G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg² gives M_E ≈ 5.97x10²⁴ kg.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Weighing the Earth: Method 2","body":"Treating the Moon as a satellite obeying T² = 4π²R³/(GM_E), with R = 3.84x10⁸ m and T = 27.3 days, gives M_E ≈ 6.02x10²⁴ kg - agreeing with Method 1 to within about 1%.","visual":"🌑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using g = 9.81 m/s², R_E = 6.37x10⁶ m and G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg², compute Earth's mass M_E = gR_E²/G, in units of 10²⁴ kg (2 sig figs).","answer":5.97,"explain":"gR_E²/G = (9.81)(6.37x10⁶)²/(6.67x10⁻¹¹) ≈ 5.97x10²⁴ kg."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the Moon's orbit (R = 3.84x10⁸ m, T = 27.3 days) and T² = 4π²R³/(GM_E), Earth's mass comes out to about 6.02x10²⁴ kg. The text notes the two methods for M_E agree to within less than what whole percent?","answer":1,"explain":"The two independently computed values of M_E (5.97 and 6.02, both x10²⁴ kg) differ by less than 1%."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The popular statement 'Cavendish weighed the Earth' refers to the fact that measuring G allowed scientists to...","options":["calculate the Earth's mass from known values of g and R_E","directly place the Earth on a scale","measure the Earth's radius for the first time","find the Earth's density without knowing its mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"With G known, M_E = gR_E²/G could finally be calculated from measurable quantities."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which quantity is NOT needed to compute Earth's mass via M_E = gR_E²/G?","options":["the Moon's orbital period","the acceleration due to gravity g","the Earth's radius R_E","the gravitational constant G"],"answer":0,"explain":"That formula only needs g, R_E and G; the Moon's period is only needed for the second, independent method."}]},{"id":"gr5","title":"Tower and Mineshaft","icon":"⛏️","kind":"lesson","story":"One expedition team scales a tower toward the clouds; another descends into a mineshaft toward the planet's core. Both radio back that their weight has changed - your job is to predict exactly by how much, before either team trusts their instruments.","teach":[{"title":"g Decreases with Altitude","body":"For a height h above the surface with h << R_E, the acceleration due to gravity becomes g(h) ≈ g(1 - 2h/R_E) - gravity weakens because you are farther from Earth's centre.","visual":"🏔️"},{"title":"g Decreases with Depth","body":"At depth d, only the smaller sphere of radius (R_E - d) pulls on you, since the outer shell of thickness d contributes no net force. This gives g(d) = g(1 - d/R_E).","visual":"⛏️"},{"title":"Altitude vs Depth","body":"For the same small distance travelled, going up reduces g about twice as much (fractionally) as going down the same distance, since the altitude formula has a factor of 2 that the depth formula lacks.","visual":"📉"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A body weighs 250 N on Earth's surface. Using g(d) = g(1 - d/R_E), what does it weigh, in N, halfway to the centre of the Earth (d = R_E/2)?","answer":125,"explain":"At d = R_E/2, g(d) = g(1 - 1/2) = g/2, so the weight halves to 125 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the same small distance h = d above and below the surface, which location has the smaller value of g?","options":["height h above the surface","depth d below the surface","they are exactly equal","cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"g(h) ≈ g(1-2h/R_E) falls faster with distance than g(d) = g(1-d/R_E), so altitude reduces g more."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the very centre of the Earth (d = R_E), the formula g(d) = g(1 - d/R_E) predicts g equals...","options":["zero","the same value as at the surface","an infinite value","a negative value"],"answer":0,"explain":"Setting d = R_E gives g(1-1) = 0: at the centre, there is no net pull in any direction."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using g(h) ≈ g(1 - 2h/R_E) with R_E = 6400 km, by what percentage does g decrease at a height of 64 km above the surface?","answer":2,"explain":"2h/R_E = 2(64)/6400 = 0.02, a 2% decrease in g."}]},{"id":"gr6","title":"Four Corners of a Square Station","icon":"🔋","kind":"lesson","story":"Mission control wants to know exactly how much energy it took to assemble four station modules at the corners of a square in deep space - and what a delicate instrument parked at the very centre would experience.","teach":[{"title":"Gravitational Potential Energy","body":"The potential energy of two point masses m₁ and m₂ separated by distance r is V = -Gm₁m₂/r, taking V = 0 at infinite separation. It is the work done in bringing the masses together from infinitely far apart.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Superposition for Many Masses","body":"For a system of several particles, the total potential energy is the sum of -Gm_im_j/r_ij over every possible pair of particles - an application of the same superposition principle used for forces.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Worked Example: Masses on a Square","body":"Four equal masses m at the corners of a square of side l have 4 side-pairs at distance l and 2 diagonal pairs at distance root-2 times l, giving total potential energy about -5.41 Gm²/l, and a potential at the centre of -4 root-2 Gm/l.","visual":"◻️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Taking gravitational PE to be zero at infinite separation, the PE of two masses at any finite separation r is always...","options":["negative","positive","zero","infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"V = -Gm₁m₂/r is negative for any finite r, becoming zero only as r approaches infinity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Four equal masses m sit at the corners of a square of side l (4 side-pairs at distance l, 2 diagonal pairs at distance root-2 l). The total potential energy is -(4 + 1/root-2)Gm²/l. What is this coefficient, 4 + 1/root-2, to 2 decimal places?","answer":5.41,"explain":"4 + 1/√2 = 4 + 0.7071 ≈ 5.41, matching the NCERT worked example."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same square of side l, the gravitational potential at the centre is -4root-2 Gm/l. What is 4root-2 to 2 decimal places?","answer":5.66,"explain":"4√2 ≈ 5.66, since each corner mass is a distance (√2 l)/2 from the centre."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The total potential energy of an isolated system of particles equals...","options":["the sum of the pairwise potential energies of every possible pair","the potential energy of only the closest pair","the average potential energy of all pairs","always zero, by symmetry"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the superposition principle applied to gravitational potential energy."}]},{"id":"gr7","title":"Breaking Free","icon":"🚀","kind":"lesson","story":"A probe must leave Earth forever, never to fall back - and on the way to a distant outpost it must thread the exact point between two asteroids where their pulls cancel, using no more fuel than necessary.","teach":[{"title":"Escape Speed","body":"Using conservation of energy, the minimum launch speed for an object to just reach infinity from Earth's surface is V_min = sqrt(2GM_E/R_E) = sqrt(2gR_E), which works out numerically to about 11.2 km/s.","visual":"🚀"},{"title":"Escape Speed Elsewhere","body":"The same formula applies to any body. The Moon's escape speed is only about 2.3 km/s, roughly five times smaller than Earth's, because of the Moon's smaller g and radius - which is exactly why the Moon has no atmosphere.","visual":"🌑"},{"title":"Neutral Point Between Two Masses","body":"Between two spheres of mass M and 4M with centres 6R apart, the gravitational pulls cancel at a neutral point; a projectile launched from the smaller sphere needs only enough speed to just reach that point, since beyond it the stronger sphere's pull takes over.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Using V_min = sqrt(2gR_E), the numerically computed escape speed from Earth's surface is approximately...","options":["11.2 km/s","2.3 km/s","1.0 km/s","100 km/s"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plugging in g and R_E for Earth gives an escape speed of about 11.2 km/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The Moon's escape speed is about 2.3 km/s versus Earth's 11.2 km/s. To the nearest whole number, about how many times smaller is the Moon's escape speed?","answer":5,"explain":"11.2/2.3 ≈ 5, so the Moon's escape speed is roughly five times smaller than Earth's."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two spheres of mass M and 4M (equal radius R) have centres 6R apart. Their neutral point, where gravitational pulls cancel, satisfies (6R - r)² = 4r² for r measured from M. Solve for r in units of R.","answer":2,"explain":"6R - r = 2r gives r = 2R (the other root, r = -6R, is not physical here)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A rocket is fired vertically at 5 km/s from Earth's surface (M_E = 6.0x10²⁴ kg, R_E = 6.4x10⁶ m, G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²). Using energy conservation, how high above the surface (in units of 10⁶ m) does it rise before falling back?","answer":1.6,"explain":"Energy conservation gives a maximum distance of about 8.0x10⁶ m from Earth's centre, i.e. 1.6x10⁶ m above the surface."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Escape speed from Earth's surface is 11.2 km/s. A body is launched at thrice this speed (33.6 km/s). Ignoring the Sun and other planets, what is its speed (in km/s, to 1 decimal place) once it is far from Earth?","answer":31.7,"explain":"Energy conservation gives v_far = sqrt(v_i² - v_esc²) = 11.2xsqrt(9-1) ≈ 31.7 km/s."}]},{"id":"gr8","title":"Keeping the Satellites Up","icon":"🛰️","kind":"lesson","story":"You are handed control of a growing satellite network - each one balanced on the razor's edge between falling to Earth and flying off into space. Get the speed, period, and energy right, or lose the satellite forever.","teach":[{"title":"Orbital Speed","body":"For a satellite in circular orbit at distance (R_E + h) from Earth's centre, equating gravitational force to the required centripetal force gives V = sqrt(GM_E/(R_E+h)) - orbital speed decreases as height increases.","visual":"🛰️"},{"title":"Time Period Near the Surface","body":"For a satellite skimming very close to Earth's surface (h negligible compared to R_E), the period is T₀ = 2πsqrt(R_E/g); using R_E = 6400 km and g = 9.8 m/s², T₀ works out to about 85 minutes.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"Energy of an Orbiting Satellite","body":"In a circular orbit, KE = GM_Em/(2(R_E+h)) and PE = -GM_Em/(R_E+h), so the kinetic energy is exactly half the magnitude of the potential energy, making the total energy E = -GM_Em/(2(R_E+h)) negative - the satellite is gravitationally bound.","visual":"⚡"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using T₀ = 2πsqrt(R_E/g) with R_E = 6400 km and g = 9.8 m/s², what is the orbital period of a satellite skimming Earth's surface, in minutes (nearest whole number)?","answer":85,"explain":"T₀ = 2πsqrt(R_E/g) ≈ 5063 s ≈ 85 minutes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a satellite in circular orbit, the kinetic energy compares to the magnitude of the potential energy as...","options":["exactly half","exactly equal","exactly double","unrelated"],"answer":0,"explain":"KE = GM_Em/(2(R_E+h)) while |PE| = GM_Em/(R_E+h), so KE is exactly half of |PE|."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The total mechanical energy of a satellite bound in circular orbit around the Earth is always...","options":["negative","positive","zero","infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"E = -GM_Em/(2(R_E+h)) is always negative for a bound circular orbit."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 200 kg satellite orbits at height 400 km above Earth's surface (R_E = 6.4x10⁶ m, M_E = 6.0x10²⁴ kg, G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg²). The energy needed to send it out of Earth's gravitational influence equals GM_Em/(2(R_E+h)). Compute this energy in units of 10⁹ J (2 sig figs).","answer":5.9,"explain":"GM_Em/(2(R_E+h)) = (6.67x10⁻¹¹)(6.0x10²⁴)(200)/(2x6.8x10⁶) ≈ 5.9x10⁹ J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As a satellite's orbital height h increases, its orbital speed V = sqrt(GM_E/(R_E+h))...","options":["decreases","increases","stays constant","becomes negative"],"answer":0,"explain":"V shrinks as (R_E+h) grows in the denominator, so higher orbits are slower."}]},{"id":"gr-boss","title":"Nemesis: The Binary Collapse","icon":"💥","kind":"boss","story":"Two dying stars, a billion kilometres apart, are falling toward each other in the dark - and everything you have learned about orbits, energy, and escape is the only thing that can predict how this ends. Face the collapse.","boss":{"name":"Nemesis, the Binary Collapse","taunt":"Angular momentum, energy, escape speed - name your law before my stars collide and crush the difference between what you know and what you guessed."},"teach":[{"title":"Everything Converges","body":"Kepler's laws, Newton's law of gravitation, the shell theorem, g and its variation, potential energy, escape speed, and satellite motion are really one connected story: gravity is a single inverse-square force, and every result in this chapter follows from applying energy and force conservation to it.","visual":"🪐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Kepler's second law (equal areas in equal times) is a direct consequence of the conservation of...","options":["angular momentum","linear momentum","total mechanical energy","mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"Angular momentum conservation leads to Kepler's second law, and holds for any central force, not just gravity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two identical stars, each of mass 2x10³⁰ kg and radius 10⁴ km, start from rest a distance 10⁹ km apart and fall together under their mutual gravity alone. Using energy conservation, each star's collision speed works out to about 2.6x10⁶ m/s. Give this speed in units of 10⁶ m/s (2 sig figs).","answer":2.6,"explain":"-Gm²/r_i ≈ mv² - Gm²/r_f with r_f = 2x10⁴ km (surfaces touching) gives v ≈ 2.6x10⁶ m/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two 100 kg spheres of radius 0.10 m sit 1.0 m apart, centre to centre. At the midpoint (0.5 m from each centre), the gravitational potential is V = -2Gm/r. Using G = 6.67x10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg², what is |V| in units of 10⁻⁸ J/kg (2 sig figs)?","answer":2.7,"explain":"2Gm/r = 2(6.67x10⁻¹¹)(100)/0.5 ≈ 2.7x10⁻⁸ J/kg."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the midpoint between those two identical spheres, the net gravitational force on a test mass is zero. This equilibrium is...","options":["unstable","stable","neutral in all directions","impossible to determine"],"answer":0,"explain":"The midpoint between two equal masses is a point of unstable equilibrium along the line joining them."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An astronaut in a small spacecraft orbiting the Earth feels weightless because...","options":["both the astronaut and the spacecraft are in free fall towards the Earth","gravity is truly zero at orbital altitude","the spacecraft shields the astronaut from gravity","the astronaut's mass becomes zero in orbit"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weightlessness happens because astronaut and craft fall together under gravity, not because gravity vanishes."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using V = sqrt(2gR_E) with g ≈ 9.8 m/s² and R_E ≈ 6.4x10⁶ m, what is Earth's escape speed in km/s (nearest tenth)?","answer":11.2,"explain":"sqrt(2 x 9.8 x 6.4x10⁶) ≈ 11200 m/s = 11.2 km/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The gravitational force of a uniform spherical shell on a point mass located inside it is...","options":["zero, everywhere inside","maximum at the centre","equal to its surface value","infinite at the centre"],"answer":0,"explain":"By the shell theorem, forces from all parts of the shell cancel for any point mass located inside it."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For a satellite orbiting at (R_E+h), total energy E = -GM_Em/(2(R_E+h)). Compared to its kinetic energy KE, how many times larger is the magnitude of its potential energy |PE|?","answer":2,"explain":"|PE| = GM_Em/(R_E+h) = 2 x KE, since KE = GM_Em/(2(R_E+h))."}]}]},{"id":"mechpropertiessolids11","index":7,"name":"Mechanical Properties of Solids","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 8","emoji":"🔩","palette":{"from":"#94a3b8","to":"#1e293b"},"lessons":[{"id":"so1","title":"The Rigid Body That Wasn't","icon":"🪨","kind":"lesson","story":"You pull on a helical spring in the Atlas workshop and watch it snap back to shape the instant you let go — but a lump of putty nearby just stays squashed. The old smith says every material in this dungeon hides a secret: how hard does it fight to keep its shape?","teach":[{"title":"Elasticity vs plasticity","body":"A body that regains its original size and shape once the deforming force is removed is called elastic, and the deformation is elastic deformation. A body like putty or mud that stays permanently deformed is called plastic — putty and mud are close to ideal plastics.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Restoring force and stress","body":"When a deforming force acts on a body in static equilibrium, an internal restoring force develops that is equal in magnitude but opposite in direction to the applied force. The restoring force per unit area is called stress: stress = F/A.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Units of stress","body":"The SI unit of stress is N m⁻² or pascal (Pa), with dimensional formula [ML⁻¹T⁻²]. Even an 'appreciably rigid' steel bar deforms under a large enough force — no solid is perfectly rigid.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A stretched spring that snaps back to its original length the instant you let go demonstrates which property?","options":["Elasticity","Plasticity","Viscosity","Rigidity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Elasticity is the tendency of a body to regain its original size and shape when the deforming force is removed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Putty and mud, which stay permanently deformed after being squashed, are called close to ideal...","options":["plastics","elastics","fluids","rigid bodies"],"answer":0,"explain":"Substances with no tendency to regain their previous shape are called plastic; this property is plasticity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Stress is defined as:","options":["restoring force per unit area","applied force times area","change in length per unit length","force per unit volume"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stress = F/A, the restoring force developed per unit cross-sectional area."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of stress?","options":["N m⁻² (pascal)","N m","N m⁻¹","joule"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stress has the same unit as pressure: N m⁻² or pascal (Pa)."}]},{"id":"so2","title":"Three Ways to Deform","icon":"📐","kind":"lesson","story":"A cylinder in the trial chamber can be stretched, sheared, or crushed from every side by fluid pressure — three completely different kinds of stress. You must learn to name each restoring force before the chamber lets you pass.","teach":[{"title":"Longitudinal (tensile/compressive) strain","body":"When a cylinder is stretched or compressed by forces normal to its cross-section, the restoring force per unit area is tensile or compressive stress (together called longitudinal stress). The resulting change in length ΔL to original length L is the longitudinal strain = ΔL/L.","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"Shearing stress and strain","body":"When equal and opposite forces act parallel (tangential) to the cross-sectional area, they produce tangential or shearing stress, causing a relative displacement Δx between opposite faces. Shearing strain = Δx/L = tan θ ≈ θ for small angles.","visual":"✂️"},{"title":"Hydraulic stress and volume strain","body":"A solid sphere immersed in a fluid under pressure is compressed uniformly on all sides — hydraulic compression. The internal restoring force per unit area is hydraulic stress (equal to the fluid pressure), and it produces volume strain = ΔV/V, with no change in shape.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Strain is dimensionless","body":"Since strain is a ratio of change in dimension to the original dimension, it has no units or dimensional formula.","visual":"0️⃣"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Longitudinal strain is defined as:","options":["ΔL/L","ΔV/V","Δx/L","F/A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Longitudinal strain is the change in length ΔL divided by the original length L."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Shearing strain equals tan θ, which for small θ is approximately:","options":["θ","θ²","sin θ","2θ"],"answer":0,"explain":"For small angles, tan θ ≈ θ, so shearing strain ≈ θ."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The text notes that at θ = 10°, there is only about what percent difference between θ (in radians) and tan θ?","answer":1,"explain":"At θ = 10°, tan θ differs from θ by only about 1%, which is why shearing strain ≈ θ for small angles."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A solid sphere compressed uniformly on all sides by fluid pressure experiences a change in:","options":["volume only, not shape","shape only, not volume","both shape and volume","neither shape nor volume"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hydraulic compression decreases volume without any change in the geometrical shape of the body."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does strain have no units or dimensions?","options":["it is a ratio of two quantities with the same dimension","it is always a very small number","it is measured in radians","it depends on the material"],"answer":0,"explain":"Strain is a ratio of change in dimension to original dimension (e.g. ΔL/L), so the units cancel."}]},{"id":"so3","title":"The Curve That Predicts Fracture","icon":"📈","kind":"lesson","story":"The Atlas engineers hand you a stress-strain graph for a mystery metal and dare you to find the exact point where it stops being elastic and the exact point where it will snap. Read the curve wrong, and the bridge you're about to design collapses.","teach":[{"title":"Hooke's law","body":"For small deformations, stress is directly proportional to strain: stress = k × strain, where k is the modulus of elasticity. This empirical law holds for most materials, though some (like elastomers) do not obey it.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"The linear region O to A","body":"On a stress-strain graph, the region from O to A is linear — Hooke's law is obeyed here, and the body fully regains its original dimensions when the force is removed. This is the elastic region.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Yield point and plastic deformation","body":"From A to B, stress and strain are no longer proportional, but the body still returns to its original shape — B is the yield point (elastic limit), with yield strength σy. Beyond B, the body no longer fully recovers: it has undergone plastic deformation and retains a permanent set even at zero stress.","visual":"⚠️"},{"title":"Ultimate strength, fracture, brittle vs ductile","body":"Point D is the ultimate tensile strength (σu); beyond it, strain increases even as force is reduced, and fracture occurs at E. If D and E are close together the material is brittle; if far apart, it is ductile. Elastomers like rubber or aortic tissue can be stretched many times their length and still recover, but they do not obey Hooke's law over most of that range.","visual":"💥"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Hooke's law states that, for small deformations:","options":["stress is directly proportional to strain","stress equals strain squared","strain is independent of stress","stress is inversely proportional to strain"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hooke's law: stress ∝ strain, or stress = k × strain, where k is the modulus of elasticity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"On the stress-strain curve, the point marking the elastic limit (beyond which permanent deformation begins) is called the:","options":["yield point","fracture point","origin","ultimate strength point"],"answer":0,"explain":"Point B is the yield point, also called the elastic limit, with corresponding yield strength σy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The stress corresponding to point D on the curve, beyond which fracture soon follows, is called the:","options":["ultimate tensile strength","yield strength","modulus of elasticity","Poisson's ratio"],"answer":0,"explain":"Point D is the ultimate tensile strength (σu) of the material."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the ultimate strength point D and the fracture point E are close together on the curve, the material is said to be:","options":["brittle","ductile","elastomeric","plastic"],"answer":0,"explain":"A material is brittle when D and E are close; it is ductile when they are far apart."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Rubber and the elastic tissue of the aorta, which can be stretched to large strains and still recover, are called:","options":["elastomers","brittle solids","rigid bodies","plastics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Substances like rubber and aortic tissue that can be stretched to large strains are called elastomers."}]},{"id":"so4","title":"Young's Modulus and the Steel Rod","icon":"🏗️","kind":"lesson","story":"A 100 kN winch is about to stretch a structural steel rod in the Atlas testing bay, and a thighbone from a circus human-pyramid act needs checking too. Calculate the stress, the stretch, and the strain correctly, or the rod — or the performer — takes the fall.","teach":[{"title":"Defining Young's modulus","body":"The ratio of tensile (or compressive) stress σ to longitudinal strain ε is Young's modulus, Y = σ/ε = (F×L)/(A×ΔL). Its unit is the same as stress, N m⁻² (Pa). Metals have large Young's moduli, so they need a large force to produce a small change in length.","visual":"🔩"},{"title":"Comparing metals","body":"To stretch a thin steel wire of 0.1 cm² cross-section by 0.1%, a force of 2000 N is required — for the same strain, aluminium needs only 690 N, brass 900 N, and copper 1100 N. This shows steel is more elastic than copper, brass, or aluminium, which is why steel is preferred in heavy-duty machines and structural designs.","visual":"⚙️"},{"title":"Worked example: the steel rod","body":"A structural steel rod of radius 10 mm and length 1.0 m is stretched by a 100 kN force (Y = 2.0×10¹¹ N m⁻²). Stress = F/(πr²) = 3.18×10⁸ N m⁻²; elongation = stress×L/Y = 1.59×10⁻³ m = 1.59 mm; strain = ΔL/L ≈ 0.16%.","visual":"🧮"},{"title":"Worked example: the thighbone","body":"In a human pyramid, a performer's thighbone (femur, length 0.5 m, radius 2.0 cm, Y = 9.4×10⁹ N m⁻²) supporting 1078 N compresses by ΔL = FL/(YA) = 4.55×10⁻⁵ m — a fractional decrease of only 0.0091%.","visual":"🦴"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 8.1: A steel rod of radius 10 mm, length 1.0 m, is stretched by a 100 kN force. What is the stress, in units of 10⁸ N m⁻² (round to 2 decimals)?","answer":3.18,"explain":"Stress = F/(πr²) = (1×10⁵ N)/(3.14×(0.01 m)²) = 3.18×10⁸ N m⁻²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same steel rod (Y = 2.0×10¹¹ N m⁻²), what is the elongation in mm?","answer":1.59,"explain":"Elongation = (F/A)×L/Y = (3.18×10⁸ N m⁻² × 1 m)/(2×10¹¹ N m⁻²) = 1.59×10⁻³ m = 1.59 mm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For the same longitudinal strain, which wire needs the LEAST force among steel (2000 N), copper (1100 N), brass (900 N), and aluminium (690 N)?","options":["aluminium","steel","copper","brass"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aluminium needs only 690 N for the same 0.1% strain, the smallest of the four — meaning it has the smallest Young's modulus of the group."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the human-pyramid example, each thighbone (L = 0.5 m, radius 2.0 cm, Y = 9.4×10⁹ N m⁻²) supports 1078 N. What is the compression, in units of 10⁻⁵ m (round to 2 decimals)?","answer":4.55,"explain":"ΔL = FL/(YA) = (1078×0.5)/(9.4×10⁹×1.26×10⁻³) = 4.55×10⁻⁵ m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Young's modulus is a meaningful elastic constant for:","options":["solids only","solids and liquids","liquids and gases only","gases only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Young's modulus and shear modulus are relevant only for solids since only solids have definite lengths and shapes."}]},{"id":"so5","title":"Shear Modulus and the Lead Slab","icon":"🧱","kind":"lesson","story":"A square lead slab is riveted to the floor of the vault, and a tangential force is about to shove its top edge sideways. You must find exactly how far that edge slides before the vault door will open.","teach":[{"title":"Defining shear modulus","body":"The ratio of shearing stress to shearing strain is the shear modulus, G = (F/A)/(Δx/L) = (F×L)/(A×Δx), also called the modulus of rigidity. Its SI unit is N m⁻² or Pa, and for most materials G ≈ Y/3, generally smaller than Young's modulus.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Worked example: the lead slab","body":"A square lead slab of side 50 cm and thickness 10 cm has its lower edge fixed and its narrow face pushed by a shearing force of 9.0×10⁴ N. The face area is 0.05 m², so the shearing stress is 1.8×10⁶ N m⁻². With G(lead) = 5.6×10⁹ N m⁻², the displacement Δx = (stress×L)/G = 1.6×10⁻⁴ m = 0.16 mm.","visual":"📦"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The shear modulus G is also known as the:","options":["modulus of rigidity","modulus of compressibility","yield strength","Poisson ratio"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shear modulus is also called the modulus of rigidity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Lead slab example: a 9.0×10⁴ N shearing force acts on a face of area 0.05 m². What is the shearing stress, in units of 10⁶ N m⁻²?","answer":1.8,"explain":"Stress = F/A = (9.0×10⁴ N)/(0.05 m²) = 1.8×10⁶ N m⁻²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"With G(lead) = 5.6×10⁹ N m⁻² and slab thickness L = 0.5 m, how far (in mm) does the upper edge get displaced?","answer":0.16,"explain":"Δx = (stress × L)/G = (1.8×10⁶ × 0.5)/(5.6×10⁹) = 1.6×10⁻⁴ m = 0.16 mm."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For most materials, how does the shear modulus G compare to Young's modulus Y?","options":["G is roughly Y/3","G is roughly 3×Y","G equals Y exactly","G is roughly Y/10"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text notes that for most materials, G ≈ Y/3."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From the shear moduli table, what is the shear modulus of steel, in GPa?","answer":84,"explain":"Table 8.2 lists the shear modulus of steel as 84 GPa (84×10⁹ N m⁻²)."}]},{"id":"so6","title":"Bulk Modulus, Poisson's Secret, and the Ocean Floor","icon":"🌊","kind":"lesson","story":"You dive to the bottom of a simulated Indian Ocean trench, 3000 m down, where the crushing pressure compresses the water itself. Solve for how much it shrinks — and learn why a stretched wire also quietly gets thinner as it gets longer.","teach":[{"title":"Bulk modulus","body":"The ratio of hydraulic stress to volume strain is the bulk modulus, B = −p/(ΔV/V); its SI unit is N m⁻² or Pa. Since an increase in pressure decreases volume, B is always positive. Its reciprocal, compressibility k = 1/B, is the fractional change in volume per unit increase in pressure.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Solids, liquids, gases compared","body":"Bulk moduli for solids are much larger than for liquids, which are in turn much larger than for gases — solids are the least compressible and gases the most, roughly a million times more compressible than solids, because of how tightly atoms are coupled to their neighbours.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"Worked example: the ocean floor","body":"At an average Indian Ocean depth of 3000 m, the pressure is p = hρg = 3000×1000×10 = 3×10⁷ N m⁻². With B(water) = 2.2×10⁹ N m⁻², the fractional compression ΔV/V = p/B = 1.36×10⁻² or 1.36%.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Poisson's ratio","body":"The strain perpendicular to the applied force is the lateral strain; Poisson showed it is proportional to the longitudinal strain within the elastic limit. Poisson's ratio = lateral strain/longitudinal strain, a dimensionless number depending only on the material — about 0.28 to 0.30 for steel, and about 0.33 for aluminium alloys.","visual":"📎"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bulk modulus B relates hydraulic stress to which strain?","options":["volume strain, ΔV/V","longitudinal strain, ΔL/L","shearing strain, Δx/L","lateral strain"],"answer":0,"explain":"B = −p/(ΔV/V), the ratio of hydraulic stress to volume strain."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At the bottom of the 3000 m deep Indian Ocean (B water = 2.2×10⁹ N m⁻², g = 10 m s⁻²), what is the fractional compression ΔV/V, as a percentage (round to 2 decimals)?","answer":1.36,"explain":"p = hρg = 3×10⁷ N m⁻²; ΔV/V = p/B = (3×10⁷)/(2.2×10⁹) = 1.36×10⁻² = 1.36%."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which state of matter has the largest bulk modulus, i.e. is least compressible?","options":["solids","liquids","gases","all are equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bulk moduli for solids are much larger than for liquids, which are much larger than for gases — solids are least compressible."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Poisson's ratio is defined as:","options":["lateral strain divided by longitudinal strain","longitudinal strain divided by lateral strain","stress divided by strain","volume strain divided by longitudinal strain"],"answer":0,"explain":"Poisson's ratio is the ratio of lateral strain to longitudinal strain in a stretched wire."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the text, Poisson's ratio for aluminium alloys is about:","answer":0.33,"explain":"The text states Poisson's ratio for aluminium alloys is about 0.33."}]},{"id":"so7","title":"Cranes, Beams, and the Height of Mountains","icon":"🏔️","kind":"lesson","story":"The Atlas needs a crane rope strong enough to lift ten tonnes, a bridge beam that won't sag under traffic, and an explanation for why no mountain on Earth grows taller than about ten kilometres. All three answers come from the same elastic principles.","teach":[{"title":"Elastic potential energy","body":"Work done in stretching a wire is stored as elastic potential energy. The energy per unit volume is u = ½ × stress × strain = ½ × Young's modulus × strain², obtained by integrating F = YA(l/L) over the elongation.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Designing a crane rope","body":"To lift a 10-tonne load without exceeding mild steel's yield strength (σy ≈ 300×10⁶ N m⁻²), the rope's cross-section must satisfy A ≥ Mg/σy ≈ 3.3×10⁻⁴ m² (about 1 cm radius). A large safety margin (~10×) gives a practical radius of about 3 cm — made of many thin braided wires for flexibility rather than one rigid rod.","visual":"🏗️"},{"title":"Beams and buckling","body":"A beam of length l, breadth b, depth d, loaded at the centre, sags by δ = Wl³/(4bd³Y). Since δ ∝ d⁻³ but only b⁻¹, increasing the depth is far more effective than increasing the breadth at reducing sag — though too deep a bar can buckle, which is why beams commonly use an I-shaped cross-section.","visual":"🌉"},{"title":"Why mountains stop at ~10 km","body":"At the base of a mountain of height h and rock density ρ, the shear stress is about hρg. Setting this equal to the elastic limit of typical rock (30×10⁷ N m⁻², with ρ = 3×10³ kg m⁻³) gives h ≈ 10 km — greater than Mt. Everest, which is why mountains can't grow much taller before the rock at their base flows.","visual":"⛰️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Elastic potential energy per unit volume of a stretched wire is given by:","options":["½ × stress × strain","stress × strain","stress / strain","2 × stress × strain"],"answer":0,"explain":"u = ½ σ ε, i.e. half the product of stress and strain, per unit volume."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"To lift a 10-tonne (10⁴ kg) load with a mild-steel rope (yield strength 300×10⁶ N m⁻², g = 9.8 m s⁻²), what is the minimum cross-sectional area required, in units of 10⁻⁴ m² (round to 1 decimal)?","answer":3.3,"explain":"A ≥ Mg/σy = (10⁴×9.8)/(300×10⁶) = 3.3×10⁻⁴ m²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a beam sagging as δ = Wl³/(4bd³Y), which change is more effective at reducing the sag: increasing depth d, or increasing breadth b, by the same factor?","options":["increasing depth d","increasing breadth b","both are equally effective","neither affects sag"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since δ ∝ d⁻³ but only b⁻¹, increasing depth reduces sag far more effectively than increasing breadth."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the elastic limit of typical rock (30×10⁷ N m⁻²) and rock density 3×10³ kg m⁻³, the text derives a maximum mountain height of about how many km?","answer":10,"explain":"h = (30×10⁷)/(3×10³×10) = 10 km, more than the height of Mt. Everest."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why are crane ropes made of many thin wires braided together instead of one thick rigid rod?","options":["for ease of manufacture, flexibility and strength","to save on the cost of steel","because thin wires are stronger per wire than thick ones","to reduce the yield strength needed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text notes braided thin wires give ease of manufacture, flexibility, and strength compared to one rigid rod of the same radius."}]},{"id":"so-boss","title":"Boss: The Stress-Strain Tyrant","icon":"👹","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Atlas forge stands the Stress-Strain Tyrant, a shifting golem of steel, lead, and ocean water who mocks every adventurer that cannot tell elastic limit from ultimate strength. Only mastery of every modulus, curve, and worked example will crack its armor.","boss":{"name":"The Stress-Strain Tyrant","taunt":"You call yourself elastic? Push past my yield point and you'll never spring back!"},"teach":[{"title":"Full review: three moduli","body":"Young's modulus Y (stress/longitudinal strain, solids only), shear modulus G (shearing stress/shearing strain, solids only), and bulk modulus B (hydraulic stress/volume strain, solids, liquids and gases) together describe elastic behaviour under Hooke's law, stress = k × strain.","visual":"🛡️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Hooke's law can be written as:","options":["stress = k × strain","strain = k × stress²","stress = k / strain","k = stress + strain"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hooke's law: stress ∝ strain, so stress = k × strain, where k is the modulus of elasticity."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 8.2: copper (L=2.2 m, Y=1.1×10¹¹) and steel (L=1.6 m, Y=2.0×10¹¹) wires of equal diameter, connected end to end, stretch a total of 0.70 mm. What load W (in N) was applied?","answer":180,"explain":"Solving ΔLc=5.0×10⁻⁴ m, ΔLs=2.0×10⁻⁴ m gives W = (A×Yc×ΔLc)/Lc ≈ 1.8×10² N = 180 N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A material whose stress-strain curve shows a permanent set (nonzero strain at zero stress) after the load is removed has undergone:","options":["plastic deformation","elastic deformation","hydraulic compression","Poisson contraction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Once loaded beyond the yield point B, the body does not return to its original dimensions — this is plastic deformation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"From Table 8.2, what is the shear modulus of steel, in GPa?","answer":84,"explain":"Steel's shear modulus (modulus of rigidity) is 84 GPa."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bulk modulus B is defined by which equation?","options":["B = −p/(ΔV/V)","B = p × (ΔV/V)","B = F/(A×θ)","B = (F×L)/(A×ΔL)"],"answer":0,"explain":"B = −p/(ΔV/V), the ratio of hydraulic stress to volume strain (negative sign because volume decreases as pressure increases)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Poisson's ratio for aluminium alloys is about what value?","answer":0.33,"explain":"The text gives Poisson's ratio for aluminium alloys as about 0.33 (steel is 0.28–0.30)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which elastic modulus applies to solids, liquids, AND gases?","options":["bulk modulus","Young's modulus","shear modulus","modulus of rigidity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bulk modulus is relevant for solids, liquids and gases, since it only concerns volume change, not shape."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the elastic limit of rock and its density, the chapter concludes the maximum possible height of a mountain on Earth is about how many km?","answer":10,"explain":"Setting hρg equal to the rock's elastic limit (30×10⁷ N m⁻²) gives h ≈ 10 km, which is more than the height of Mt. Everest."}]}]},{"id":"mechpropertiesfluids11","index":8,"name":"Mechanical Properties of Fluids","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 9","emoji":"💧","palette":{"from":"#0ea5e9","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"fl1","title":"The Needle, the Spoon, and the Plank","icon":"📌","kind":"lesson","story":"A circus master needs you to prove why a heavy wooden plank saves a performer's chest from a placed stone, while a sharp needle pressed with the very same force would not be forgiven so kindly. Grab the idea of force and area before the next show begins.","teach":[{"title":"Force per Unit Area","body":"Pressure is defined as the average normal force per unit area exerted by a fluid, Pav = F/A. The smaller the area over which a given force acts, the greater the pressure - which is why a sharp needle pierces skin but the blunt back of a spoon pressed with the same force does not.","visual":"📌"},{"title":"Pressure is a Scalar","body":"It is only the component of force normal to a surface that enters the definition of pressure, not the force vector itself. Pressure has no direction of its own and can be assigned no direction - it is the same in all directions at a point in a fluid at rest.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Pascal's Law: Same Height, Same Pressure","body":"Blaise Pascal showed that the pressure in a fluid at rest is the same at all points at the same height. This can be proved by balancing the forces on a tiny prism-shaped element of fluid, which shows the pressure on every face must be equal.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Units of Pressure","body":"The SI unit of pressure is the pascal (Pa), equal to 1 N/m², named after Blaise Pascal. Common alternative units are 1 atm = 1.013 × 10⁵ Pa (atmospheric pressure at sea level), 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa, and 1 torr = 133 Pa.","visual":"📏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A sharp needle pierces skin far more easily than a blunt spoon pressed with the same force because...","options":["the needle concentrates the same force over a much smaller area, producing much higher pressure","the needle is heavier than the spoon","pressure depends only on force, not on area","the spoon exerts a force parallel to the skin instead of normal to it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since P = F/A, shrinking the contact area while keeping the force the same greatly increases the pressure."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Pressure is best described as a...","options":["scalar quantity, since only the normal component of force matters and no direction can be assigned to it","vector quantity, pointing in the direction of the applied force","vector quantity, always pointing downward","tensor quantity with three independent directions"],"answer":0,"explain":"Pressure is a scalar: it is the normal-force-per-area at a point, with no direction of its own, unlike the force vector that produces it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Pascal's law (in its basic form) states that in a fluid at rest...","options":["the pressure is the same at all points that are at the same height","the pressure increases linearly with horizontal distance","the pressure is greatest at the centre of the container","the pressure depends on the shape of the container at every point"],"answer":0,"explain":"Balancing forces on a small prism of fluid shows the pressure must be equal at all points at the same height in a fluid at rest."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The two femurs (thigh bones), each of cross-sectional area 10 cm², support the upper body of a person of mass 40 kg. Taking g = 10 m/s², what average pressure (in units of 10⁵ Pa) do the femurs sustain?","answer":2,"explain":"Total area A = 20 × 10⁻⁴ m², force F = 40 × 10 = 400 N, so Pav = F/A = 400 / (20×10⁻⁴) = 2 × 10⁵ Pa."}]},{"id":"fl2","title":"Ten Metres Under","icon":"🌊","kind":"lesson","story":"A diving bell is being lowered into a lake for a rescue, and the crew needs to know exactly how hard the water will squeeze it at ten metres down - and again at a thousand. Chart the pressure with depth before the hull is pushed past its limit.","teach":[{"title":"Pressure Grows with Depth","body":"In a liquid open to the atmosphere, the pressure at depth h below the surface is P = Pa + ρgh, where ρ is the fluid's density and Pa is atmospheric pressure. Only the height of the fluid column matters - not the cross-sectional area or shape of the container.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Gauge Pressure","body":"The excess of pressure over atmospheric, P − Pa = ρgh, is called the gauge pressure at that point. Most pressure-measuring instruments, such as a tyre gauge or a blood-pressure gauge, read gauge pressure rather than absolute pressure.","visual":"🩺"},{"title":"The Hydrostatic Paradox","body":"Three vessels of very different shapes, connected at the bottom and filled with water, all end up with liquid at the same level - even though they hold very different amounts of water. This is because pressure at a given depth depends only on that depth, not on the container's shape.","visual":"🏺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A swimmer is 10 m below the surface of a lake. Taking ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 10 m/s² and Pa = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa, the pressure on the swimmer works out to about 2.01 × 10⁵ Pa. Rounded to the nearest whole number, about how many atmospheres is that?","answer":2,"explain":"P = Pa + ρgh = 1.01×10⁵ + 1000×10×10 = 2.01×10⁵ Pa ≈ 2 atm, a 100% increase over surface pressure."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At a depth of 1000 m in the ocean (ρ = 1.03 × 10³ kg/m³, g = 10 m/s², Pa = 1.01 × 10⁵ Pa), what is the absolute pressure, in units of atm (nearest whole number, using 1 atm ≈ 1.01×10⁵ Pa)?","answer":104,"explain":"P = Pa + ρgh = 1.01×10⁵ + (1.03×10³)(10)(1000) = 104.01×10⁵ Pa ≈ 104 atm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same submarine window (area 0.04 m²) at 1000 m depth, the gauge pressure outside is Pg = 103 × 10⁵ Pa while the inside is kept at sea-level pressure. What net force (in units of 10⁵ N) acts on the window?","answer":4.12,"explain":"F = Pg × A = 103×10⁵ Pa × 0.04 m² = 4.12×10⁵ N, since atmospheric pressure inside cancels the atmospheric part outside."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Three connecting vessels A, B and C, all of different shapes, are filled with water and connected at the bottom. Why does the water settle at the same level in all three?","options":["Because pressure at the bottom depends only on the depth of water above it, not on the vessel's shape or cross-section","Because all three vessels contain the same volume of water","Because the vessel with the widest base always fills highest","Because atmospheric pressure only acts on the widest vessel"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the hydrostatic paradox: since P = Pa + ρgh depends only on h, equal pressures at the bottom force equal liquid levels regardless of shape."}]},{"id":"fl3","title":"Torricelli's Tube","icon":"🧪","kind":"lesson","story":"An old workshop holds two forgotten pressure-measuring devices - a tall glass tube of mercury and a bent U-shaped tube - while a storm gathers outside. Learn to read them before the falling mercury tells you it is too late.","teach":[{"title":"The Mercury Barometer","body":"Torricelli's barometer is a glass tube, closed at one end, filled with mercury and inverted into a trough of mercury; the near-vacuum above the column lets the atmosphere's own pressure be measured as Pa = ρgh. At sea level this mercury column stands about 76 cm tall - one atmosphere.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"The Open-Tube Manometer","body":"A U-tube manometer, with one end open to the atmosphere and the other connected to the system being measured, gives the gauge pressure P − Pa directly, proportional to the height difference h of the liquid levels in its two arms.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Everyday Units of Pressure","body":"A pressure equivalent to 1 mm of mercury is called a torr (1 torr = 133 Pa), used in medicine and physiology; meteorologists use the bar (1 bar = 10⁵ Pa). A drop in the barometer's mercury level by 10 mm or more is a classic sign of an approaching storm.","visual":"🌪️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a mercury barometer, atmospheric pressure is measured by...","options":["the height of the mercury column it can support against the near-vacuum above it, via Pa = ρgh","the diameter of the glass tube used","the temperature of the mercury vapour above the column","the weight of the trough of mercury at the bottom"],"answer":0,"explain":"The vacuum above the column has negligible pressure, so the supported mercury column height directly gives Pa = ρgh."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1 torr equals how many pascals?","answer":133,"explain":"A torr is defined as the pressure of a 1 mm column of mercury, which equals 133 Pa."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If the atmosphere's density were a constant 1.29 kg/m³ all the way up, and Pa = 1.01×10⁵ Pa with g = 9.8 m/s², roughly how high (in km, nearest whole number) would the atmosphere extend?","answer":8,"explain":"ρgh = Pa gives h = 1.01×10⁵ / (1.29×9.8) ≈ 7989 m ≈ 8 km (in reality density falls with height, so air extends much farther)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A sudden drop in the barometer's mercury level by 10 mm or more is traditionally taken as a sign of...","options":["an approaching storm","a rise in sea level","an increase in water density","the mercury evaporating"],"answer":0,"explain":"Falling atmospheric pressure, seen as a dropping mercury column, is a classic storm warning."}]},{"id":"fl4","title":"The Hydraulic Advantage","icon":"🏗️","kind":"lesson","story":"The garage's single small piston must somehow lift an entire car onto its ramp, and the mechanic swears it is not magic - just Pascal's law waiting to be trusted. Work through the numbers before the jack is trusted with real weight.","teach":[{"title":"Pascal's Law, Applied","body":"Whenever external pressure is applied to any part of a fluid enclosed in a vessel, it is transmitted undiminished and equally in all directions throughout the fluid and to the walls of the container. This is the practical, everyday form of Pascal's law.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"The Hydraulic Lift","body":"A small force F1 on a small piston of area A1 creates a pressure P = F1/A1 that is transmitted through the liquid to a larger piston of area A2, producing an upward force F2 = F1(A2/A1). The area ratio A2/A1 is the mechanical advantage of the device.","visual":"🏗️"},{"title":"Hydraulic Brakes","body":"Pressing the brake pedal moves a small master piston; the resulting pressure is transmitted undiminished through the brake oil to pistons at all four wheels, so a small force on the pedal produces a large, and equal, braking effort at every wheel.","visual":"🛑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two connected syringes hold water; the smaller piston has diameter 1.0 cm and the larger 3.0 cm. If a force of 10 N is applied to the smaller piston, what force (in N) is exerted on the larger piston?","answer":90,"explain":"F2 = F1 (A2/A1) = F1 (d2/d1)² = 10 × (3/1)² = 90 N."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a car lift, compressed air pushes a small piston of radius 5.0 cm, transmitting pressure to a second piston of radius 15 cm that must lift a 1350 kg car (g = 9.8 m/s²). What force F1 (in N, nearest 10) must the small piston exert?","answer":1470,"explain":"F1 = F2 (A1/A2) = mg (r1/r2)² = 1350×9.8×(5/15)² = 13230/9 ≈ 1470 N ≈ 1.5×10³ N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a hydraulic lift with a small piston of area A1 and a large piston of area A2, the mechanical advantage (the factor by which the applied force is multiplied) equals...","options":["A2/A1","A1/A2","A1 × A2","A2 − A1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since pressure F1/A1 = F2/A2, the output force F2 = F1(A2/A1), so A2/A1 is the mechanical advantage."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An important advantage of hydraulic brakes in a car is that...","options":["the pressure from pressing the pedal is transmitted equally to all four wheel cylinders, so braking effort is equal on all wheels","only the front wheels ever receive braking force","the brake fluid must be compressible for the system to work","each wheel needs a different pedal to brake independently"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because pressure is transmitted undiminished and equally throughout the fluid, all four wheel cylinders get the same braking pressure."}]},{"id":"fl5","title":"Rivers That Don't Cross","icon":"〰️","kind":"lesson","story":"A river guide asks you to mark where the water will run smooth and where it will churn into white-water rapids around the rocks ahead. Trace the streamlines and the equation that governs them before the rapids catch you off guard.","teach":[{"title":"Streamlines","body":"A streamline is the curve whose tangent at any point gives the direction of the fluid's velocity there. In steady flow, no two streamlines ever cross - if they did, an arriving fluid particle would have to have two different velocities at once.","visual":"〰️"},{"title":"Laminar vs Turbulent Flow","body":"Steady, layered (laminar) flow is only maintained up to a limiting 'critical speed'; beyond that, the flow loses its steadiness and becomes turbulent, as seen in the foamy, whirlpool-like white-water rapids where a fast stream meets rocks.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"The Equation of Continuity","body":"For the steady flow of an incompressible fluid, the same mass must cross every cross-section of a pipe each second, giving Av = constant. So the fluid speeds up where the pipe narrows and slows down where it widens.","visual":"🚿"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A streamline is defined as...","options":["a curve whose tangent at any point gives the direction of the fluid velocity at that point","the exact path every fluid particle takes regardless of time","a line showing where fluid pressure is zero","the boundary of the container holding the fluid"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the textbook definition: a streamline's tangent at any point matches the fluid's velocity direction there."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can two streamlines never cross each other in steady flow?","options":["Because a fluid particle arriving at the crossing point would need two different velocities at once, contradicting steady flow","Because streamlines are always parallel straight lines","Because fluids cannot have curved paths","Because crossing streamlines would violate Pascal's law"],"answer":0,"explain":"If two streamlines crossed, the fluid at that point would have an ambiguous velocity, which is impossible in steady flow."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a pipe of varying cross-section carrying an incompressible fluid in steady flow, at a point where the pipe's area is smaller, the fluid's speed is...","options":["higher, since Av = constant means speed and area vary inversely","lower, since a narrow pipe always slows fluid down","unrelated to the area, only to the pressure","zero, since narrow pipes block flow"],"answer":0,"explain":"The equation of continuity, Av = constant, means a smaller cross-sectional area A must be matched by a larger speed v."}]},{"id":"fl6","title":"Bernoulli's Bargain","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"An engineer needs the pressure drop through a pipe of changing width and changing height, but has no gauge to spare - only Bernoulli's equation and the fluid's own conservation of energy. Work it out before the pipeline is sealed shut.","teach":[{"title":"Bernoulli's Equation","body":"For the steady flow of a non-viscous, incompressible fluid along a streamline, the sum of pressure, kinetic energy per unit volume, and potential energy per unit volume stays constant: P + ½rv² + rgh = constant. Two points on the same streamline satisfy P1+½rv1²+rgh1 = P2+½rv2²+rgh2.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Where It Comes From","body":"Bernoulli's equation follows from the work-energy theorem applied to a moving element of fluid: the net work done on it by the pressure difference between two ends equals the sum of its change in kinetic energy and its change in gravitational potential energy.","visual":"🛠️"},{"title":"Its Limits","body":"Bernoulli's equation strictly applies only to steady, non-turbulent flow of a non-viscous (frictionless), incompressible fluid. Real fluids have viscosity, which converts some kinetic energy into heat, so the pressure downstream ends up a little lower than the ideal prediction.","visual":"⚠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As we move along a streamline in steady, non-viscous, incompressible flow, Bernoulli's principle says that the sum which stays constant is...","options":["pressure + kinetic energy per unit volume + potential energy per unit volume","pressure alone","velocity alone","mass flow rate divided by pressure"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bernoulli's equation is P + ½ρv² + ρgh = constant along a streamline."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bernoulli's equation, in the form derived in the text, strictly requires the fluid to be...","options":["non-viscous and incompressible, with steady (non-turbulent) flow","viscous and compressible","turbulent and incompressible","at absolute zero temperature"],"answer":0,"explain":"The derivation assumes no energy lost to internal friction (non-viscous), constant density (incompressible), and steady flow."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For horizontal flow (h1 = h2), if the fluid speeds up as it passes through a constriction, Bernoulli's equation predicts that the pressure there...","options":["decreases","increases","stays exactly the same","becomes negative"],"answer":0,"explain":"With h1=h2, P + ½ρv² = constant, so a rise in v must be accompanied by a fall in P."}]},{"id":"fl7","title":"The Leak and the Wing","icon":"✈️","kind":"lesson","story":"A punctured water tank is draining fast, and across town a spinning cricket ball is curving wildly past the batsman - both riddles share the very same Bernoulli's-principle answer. Work out the speed of the leak and the lift on the wing before the tank runs dry.","teach":[{"title":"Speed of Efflux: Torricelli's Law","body":"For a tank open to the atmosphere with a small hole at depth h below the free surface, applying Bernoulli's equation gives the efflux speed v = √(2gh) - identical to the speed of a body that has fallen freely through height h.","visual":"🕳️"},{"title":"Dynamic Lift and the Magnus Effect","body":"A spinning ball drags air along with it, crowding the streamlines (and raising the flow speed) on one side while thinning them on the other. The resulting pressure difference pushes the ball sideways - this dynamic lift due to spin is called the Magnus effect.","visual":"🎾"},{"title":"Lift on an Aerofoil","body":"An aeroplane wing (aerofoil) is oriented so that air is forced to travel faster over its curved top than underneath, crowding the streamlines above it. By Bernoulli's principle this lowers the pressure above the wing, producing the upward dynamic lift that balances the plane's weight.","visual":"✈️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Torricelli's law for the speed of efflux from a small hole at depth h in an open tank gives v = √(2gh), which is the same expression as the speed of...","options":["a body that has fallen freely through a height h","sound travelling through the same fluid","a satellite in circular orbit at height h","a wave travelling along the tank's surface"],"answer":0,"explain":"Applying Bernoulli's equation between the free surface and the hole yields exactly the free-fall speed formula, v = √(2gh)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ball moving through air without any spin experiences no sideways force because...","options":["the streamlines above and below it are symmetric, so the air speed (and hence pressure) is the same on both sides","gravity cancels all aerodynamic forces on a non-spinning ball","air has no viscosity at all","Bernoulli's principle only applies to spinning objects"],"answer":0,"explain":"With symmetric streamlines, there is zero pressure difference between the top and bottom of a non-spinning ball, so no net vertical force acts on it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"An aerofoil shaped wing produces upward dynamic lift mainly because...","options":["air flows faster over the curved upper surface than below it, lowering pressure above and creating a net upward force","the wing is heavier on its lower surface","air is compressed to a solid layer beneath the wing","gravity pulls harder on air below the wing than above it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Faster flow above the wing means lower pressure there by Bernoulli's principle, so the higher pressure below pushes the wing upward."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A fully loaded Boeing aircraft of mass 3.3 × 10⁵ kg has a total wing area of 500 m² and is in level flight (g = 9.8 m/s²). What is the pressure difference between the lower and upper wing surfaces, in units of 10³ Pa (nearest tenth)?","answer":6.5,"explain":"The weight is balanced by ΔP × A, so ΔP = mg/A = (3.3×10⁵ × 9.8)/500 ≈ 6.5×10³ Pa."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same Boeing wing (density of air = 1.2 kg/m³, average flow speed 267 m/s, ΔP ≈ 6.5×10³ Pa), the fractional increase (v2−v1)/vav needed on the upper surface works out to about what percentage (nearest whole number)?","answer":8,"explain":"(v2−v1)/vav = ΔP/(ρ vav) ≈ 6.5×10³/(1.2×267) ≈ 0.08, i.e. the flow above the wing need only be about 8% faster than below."}]},{"id":"fl8","title":"Honey, Oil, and the Falling Raindrop","icon":"🍯","kind":"lesson","story":"A jar of honey and a jar of oil sit side by side, daring you to explain why one drags a stirring spoon so much harder than the other. Trace that drag all the way down to a single raindrop falling at its final, constant speed.","teach":[{"title":"Internal Friction: Viscosity","body":"Viscosity is a fluid's internal friction: when layers of fluid move at different speeds relative to each other, they drag on one another. A greater force is needed to shear a more viscous fluid, like honey, than a less viscous one, like oil, at the same speed.","visual":"🍯"},{"title":"Coefficient of Viscosity","body":"The coefficient of viscosity η is defined as the ratio of shearing stress to the rate of strain (shear stress divided by v/l). Its SI unit is the poiseiulle (Pa s). Liquids get less viscous as they warm up, while gases get more viscous with rising temperature.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Stokes' Law","body":"A sphere of radius a moving with speed v through a fluid of viscosity η experiences a viscous drag force F = 6πηav, directed opposite to its motion. This is Stokes' law.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Terminal Velocity","body":"A sphere falling through a fluid speeds up until the viscous drag plus buoyant force exactly balance its weight; from then on it falls at a constant terminal velocity, vt = 2a²(ρ−σ)g / (9η), which grows with the square of the sphere's radius.","visual":"🌧️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Viscosity is best understood as...","options":["an internal friction between fluid layers moving with different velocities relative to each other","the weight of a fluid per unit volume","the pressure a fluid exerts on a container wall","the surface tension of a fluid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Viscosity arises from frictional forces between fluid layers sliding past each other at different velocities."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"As temperature rises, the coefficient of viscosity...","options":["decreases for liquids but increases for gases","increases for both liquids and gases","decreases for both liquids and gases","increases for liquids but decreases for gases"],"answer":0,"explain":"Warmer liquid molecules move more freely (lower η), while warmer gas molecules collide more (higher η)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A metal block of area 0.10 m² is dragged at a constant 0.085 m/s by a hanging 0.010 kg mass over a pulley (g = 9.8 m/s²), sliding on a liquid film of thickness 0.30 mm. What is the liquid's viscosity, in units of 10⁻³ Pa s (2 decimal places)?","answer":3.46,"explain":"F = mg = 0.098 N, shear stress = F/A = 0.98 Pa, strain rate = v/l = 0.085/(0.30×10⁻³) ≈ 283 s⁻¹, so η = stress/strain rate ≈ 3.46×10⁻³ Pa s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A copper sphere of radius 2.0 mm (density 8.9×10³ kg/m³) falls through oil (density 1.5×10³ kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s²) reaching terminal velocity 6.5 cm/s. Using vt = 2a²(ρ−σ)g/(9η), what is the oil's viscosity in Pa s (2 decimal places)?","answer":0.99,"explain":"η = 2a²(ρ−σ)g / (9vt) = 2(2×10⁻³)²(7.4×10³)(9.8) / (9×0.065) ≈ 0.99 Pa s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By Stokes' law, F = 6πηav, the viscous drag force on a falling sphere is...","options":["directly proportional to the fluid's viscosity, the sphere's radius, and its speed","independent of the fluid's viscosity","proportional to the square of the sphere's radius only","independent of the sphere's speed"],"answer":0,"explain":"F = 6πηav shows direct proportionality to η, a (radius), and v (speed), each to the first power."}]},{"id":"fl9","title":"Beading, Bubbles, and the Rising Tube","icon":"🫧","kind":"lesson","story":"A lotus leaf sheds water in perfect beads while a soap film stretches thin between two wires, and a thirsty plant pulls water up its stem against gravity itself. Uncover the one property - surface tension - hiding behind all three.","teach":[{"title":"Surface Energy","body":"Molecules at a liquid's surface have fewer neighbours than those fully inside it, so they carry extra potential energy compared with molecules in the bulk. Because increasing surface area costs energy, a liquid tends to take the shape with the least possible surface area for its volume - a sphere for a free drop or bubble.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Surface Tension","body":"Surface tension S is the force per unit length acting in the plane of a liquid's surface, equal to the surface energy per unit area of that interface. For water at 20°C it is about 0.0727 N/m, and like viscosity, it generally falls as temperature rises.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Angle of Contact","body":"The angle of contact between a liquid and a solid decides whether the liquid wets the surface (a small, acute angle, as water does on clean glass) or beads up on it (a large, obtuse angle, as water does on a lotus leaf, and mercury does on glass).","visual":"🪷"},{"title":"Excess Pressure and Capillary Rise","body":"Because a curved liquid surface pulls inward, the pressure inside a spherical drop exceeds the outside pressure by 2S/r; a bubble, having two surfaces, has double that excess, 4S/r. The same curvature effect drives water up a narrow capillary tube to a height h = 2S cosθ / (ρga).","visual":"🧪"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Free liquid drops and bubbles are spherical (when gravity can be neglected) because...","options":["a sphere has the least surface area for a given volume, minimising the liquid's extra surface energy","spheres are the only shape surface tension allows to exist","gravity always pulls liquids into spheres","air pressure is uniform only around spheres"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since extra surface area costs energy, a liquid naturally settles into the minimum-area shape for its volume - the sphere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Water forms beads on a lotus leaf (obtuse angle of contact) but spreads out on a clean glass plate (acute angle of contact). This difference means that, compared with the water-glass interface, the water-leaf interface has...","options":["a solid-liquid surface energy Ssl that is large enough (Ssl > Sla) that it costs too much energy for water to wet the leaf","no surface tension at all","exactly the same angle of contact as water-glass","a negative surface tension"],"answer":0,"explain":"An obtuse angle of contact occurs when Ssl > Sla, meaning wetting the solid costs more energy than staying beaded, as on a lotus leaf."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A drop and a soap bubble of the same liquid have the same radius r. The bubble's excess pressure (it has two liquid surfaces) is how many times the drop's excess pressure (which has only one surface)?","answer":2,"explain":"Excess pressure is 2S/r for a drop but 4S/r for a bubble (double, for its two surfaces), so the ratio is 2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A capillary tube of diameter 2.00 mm is dipped 8.00 cm below the surface of water (surface tension 7.30×10⁻² N/m, ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.80 m/s², atmospheric pressure 1.01×10⁵ Pa). What is the excess pressure (in Pa) needed to blow a hemispherical bubble at its end (radius = tube radius = 1.00 mm)?","answer":146,"explain":"Excess pressure = 2S/r = 2(7.30×10⁻²)/(1.00×10⁻³) = 146 Pa (added on top of the 8 cm water column's pressure to give total Pi ≈ 1.02×10⁵ Pa)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For a fine capillary of radius a = 0.05 cm dipped in water (S = 0.073 N/m, ρ = 1000 kg/m³, g = 9.8 m/s², cosθ ≈ 1), what capillary rise h (in cm, 2 decimal places) does h = 2S/(ρga) predict?","answer":2.98,"explain":"h = 2(0.073) / (1000 × 9.8 × 5×10⁻⁴) = 0.146/4.9 ≈ 0.0298 m = 2.98 cm."}]},{"id":"fl-boss","title":"Maelstrom, the Fluid Warden","icon":"🌀","kind":"boss","story":"The deep-sea rescue capsule you have been building must survive crushing depth, transmit force through its hydraulic arm, hold lift against water rushing past its hull, and seal every joint against water forcing its way through the smallest curved gap. Maelstrom, the Fluid Warden, guards the surface and will only let a fully-tested engineer through.","boss":{"name":"Maelstrom, the Fluid Warden","taunt":"Pressure, hydraulics, lift, viscosity, tension - name the law behind each depth, each drop, each gushing pipe, or be swept under."},"teach":[{"title":"One Fluid Story","body":"Pressure and Pascal's law, hydraulic machines, the equation of continuity, Bernoulli's principle, viscosity and Stokes' law, and surface tension are not separate topics - each follows from applying force balance (for fluids at rest) or energy conservation (for fluids in motion) to an element of fluid.","visual":"💧"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 50 kg girl balances on a single heel of diameter 1.0 cm (g = 9.8 m/s²). What pressure does she exert on the floor, in units of 10⁶ Pa (2 decimal places)?","answer":6.24,"explain":"F = 50×9.8 = 490 N, A = π(0.005)² ≈ 7.85×10⁻⁵ m², so P = F/A ≈ 6.24×10⁶ Pa."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Pascal duplicated Torricelli's mercury barometer (76 cm of mercury, specific gravity 13.6) using French wine of density 984 kg/m³. What height of wine column (in m, nearest tenth) balances normal atmospheric pressure?","answer":10.5,"explain":"Equal pressures require ρ_Hg g h_Hg = ρ_wine g h_wine, so h_wine = 0.76 × 13600/984 ≈ 10.5 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A hydraulic automobile lift must lift cars of mass up to 3000 kg using a piston of cross-sectional area 425 cm² (g = 9.8 m/s²). What maximum pressure (in units of 10⁵ Pa, 2 decimal places) must the smaller piston bear?","answer":6.92,"explain":"P = mg/A = (3000×9.8)/(425×10⁻⁴ m²) ≈ 6.92×10⁵ Pa."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Pascal's law, in the form used for hydraulic machines, states that a change in pressure applied to an enclosed fluid is transmitted...","options":["undiminished and equally to every point of the fluid and to the walls of the containing vessel","only to the point nearest where it was applied","with a time delay proportional to the distance travelled","only in the vertically downward direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"This undiminished, equal transmission in all directions is exactly what makes hydraulic lifts and brakes work."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In a wind-tunnel test on a model wing of area 2.5 m², air flows at 70 m/s over the top and 63 m/s underneath (density of air 1.3 kg/m³). Using Bernoulli's principle, what lift (in units of 10³ N, 2 decimal places) acts on the wing?","answer":1.51,"explain":"Lift = ½ρ(v2²−v1²)A = 0.5×1.3×(70²−63²)×2.5 ≈ 1.51×10³ N."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By Stokes' law, F = 6πηav, the viscous drag on a small sphere falling through a fluid is directed...","options":["opposite to the sphere's velocity, and grows in proportion to both its radius and its speed","in the same direction as the sphere's velocity","perpendicular to the sphere's velocity","independent of the sphere's velocity entirely"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stokes' drag always opposes the motion and scales linearly with both radius a and speed v."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A U-shaped wire dipped in soap solution supports a slider of length 30 cm against a weight of 1.5×10⁻² N via a thin soap film. What is the film liquid's surface tension, in units of 10⁻² N/m?","answer":2.5,"explain":"S = W/(2l) = 1.5×10⁻² / (2×0.30) = 2.5×10⁻² N/m (the factor 2 is for the film's two surfaces)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A drop of mercury has radius 3.00 mm at room temperature, where mercury's surface tension is 4.65×10⁻¹ N/m. What is the excess pressure inside the drop, in Pa?","answer":310,"explain":"Excess pressure = 2S/r = 2(4.65×10⁻¹)/(3.00×10⁻³) = 310 Pa."}]}]},{"id":"thermalproperties11","index":9,"name":"Thermal Properties of Matter","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 10","emoji":"🌡️","palette":{"from":"#f87171","to":"#7f1d1d"},"lessons":[{"id":"tp1","title":"Hot Tea, Cold Ice","icon":"☕","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Sunbaked Bazaar where a cup of hot tea is cooling on a table and a glass of ice-water is warming beside it. The old chaiwala tells you: master the difference between heat and temperature, or you'll never understand why either cup changes at all.","teach":[{"title":"Temperature vs Heat","body":"Temperature is a relative measure of hotness or coldness of a body. Heat is the form of energy that transfers between two systems (or a system and its surroundings) because of a temperature difference between them. The SI unit of heat energy is the joule (J); the SI unit of temperature is the kelvin (K), with degree Celsius (°C) commonly used too.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Which way does heat flow?","body":"Heat always flows from the hotter body or region to the colder one, until both reach the same temperature (thermal equilibrium). This is why a glass of ice-cold water left on a hot table warms up, while a cup of hot tea on the same table cools down — heat flows from the environment into the water, and from the tea into the environment.","visual":"↔️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of heat energy transferred?","options":["Joule (J)","Kelvin (K)","Celsius (°C)","Watt (W)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat is a form of energy, so its SI unit is the joule, same as any other energy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A cup of hot tea left on a table cools down over time. In which direction does heat flow?","options":["From the tea to the surrounding air","From the air into the tea","Heat does not flow; only temperature changes","From the table into the tea"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat flows from the hotter tea to the cooler surroundings until they reach the same temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What ultimately determines the direction in which heat flows between two bodies in contact?","options":["The difference in their temperatures","The difference in their masses","The difference in their colours","The difference in their volumes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat transfer takes place by virtue of a temperature difference between the system and its surroundings."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When an object is heated, which of these changes may take place in it?","options":["Its temperature may rise, it may expand, or it may change state","Only its temperature can ever change","Only its state can ever change","Nothing changes unless it melts"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text notes that on heating, an object's temperature may rise, it may expand, or it may change state."}]},{"id":"tp2","title":"Calibrating the Thermometer","icon":"🌡️","kind":"lesson","story":"The bazaar's instrument-maker hands you a broken liquid-in-glass thermometer and two scales scrawled on parchment: Celsius and Fahrenheit. To repair it, you must learn how thermometers are calibrated using two fixed reference points.","teach":[{"title":"Fixed points of a scale","body":"Constructing a temperature scale needs two fixed reference points. The ice point (freezing point of water) and steam point (boiling point of water) at standard pressure are used: 0°C and 100°C on the Celsius scale, 32°F and 212°F on the Fahrenheit scale. There are 100 equal intervals between the fixed points on the Celsius scale, and 180 on the Fahrenheit scale.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Converting between scales","body":"Because the two scales share the same straight-line relationship between fixed points, Fahrenheit temperature tF and Celsius temperature tC are related by tF = (9/5)tC + 32. A common liquid-in-glass thermometer works because the volume of a liquid like mercury or alcohol varies linearly with temperature.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using tF = (9/5)tC + 32, what is the steam point of water in °F when tC = 100°C?","answer":212,"explain":"tF = (9/5)(100) + 32 = 180 + 32 = 212°F, the steam point on the Fahrenheit scale."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the ice point of water in °F when tC = 0°C?","answer":32,"explain":"tF = (9/5)(0) + 32 = 32°F, the ice point on the Fahrenheit scale."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many equal intervals lie between the ice point and steam point on the Celsius scale?","options":["100","180","273","212"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Celsius scale divides the interval between ice point (0°C) and steam point (100°C) into 100 equal parts."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A healthy human body temperature is about 37°C. Using tF = (9/5)tC + 32, what is this in °F (to one decimal place)?","answer":98.6,"explain":"tF = (9/5)(37) + 32 = 66.6 + 32 = 98.6°F."}]},{"id":"tp3","title":"The Gas Thermometer's Secret","icon":"🎈","kind":"lesson","story":"A traveling scientist shows you a balloon that shrinks in cold water and swells in warm water. She challenges you: if liquid thermometers disagree with each other, what temperature scale could every gas agree on?","teach":[{"title":"Boyle's law and Charles' law","body":"At constant temperature, pressure and volume of a fixed quantity of gas obey PV = constant (Boyle's law, after Robert Boyle). At constant pressure, volume and temperature obey V/T = constant (Charles' law, after Jacques Charles). All low-density gases obey both laws.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"The ideal-gas equation","body":"Combining Boyle's and Charles' laws gives the ideal-gas equation PV = µRT, where µ is the number of moles and R is the universal gas constant, R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹. A constant-volume gas thermometer uses P ∝ T to read temperature from pressure.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"Absolute zero and the Kelvin scale","body":"Extrapolating a graph of pressure versus temperature for low-density gases to zero pressure always gives the same temperature: −273.15°C, called absolute zero. This is the zero of the Kelvin (absolute) scale, and the Kelvin unit is the same size as the Celsius degree: T = tC + 273.15.","visual":"❄️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Absolute zero on the Celsius scale is −273.15°C. What is this temperature in Kelvin?","answer":0,"explain":"Absolute zero is defined as 0 K, the origin of the Kelvin scale."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using T = tC + 273.15, what is 27°C in Kelvin (rounded to the nearest whole number)?","answer":300,"explain":"T = 27 + 273.15 ≈ 300 K."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the value of the universal gas constant R in the ideal-gas equation PV = µRT?","options":["8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹","6.02 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹","9.81 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹","1.38 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹"],"answer":0,"explain":"The universal gas constant R = 8.31 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which relationship is known as Boyle's law?","options":["PV = constant, at constant temperature","V/T = constant, at constant pressure","PV = µRT for any gas","P/T = constant, at constant volume"],"answer":0,"explain":"Boyle's law states that at constant temperature, pressure and volume of a fixed amount of gas obey PV = constant."}]},{"id":"tp4","title":"The Blacksmith's Iron Ring","icon":"🔨","kind":"lesson","story":"The village blacksmith needs an iron ring fitted tightly onto the rim of a wooden cart wheel, but the ring is just a little too small. He knows that heating metal makes it expand — he needs you to calculate exactly how hot the ring must get.","teach":[{"title":"Linear, area and volume expansion","body":"Most substances expand on heating. The fractional change in length of a rod, Δl/l, is proportional to the temperature change ΔT, with constant of proportionality αl, the coefficient of linear expansion. Similarly, the coefficient of volume expansion αv relates ΔV/V to ΔT, and for a cube expanding equally in all directions, αv = 3αl. Copper expands about five times more than glass (pyrex) for the same temperature rise.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Water's anomalous expansion","body":"Unlike most substances, water contracts on heating between 0°C and 4°C, and expands above 4°C — so water has its maximum density at 4°C. This is why lakes and ponds freeze at the top first: surface water cooling below 4°C becomes less dense and stays on top, protecting life below from freezing solid.","visual":"🧊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 10.2: An iron ring of diameter 5.231 m at 27°C must expand to fit a wooden wheel rim of diameter 5.243 m. Using αl(steel) = 1.20×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹ and LT2 = LT1[1 + αl(T2−T1)], to what temperature (in °C) must the ring be heated?","answer":218,"explain":"5.243 = 5.231[1 + 1.20×10⁻⁵(T2−27)] solves to T2 ≈ 218°C."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the relation between the coefficient of volume expansion αv and the coefficient of linear expansion αl?","options":["αv = 3αl","αv = αl","αv = 2αl","αv = αl / 3"],"answer":0,"explain":"For a substance expanding equally in all directions, αv = 3αl."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At what temperature does water have its maximum density?","options":["4°C","0°C","100°C","−40°C"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water contracts on heating from 0°C to 4°C and expands above 4°C, so its density is maximum at 4°C."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"From Table 10.1 in the chapter (αl values ×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹: Aluminium 2.5, Brass 1.8, Iron 1.2, Copper 1.7, Silver 1.9, Gold 1.4, Glass 0.32, Lead 0.29), which material has the highest coefficient of linear expansion?","options":["Aluminium","Copper","Silver","Gold"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aluminium has αl = 2.5×10⁻⁵ K⁻¹, the highest value listed in Table 10.1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do lakes freeze at the top first instead of from the bottom up?","options":["Because water has maximum density at 4°C, so colder surface water below 4°C stays less dense and floats","Because ice is heavier than water","Because the bottom of a lake is always warmer than the sun","Because wind only cools the surface of the water"],"answer":0,"explain":"Once surface water cools below 4°C it becomes less dense and remains on top, where it freezes, protecting life below."}]},{"id":"tp5","title":"Water, the Great Coolant","icon":"💧","kind":"lesson","story":"Sailing past a coastline, you notice the sea breeze cools the shore in the day but reverses at night. A sailor explains that the secret lies in one number: how much heat it takes to warm water compared to land.","teach":[{"title":"Specific heat capacity","body":"The heat capacity S of a substance is S = ΔQ/ΔT, the heat needed to raise its temperature by ΔT. Specific heat capacity s = ΔQ/(mΔT) is heat capacity per unit mass, with SI unit J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. It depends on the nature of the substance — it is a property that determines the temperature rise for a given amount of absorbed heat.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Water's high specific heat capacity","body":"Water has the highest specific heat capacity (4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹) among common substances, much higher than copper (386.4) or aluminium (900.0). This is why water is used as a coolant in radiators and as a heater in hot water bags, why water warms up more slowly than land in summer, and why desert regions (with little water) heat up quickly by day and cool quickly at night.","visual":"🌊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these substances has the highest specific heat capacity?","options":["Water (4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)","Ice (2060 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)","Copper (386.4 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)","Aluminium (900.0 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water's specific heat capacity of 4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ is the highest among substances listed in the chapter's table."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the specific heat capacity of water, in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹?","answer":4186,"explain":"The chapter lists the specific heat capacity of water as 4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the specific heat capacity of copper, in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹?","answer":386.4,"explain":"The chapter lists the specific heat capacity of copper as 386.4 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does desert sand heat up quickly during the day and cool quickly at night, compared to a coastal area?","options":["Sand has a much lower specific heat capacity than water, so the same heat input changes its temperature more","Sand absorbs no heat at all","Water reflects all sunlight while sand does not","Desert air has no heat capacity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Because water has a much higher specific heat capacity than land/sand, land warms and cools faster for the same heat exchanged."}]},{"id":"tp6","title":"The Calorimeter's Verdict","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"A metalsmith hands you a mysterious aluminium sphere fresh from a boiling-water bath and challenges you to find its specific heat capacity using nothing but a copper calorimeter, some water, and a thermometer.","teach":[{"title":"The principle of calorimetry","body":"Calorimetry means measurement of heat. In an isolated system, when a hot body is brought into contact with a colder body, heat lost by the hot body equals heat gained by the colder body, provided no heat escapes to the surroundings. A calorimeter (a metallic vessel with a stirrer, insulated inside a wooden jacket) is the device used to measure heat this way.","visual":"🪣"},{"title":"Worked example: finding specific heat by calorimetry","body":"Example 10.3: a 0.047 kg aluminium sphere at 100°C is dropped into a 0.14 kg copper calorimeter containing 0.25 kg water at 20°C; the mixture settles at 23°C. Setting heat lost by aluminium equal to heat gained by water and calorimeter (using swater = 4.18×10³ J kg⁻¹K⁻¹ and scopper = 0.386×10³ J kg⁻¹K⁻¹) gives the specific heat capacity of aluminium as 0.911 kJ kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.","visual":"🧮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the core principle used in calorimetry to find an unknown specific heat capacity?","options":["Heat lost by the hotter body equals heat gained by the colder body","Heat is always destroyed when two bodies mix","Only the body with greater mass loses heat","Heat capacity is the same for all substances"],"answer":0,"explain":"In an isolated system, heat lost by the hot object equals heat gained by the cooler objects it contacts."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Example 10.3, the aluminium sphere starts at 100°C and the mixture settles at 23°C. What is ΔT (in °C) for the aluminium sphere?","answer":77,"explain":"ΔT = 100°C − 23°C = 77°C, as used in the worked example."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In Example 10.3, using heat lost by aluminium = heat gained by water and calorimeter, the computed specific heat capacity of aluminium is 0.911 kJ kg⁻¹ K⁻¹. What is this value in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹?","answer":911,"explain":"0.911 kJ kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ = 911 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, close to the standard tabulated value of 900.0 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹ for aluminium."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What material is a calorimeter's vessel and stirrer typically made from?","options":["Copper or aluminium","Wood","Rubber","Glass wool"],"answer":0,"explain":"The calorimeter consists of a metallic vessel and stirrer of the same material, like copper or aluminium, inside an insulating wooden jacket."}]},{"id":"tp7","title":"Ice, Water, Steam","icon":"🧊","kind":"lesson","story":"In the frozen caverns, you find a block of ice that refuses to warm up even as fire roars beneath it — its temperature holds steady while it slowly turns to water. The cavern spirit demands you explain where all that heat is going.","teach":[{"title":"Change of state and latent heat","body":"When a substance changes state (melting/freezing, boiling/vaporisation, or sublimation), its temperature stays constant even while heat keeps flowing in or out — the heat is used to change the state, not the temperature. The heat per unit mass needed for this change is the latent heat, L, defined by Q = mL. For water, the latent heat of fusion is Lf = 3.33×10⁵ J kg⁻¹ and the latent heat of vaporisation is Lv = 22.6×10⁵ J kg⁻¹.","visual":"❄️"},{"title":"Melting, boiling, and sublimation","body":"Melting point is the temperature at which solid and liquid coexist in equilibrium; boiling point is where liquid and vapour coexist. Sublimation is a direct solid-to-vapour change (e.g. dry ice, iodine) without passing through the liquid state. The triple point of water — where solid, liquid and vapour all coexist — is at exactly 273.16 K and 6.11×10⁻³ Pa.","visual":"♨️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The latent heat of fusion of water is Lf = 3.33×10⁵ J kg⁻¹. Expressed as a multiple of 10⁵ J kg⁻¹, what is this value?","answer":3.33,"explain":"Lf for water = 3.33×10⁵ J kg⁻¹, i.e. 3.33 in units of 10⁵ J kg⁻¹."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The latent heat of vaporisation of water is Lv = 22.6×10⁵ J kg⁻¹. Expressed as a multiple of 10⁵ J kg⁻¹, what is this value?","answer":22.6,"explain":"Lv for water = 22.6×10⁵ J kg⁻¹, i.e. 22.6 in units of 10⁵ J kg⁻¹."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is it called when a solid changes directly into vapour, without passing through the liquid state?","options":["Sublimation","Fusion","Vaporisation","Regelation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sublimation is the direct solid-to-vapour transition, seen in substances like dry ice (solid CO2) and iodine."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 10.5: heat is calculated to convert 3 kg of ice at −12°C entirely to steam at 100°C, summing the heat to warm the ice, melt it, heat the water, then vaporise it. The chapter finds the total as 9.1×10⁶ J. What is this value as a multiple of 10⁶ J?","answer":9.1,"explain":"Summing Q1+Q2+Q3+Q4 (75600 + 1005000 + 1255800 + 6768000 J) gives Q = 9.1×10⁶ J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the temperature of the triple point of water, in Kelvin?","answer":273.16,"explain":"The triple point of water — where solid, liquid and vapour coexist — is defined as exactly 273.16 K."}]},{"id":"tp8","title":"Furnace, Wind, and Sunlight","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in a forge, a steel rod glows at one end while your bare hand can barely hold the other; outside, a sea breeze shifts direction at dusk; above, the sun warms your face across empty space. Three journeys, three ways heat travels — learn them all.","teach":[{"title":"Conduction","body":"Conduction transfers heat between adjacent parts of a body due to a temperature difference, without any flow of matter. For a bar of length L, cross-section A, and ends at temperatures TC and TD, the heat current is H = KA(TC−TD)/L, where K is the thermal conductivity. Metals like silver (406 J s⁻¹m⁻¹K⁻¹) and copper (385) conduct well; gases like air (0.024) conduct poorly.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Convection","body":"Convection transfers heat by actual bulk motion of a fluid, and can be natural (driven by buoyancy, as hot less-dense fluid rises) or forced (driven by a pump, like the human circulatory system). Natural convection drives sea breezes: land heats faster than water by day, warm air over land rises and cool sea air flows in; at night the cycle reverses as land cools faster than water.","visual":"🌬️"},{"title":"Radiation and Stefan-Boltzmann law","body":"Radiation transfers heat as electromagnetic waves and needs no medium — this is how the Sun heats the Earth across empty space, travelling at the speed of light, 3×10⁸ m/s. Every body emits thermal radiation; black bodies absorb and emit it best. A perfect radiator emits at rate H = AσT⁴ (Stefan-Boltzmann law), with σ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ W m⁻²K⁻⁴; a real body with emissivity e emits H = AeσT⁴.","visual":"☀️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 10.6: a steel rod (15.0 cm long) and copper rod (10.0 cm long) are joined, with the steel's free end at 300°C and copper's free end at 0°C. The steel rod's cross-section area is twice the copper's. Using K(steel)=50.2 and K(copper)=385 J s⁻¹m⁻¹K⁻¹, the chapter finds the junction temperature T (in °C) to be:","answer":44.4,"explain":"Setting heat current equal through both rods, [50.2×2(300−T)]/15 = [385(T−0)]/10 gives T = 44.4°C."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which mode of heat transfer needs no material medium at all?","options":["Radiation","Conduction","Convection","Both conduction and convection"],"answer":0,"explain":"Radiation transfers energy via electromagnetic waves and can travel through vacuum, unlike conduction and convection."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the value of the Stefan-Boltzmann constant σ, in units of 10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴?","answer":5.67,"explain":"σ = 5.67×10⁻⁸ W m⁻² K⁻⁴ in SI units."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"During the day, why does a sea breeze blow from the sea towards the land?","options":["Land heats up faster than water, so warm air over land rises and cooler air from the sea flows in to replace it","The sea is always colder than the land at night and day alike","Water has a lower specific heat capacity than land","Radiation from the sun pushes air directly from sea to land"],"answer":0,"explain":"Land heats faster (lower specific heat capacity) than water; warm air rises over land, pulling in cooler air from the sea — a natural convection cycle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do metal cooking pots often have their bottoms blackened?","options":["Black surfaces absorb radiant heat from the fire better than lighter colours","Black paint prevents the pot from conducting heat","It is purely decorative and has no thermal effect","Black surfaces reflect heat back into the fire"],"answer":0,"explain":"Black bodies absorb (and emit) radiant energy better than bodies of lighter colours, so blackened pot bottoms absorb more heat from the flame."}]},{"id":"tp9","title":"The Cooling Curve","icon":"📉","kind":"lesson","story":"A pan of hot food is set aside to cool before a feast can begin, and everyone wants to know exactly when it will be ready. You realize the rate at which anything cools isn't constant — it depends on how much hotter it still is than the room.","teach":[{"title":"Newton's law of cooling","body":"Newton's law of cooling states that the rate of loss of heat, −dQ/dt, of a body is directly proportional to the difference in temperature ΔT between the body and its surroundings, for small temperature differences: −dQ/dt = kΔT, where k depends on the area and nature of the surface. This is why a hot body cools quickly at first, then more slowly as it approaches room temperature.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Worked example: timing the cooling","body":"Example 10.8: a pan cools from 94°C to 86°C in 2 minutes with room temperature 20°C — an average excess temperature of 70°C above the room, cooling 8°C in that time. To cool from 71°C to 69°C (average excess 50°C above the room), comparing rates via K gives a time of 0.7 minutes, i.e. 42 seconds.","visual":"⏱️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to Newton's law of cooling, the rate of loss of heat of a body is proportional to:","answer":0,"options":["The temperature difference between the body and its surroundings","The absolute temperature of the body only","The mass of the body only","The specific heat capacity of the surroundings"],"explain":"Newton's law of cooling: −dQ/dt = k(T2−T1), proportional to the excess temperature of the body over its surroundings."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Newton's law of cooling, as given in the chapter, is a valid approximation only for:","options":["Small temperature differences between body and surroundings","Any temperature difference, however large","Gases only, never liquids or solids","Bodies radiating in a perfect vacuum only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states the law holds good only for small differences of temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A graph of loge(T2−T1) against time t, used to verify Newton's law of cooling, has what shape?","options":["A straight line with negative slope","A straight line with positive slope","A parabola opening upward","A horizontal straight line"],"answer":0,"explain":"Integrating the cooling equation gives loge(T2−T1) = −Kt + c, a straight line with negative slope when plotted against t."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 10.8: a pan of hot food cools from 94°C to 86°C in 2 minutes with the room at 20°C. Using the same cooling constant, the chapter calculates the time (in seconds) to cool from 71°C to 69°C. What is that time?","answer":42,"explain":"Comparing (8°C/2min)/(70°C) with (2°C/time)/(50°C) gives time = 0.7 min = 42 s."}]},{"id":"tp-boss","title":"Duel with Pyrovex, the Furnace Warden","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the forge stands Pyrovex, Warden of the Furnace, wreathed in heat that has scorched every scale, thermometer and calorimeter you've carried this far. To pass, you must prove you understand heat itself — from a single degree of temperature to a raging fire's radiation.","boss":{"name":"Pyrovex, the Furnace Warden","taunt":"You've measured my heat with your little thermometers — now let's see if you can survive the whole furnace!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the SI unit of heat energy transferred between two bodies?","options":["Joule (J)","Kelvin (K)","Watt (W)","Pascal (Pa)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat is energy, so it is measured in joules, the SI unit of energy."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using T = tC + 273.15, what is 27°C in Kelvin (rounded to the nearest whole number)?","answer":300,"explain":"T = 27 + 273.15 ≈ 300 K."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the relation between the coefficient of volume expansion αv and coefficient of linear expansion αl for a solid?","options":["αv = 3αl","αv = αl","αv = 2αl","αv = αl²"],"answer":0,"explain":"For a solid expanding equally in all directions, αv = 3αl."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the specific heat capacity of water, in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹?","answer":4186,"explain":"Water's specific heat capacity, 4186 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, is the highest among common substances listed in the chapter."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 10.6: for the joined steel-copper rod system heated at one end to 300°C and cooled at the other to 0°C, the chapter calculates the junction temperature (in °C) as:","answer":44.4,"explain":"Solving the steady-state heat-current equation for the steel-copper junction gives T = 44.4°C."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The latent heat of vaporisation of water is Lv = 22.6×10⁵ J kg⁻¹. Expressed as a multiple of 10⁵ J kg⁻¹, what is this value?","answer":22.6,"explain":"Lv for water = 22.6×10⁵ J kg⁻¹, as given in the chapter's Table 10.5."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which mode of heat transfer allows the Sun to warm the Earth across empty space?","options":["Radiation","Conduction","Convection","Sublimation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Radiation, carried by electromagnetic waves, needs no medium and is how heat crosses the vacuum from the Sun to the Earth."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Example 10.8: a pan cools from 94°C to 86°C in 2 minutes at room temperature 20°C. By the same cooling law, the chapter finds the time (in seconds) to cool from 71°C to 69°C to be:","answer":42,"explain":"Comparing cooling rates at the two average excess temperatures (70°C and 50°C above room) gives time = 0.7 min = 42 s."}]}]},{"id":"thermodynamics11","index":10,"name":"Thermodynamics","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 11","emoji":"🔥","palette":{"from":"#fb7185","to":"#881337"},"lessons":[{"id":"th1","title":"The Caloric Ghost and the Zeroth Law","icon":"🌡️","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Cooling Chambers of the Atlas, where two ancient gas-filled vaults sit behind stone walls. An inscription warns: 'Only those who understand what truly flows between hot and cold may pass.' A ghostly caloric-fluid theory flickers on the wall, clearly outdated and waiting to mislead you.","teach":[{"title":"The Caloric Myth, Busted","body":"Before modern physics, heat was thought to be an invisible fluid called caloric that flowed from hot bodies to cold ones, like water finding its own level. In 1798, Benjamin Thompson (Count Rumford) showed that boring a brass cannon produced heat depending only on the work done by the horses turning the drill, not on the drill's sharpness. This proved heat is a form of energy, not a fluid.","visual":"👻"},{"title":"Adiabatic vs Diathermic Walls","body":"An adiabatic wall is an insulating wall that blocks the flow of heat, so any pressure and volume on one side can coexist with any values on the other. A diathermic wall conducts heat, so the two systems' pressures and volumes change spontaneously until they stop changing altogether — that final state is thermal equilibrium.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics","body":"If system A is separately in thermal equilibrium with system C, and system B is also in thermal equilibrium with C, then A and B are in thermal equilibrium with each other. This law (formulated by R.H. Fowler in 1931, after the First and Second Laws were already named) is what gives rise to the very concept of temperature.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which kind of wall does NOT allow heat to flow between two systems?","options":["Adiabatic wall","Diathermic wall","Conducting wall","Isothermal wall"],"answer":0,"explain":"An adiabatic wall is an insulating wall that blocks heat flow between systems."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Count Rumford's cannon-boring experiment showed the heat produced depended on:","options":["the work done by the horses turning the drill","the sharpness of the drill","the material of the cannon","the color of the cannon"],"answer":0,"explain":"The heat depended on work done, not drill sharpness — disproving the caloric fluid theory."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics leads directly to the concept of:","options":["temperature","entropy","internal energy","pressure"],"answer":0,"explain":"The quantity equal for two systems in thermal equilibrium is defined as temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Zeroth Law, if A and C are in thermal equilibrium, and B and C are also in thermal equilibrium, then:","options":["A and B are in thermal equilibrium with each other","A and B must have zero internal energy","A and B cannot ever interact","A must be hotter than B"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is precisely the statement of the Zeroth Law."}]},{"id":"th2","title":"Three Doors: Heat, Work, and Energy Within","icon":"⚙️","kind":"lesson","story":"A locked chamber has three levers labeled Heat, Work, and Internal Energy. Pulling the wrong lever collapses the floor — the guardian spirit insists you must know exactly which of these three is 'stored' in a system and which are merely energy passing through.","teach":[{"title":"Internal Energy, U","body":"Internal energy is the sum of the kinetic and potential energies of all the molecules of a system, excluding the bulk motion of the system as a whole — a fired bullet's kinetic energy isn't internal energy, but the disordered molecular motion is. Internal energy is a state variable: its value depends only on the system's current state, never on the path taken to reach it.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Two Ways to Change Internal Energy","body":"Heat is energy transfer caused by a temperature difference between a system and its surroundings. Work is energy transfer by other means, such as pushing a piston down. Both raise (or lower) the internal energy of a gas in a cylinder — heating it from a hotter body, or compressing it by doing work, both increase U.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Heat and Work Are NOT State Variables","body":"Heat and work are energy 'in transit', not properties a system possesses. It is meaningless to say 'a gas in a given state has a certain amount of heat', but perfectly meaningful to say 'a certain amount of heat was supplied to the system' or 'a certain amount of work was done by the system'.","visual":"⏱️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Internal energy of a gas is the sum of:","options":["the kinetic and potential energies of its molecules due to random motion","only the kinetic energy of the gas moving as a whole","only the potential energy between the container walls","the total heat ever supplied to it"],"answer":0,"explain":"Internal energy excludes the overall (bulk) motion of the system, including only disordered molecular energy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT a state variable?","options":["Heat supplied (Q)","Internal energy (U)","Pressure (P)","Temperature (T)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat is a mode of energy transfer, not a property the system possesses in a given state."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When a moving bullet is stopped by a block of wood, its kinetic energy is converted into:","options":["heat, raising the temperature of the bullet and the wood","internal energy of the gun","potential energy of the wood","nothing — the energy simply disappears"],"answer":0,"explain":"The bullet's ordered kinetic energy becomes disordered molecular motion — heat — in the bullet and wood."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement is meaningful in thermodynamics?","options":["A gas in a given state has a certain amount of internal energy","A gas in a given state has a certain amount of heat","A gas in a given state has a certain amount of work","A gas has a fixed amount of heat and work combined"],"answer":0,"explain":"Only internal energy is a state variable; heat and work are not properties of a state."}]},{"id":"th3","title":"The Energy Ledger Vault","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"In the Vaporization Vault, you find 1 gram of water about to boil away. A ledger on the wall demands you balance the books: how much of the heat becomes useful work, and how much stays behind as internal energy?","teach":[{"title":"The First Law: ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW","body":"Heat supplied to a system (ΔQ) either raises its internal energy (ΔU) or leaves as work done by the system on its surroundings (ΔW), or both. This is simply the general principle of conservation of energy applied to a thermodynamic system.","visual":"📒"},{"title":"Work Done by an Expanding Gas","body":"When a gas in a cylinder pushes a piston against a constant external pressure P, the work it does is ΔW = PΔV, where ΔV is the change in volume.","visual":"🛠️"},{"title":"Worked Example: Water Turning to Steam","body":"For 1 g of water becoming vapour at atmospheric pressure, the latent heat supplies ΔQ = 2256 J. The volume grows from 1 cm³ (liquid) to 1671 cm³ (vapour), so the gas does work ΔW = P(Vg−Vl) = 1.013×10⁵ × 1671×10⁻⁶ ≈ 169.2 J. By the First Law, ΔU = ΔQ − ΔW = 2256 − 169.2 = 2086.8 J — most of the heat goes into raising internal energy, not doing external work.","visual":"💨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1 g of water absorbs ΔQ = 2256 J of heat as it turns entirely to vapour at atmospheric pressure, doing ΔW = 169.2 J of work on the surroundings as it expands. What is the increase in internal energy ΔU (in J)?","answer":2086.8,"explain":"ΔU = ΔQ − ΔW = 2256 − 169.2 = 2086.8 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The First Law of Thermodynamics, ΔQ = ΔU + ΔW, is really just:","options":["the law of conservation of energy applied to a thermodynamic system","Newton's third law applied to gases","a special case of the Zeroth law","valid only for adiabatic processes"],"answer":0,"explain":"The First Law is energy conservation, accounting for heat and work exchanged with the surroundings."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electric heater supplies heat to a system at a rate of 100 W, while the system does work at a rate of 75 J/s. At what rate (in W) is the internal energy of the system increasing?","answer":25,"explain":"Rate of ΔU = rate of ΔQ − rate of ΔW = 100 − 75 = 25 W."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Work done by a gas expanding against a constant external pressure P, with volume change ΔV, is given by:","options":["ΔW = PΔV","ΔW = VΔP","ΔW = ΔU + ΔQ","ΔW = PΔT"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since force is pressure × area and area × displacement is volume, ΔW = PΔV."}]},{"id":"th4","title":"The Coolant Chamber","icon":"💧","kind":"lesson","story":"A reactor at the heart of the Atlas is overheating, and you must choose the right coolant and understand a geyser's fuel consumption before the chamber floods. Only substances with the right specific heat capacity will save the day.","teach":[{"title":"Heat Capacity and Specific Heat","body":"Heat capacity S = ΔQ/ΔT is the heat needed per unit rise in temperature. Dividing by mass m gives the specific heat capacity s = (1/m)(ΔQ/ΔT), a property of the substance alone, independent of how much of it you have, measured in J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Molar Specific Heat and the Law of Equipartition","body":"Per mole, the molar specific heat capacity is C = (1/μ)(ΔQ/ΔT). For solids, the law of equipartition of energy predicts C = 3R (about 24.9 J mol⁻¹K⁻¹), which agrees well with experiment at ordinary temperatures, though it breaks down at low temperatures (and carbon is an exception).","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Cp − Cv = R for an Ideal Gas","body":"A gas heated at constant pressure expands and does extra work PΔV, so its molar specific heat at constant pressure Cp exceeds its specific heat at constant volume Cv. For an ideal gas the exact relation is Cp − Cv = R, where R is the universal gas constant.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Water's Unusually High Specific Heat","body":"Water has a specific heat capacity of 4186 J kg⁻¹K⁻¹ (4.186 J g⁻¹K⁻¹). One calorie, the old unit of heat, is defined as the heat needed to raise the temperature of 1 g of water from 14.5°C to 15.5°C, and 1 cal = 4.186 J. Water's high specific heat is why it makes a good coolant and why coastal towns have milder climates than deserts at the same latitude.","visual":"🌊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A geyser heats water flowing at 3.0 litres (3000 g) per minute from 27°C to 77°C, using a gas burner whose heat of combustion is 4.0×10⁴ J/g. Using water's specific heat of 4.186 J/g/K, roughly how many grams of fuel are consumed per minute? (round to 1 decimal)","answer":15.7,"explain":"Heat needed = 3000 × 4.186 × 50 = 627900 J/min; fuel = 627900 / 40000 ≈ 15.7 g/min."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For solids, the law of equipartition of energy predicts the molar specific heat capacity C to be:","options":["3R","R","R/2","5R/2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Each atom vibrating in 3 dimensions has average energy 3kBT, giving C = 3R per mole."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For an ideal gas, the molar specific heats at constant pressure and volume satisfy:","options":["Cp − Cv = R","Cp + Cv = R","Cp × Cv = R","Cp = Cv"],"answer":0,"explain":"This follows from the ideal gas equation and the First Law of Thermodynamics."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One calorie is defined as the heat needed to raise the temperature of 1 g of water:","options":["from 14.5°C to 15.5°C","from 0°C to 1°C","from 99°C to 100°C","by exactly 10°C"],"answer":0,"explain":"This precise interval was chosen because specific heat varies slightly with temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A harbour town has a milder climate than a desert town at the same latitude mainly because:","options":["nearby sea water has a high specific heat capacity, moderating temperature swings","the harbour town gets less sunlight","desert sand has a lower density","wind never blows near deserts"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water absorbs or releases large amounts of heat with only small temperature changes, moderating the coastal climate."}]},{"id":"th5","title":"The Vault of Variables","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"The next chamber splits itself perfectly in half before your eyes. A riddle appears: 'Some of my properties halved, some stayed the same. Name which is which, and know the equation that binds me, to pass.'","teach":[{"title":"State Variables and Equilibrium","body":"A thermodynamic system's equilibrium state is completely described by macroscopic state variables such as pressure, volume, temperature, and mass. These variables are only well-defined when the system IS in equilibrium — during a gas's free expansion into vacuum, or an explosive chemical reaction, pressure and temperature aren't uniform until the system settles down.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"The Equation of State","body":"The relation connecting a system's state variables is called its equation of state. For an ideal gas, this is PV = μRT (μ = number of moles). For a fixed amount of gas, only two of P, V, T are independent — the pressure-volume curve at fixed temperature is called an isotherm.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Extensive vs Intensive Variables","body":"Imagine splitting an equilibrium system into two equal halves. Extensive variables — like internal energy U, volume V, and mass M — get halved in each part. Intensive variables — like pressure P, temperature T, and density ρ — stay unchanged. In ΔQ = ΔU + PΔV, both sides are extensive, since an intensive variable (P) times an extensive one (ΔV) is extensive.","visual":"✂️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If you split an equilibrium system into two identical halves, which of these variables gets halved?","options":["Volume V","Pressure P","Temperature T","Density ρ"],"answer":0,"explain":"Volume is extensive — it scales with the size (amount) of the system."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an intensive variable?","options":["Temperature","Internal energy","Volume","Mass"],"answer":0,"explain":"Temperature does not change when a system in equilibrium is split into parts, so it is intensive."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The ideal gas equation of state is:","options":["PV = μRT","PV = μR/T","P = μRTV","PV² = μRT"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the standard ideal gas relation connecting pressure, volume, moles, and temperature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A gas expanding freely into a vacuum is NOT in equilibrium during the expansion because:","options":["its pressure is not uniform throughout during the rapid expansion","its temperature instantly becomes zero","volume is undefined for gases","the ideal gas equation stops existing"],"answer":0,"explain":"During rapid free expansion, pressure and temperature are not well-defined until the gas settles."}]},{"id":"th6","title":"The Four Process Gates","icon":"🌀","kind":"lesson","story":"Four gates stand before you, each glowing with a different symbol: constant temperature, zero heat flow, constant volume, constant pressure. Each gate demands the correct formula for the work it permits, and a final loop gate tests whether you understand what happens when you return exactly where you started.","teach":[{"title":"The Quasi-static Process","body":"A quasi-static process is an idealized, infinitely slow process in which the system remains in thermal and mechanical equilibrium with its surroundings at every stage, differing only infinitesimally in pressure and temperature. Real processes only approximate this when they are slow and gentle.","visual":"🐢"},{"title":"Isothermal Process","body":"Temperature stays fixed, so for an ideal gas PV = constant (Boyle's Law). Since internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on T, ΔU = 0, and the First Law gives Q = W: heat absorbed equals work done. Work done in an isothermal expansion from V1 to V2 at temperature T is W = μRT ln(V2/V1).","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Adiabatic Process","body":"No heat is exchanged with the surroundings (ΔQ = 0), since the system is insulated. For an ideal gas, PVᵧ = constant, where γ = Cp/Cv. Work done by the gas in an adiabatic change from (P1,V1,T1) to (P2,V2,T2) is W = μR(T1−T2)/(γ−1); if the gas does positive work, its temperature falls.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Isochoric, Isobaric and Cyclic Processes","body":"Isochoric: volume is constant, so no work is done (W=0) and all supplied heat raises internal energy. Isobaric: pressure is constant, so W = PΔV = μR(T2−T1). Cyclic: the system returns to its initial state, so ΔU = 0 over the whole cycle, meaning total heat absorbed equals total work done by the system.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an isothermal process for an ideal gas, the internal energy change ΔU is:","options":["zero, since U depends only on temperature","always positive","always negative","equal to PΔV"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since T is fixed and U of an ideal gas depends only on T, ΔU = 0 in an isothermal process."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"3 moles of hydrogen gas (γ = 1.4) at STP are compressed adiabatically to half their original volume. Using P1V1^γ = P2V2^γ, by what factor does the pressure increase? (i.e. compute 2^1.4, round to 2 decimals)","answer":2.64,"explain":"P2/P1 = (V1/V2)^γ = 2^1.4 ≈ 2.64."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an adiabatic process:","options":["no heat is exchanged with the surroundings","temperature is always constant","pressure is always constant","volume is always constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"Adiabatic means the system is thermally insulated, so ΔQ = 0."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an isochoric process, since volume doesn't change:","options":["no work is done, and all supplied heat increases internal energy","all supplied heat is converted to work","pressure remains constant","temperature cannot change"],"answer":0,"explain":"With ΔV = 0, W = PΔV = 0, so by the First Law all heat goes into ΔU."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A gas is changed adiabatically from state A to state B, with 22.3 J of work done ON the system (so its internal energy rises by 22.3 J, since ΔQ=0). If instead the gas goes from A to B by a different path where the net heat absorbed is 9.35 cal (1 cal = 4.19 J), what is the net work done BY the system in this second path (in J, round to 1 decimal)?","answer":16.9,"explain":"ΔU = 22.3 J (same for both paths, since U is a state variable). ΔQ = 9.35 × 4.19 ≈ 39.2 J. So ΔW = ΔQ − ΔU ≈ 39.2 − 22.3 = 16.9 J."}]},{"id":"th7","title":"The Perpetual Motion Salesman","icon":"🚫","kind":"lesson","story":"A smooth-talking merchant offers to sell you a machine that converts heat entirely into work forever, with nothing else changing. Something about this deal feels impossible — and it is. You'll need the Second Law to see through the scam.","teach":[{"title":"Why We Need a Second Law","body":"The First Law (energy conservation) alone does not forbid absurd things — like a table spontaneously cooling itself and converting that energy to fling a book into the air. Such things are never observed in nature, so some additional principle beyond energy conservation must be forbidding them: the Second Law of Thermodynamics.","visual":"📖"},{"title":"The Kelvin-Planck Statement","body":"No process is possible whose sole result is the absorption of heat from a reservoir and the complete conversion of that heat into work. In simple terms, no heat engine can have an efficiency of exactly 1 (100%).","visual":"⛔"},{"title":"The Clausius Statement","body":"No process is possible whose sole result is the transfer of heat from a colder object to a hotter object. Equivalently, no refrigerator can have an infinite coefficient of performance. The Kelvin-Planck and Clausius statements are completely equivalent to one another.","visual":"❄️"},{"title":"Reversible vs Irreversible Processes","body":"A process is reversible only if it is quasi-static AND free of dissipative effects like friction or viscosity. Nearly all real processes — free expansion, explosive chemical reactions, gas leaking and diffusing through a room, stirring a liquid — are irreversible. Irreversibility is the rule, not the exception, in nature.","visual":"♻️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Kelvin-Planck statement of the Second Law says no engine can:","options":["convert absorbed heat completely into work, with no other effect","ever do any work at all","have an efficiency greater than zero","run without fuel"],"answer":0,"explain":"This rules out a perfect, 100%-efficient heat engine."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Clausius statement of the Second Law forbids:","options":["heat flowing spontaneously from a colder to a hotter object with no other effect","heat ever flowing from hot to cold","any refrigerator from working at all","doing work on a gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"A refrigerator can move heat from cold to hot, but only by having external work done on it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A process is reversible only if it is:","options":["quasi-static and free of dissipative effects like friction","very fast and violent","adiabatic only","isothermal only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both conditions — quasi-static equilibrium and no dissipation — are required for reversibility."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of an irreversible process mentioned in the chapter?","options":["Cooking gas leaking from a cylinder and diffusing through a room","A frictionless piston moving infinitely slowly","An idealised reversible Carnot cycle","Two systems already in thermal equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Diffusion of leaking gas never spontaneously reverses itself — a classic irreversible process."}]},{"id":"th8","title":"The Legendary Carnot Engine","icon":"🚂","kind":"lesson","story":"At last, the final gate: a majestic engine straddling two reservoirs, one blazing hot, one icy cold. Sadi Carnot's ghost challenges you to operate the most efficient engine physics allows — no more, no less — before the boss chamber opens.","teach":[{"title":"The Carnot Cycle","body":"A Carnot engine is a reversible heat engine operating between two temperatures, T1 (hot) and T2 (cold). Its cycle has four reversible steps: isothermal expansion absorbing heat Q1 at T1, adiabatic expansion cooling the gas from T1 to T2, isothermal compression releasing heat Q2 at T2, and adiabatic compression back to T1.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Carnot Efficiency","body":"The efficiency of a Carnot engine is η = W/Q1 = 1 − T2/T1. This is the maximum possible efficiency for any heat engine operating between temperatures T1 and T2 — no real engine between the same two reservoirs can beat it, and this efficiency does not depend on the working substance used.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"The Universal Ratio Q1/Q2 = T1/T2","body":"This relation holds for any Carnot engine regardless of the working substance, since the Carnot efficiency is universal. It lets physicists define a truly universal thermodynamic temperature scale based purely on heat exchanged, independent of the properties of any particular material.","visual":"♾️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A Carnot engine operates between a hot reservoir at 600 K and a cold reservoir at 300 K. What is its efficiency (as a percentage)?","answer":50,"explain":"η = 1 − T2/T1 = 1 − 300/600 = 0.5 = 50%."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Carnot cycle consists of:","options":["two isothermal processes connected by two adiabatic processes","four isobaric processes","two isochoric and two isothermal processes","one long adiabatic expansion only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Carnot cycle alternates isothermal heat exchange with adiabatic temperature changes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The efficiency of a Carnot engine depends on:","options":["only the temperatures of the hot and cold reservoirs","the working substance used","the size of the engine","the pressure at which it operates"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carnot's theorem shows efficiency depends only on T1 and T2, not on the substance or engine size."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A Carnot engine absorbs Q1 = 1000 J of heat from a hot reservoir at 500 K and releases heat to a cold reservoir at 300 K. Using Q1/Q2 = T1/T2, how much heat Q2 (in J) is released to the cold reservoir?","answer":600,"explain":"Q2 = Q1 × (T2/T1) = 1000 × (300/500) = 600 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"No heat engine working between two given temperatures can be more efficient than:","options":["a Carnot engine operating between the same two temperatures","an isochoric engine","a free-expansion engine","any irreversible engine"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carnot's theorem: the reversible Carnot engine sets the maximum possible efficiency."}]},{"id":"th-boss","title":"Boss: Perpetuum, the Caloric Wraith","icon":"👻","kind":"boss","story":"The caloric ghost from the very first chamber has grown into a full wraith, powered by an engine it claims converts heat entirely into work, forever, with no other effect. It cackles as gears spin behind it. Only a full mastery of thermodynamics can unmake this impossible machine.","boss":{"name":"Perpetuum, the Caloric Wraith","taunt":"Foolish traveler — I have built an engine that converts heat completely into work, forever! Your so-called 'laws' are mere suggestions to a creature like me!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything You've Learned","body":"From the Zeroth Law giving us temperature, through the First Law's energy bookkeeping, specific heats, state variables, the four thermodynamic processes, to the Second Law and the Carnot engine — Perpetuum's claim of a perfect, 100%-efficient engine violates the Kelvin-Planck statement itself. No such machine can exist.","visual":"🔥"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Perpetuum claims his engine converts heat completely into work with no other effect. Which law dooms this claim?","options":["The Kelvin-Planck statement of the Second Law of Thermodynamics","The Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics","Newton's First Law","The ideal gas equation"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Kelvin-Planck statement explicitly forbids a process whose sole result is 100% conversion of heat into work."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"\"Two systems in thermal equilibrium with a third system separately are in thermal equilibrium with each other.\" This is the:","options":["Zeroth Law of Thermodynamics","First Law of Thermodynamics","Second Law of Thermodynamics","Carnot theorem"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the exact statement of the Zeroth Law, which gives rise to the concept of temperature."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"1 g of water absorbs ΔQ = 2256 J of heat converting fully to vapour, doing ΔW = 169.2 J of work as it expands. What is ΔU (in J)?","answer":2086.8,"explain":"ΔU = ΔQ − ΔW = 2256 − 169.2 = 2086.8 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following is NOT a state variable?","options":["Heat (Q)","Pressure (P)","Volume (V)","Internal energy (U)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Heat is a mode of energy transfer, not a property possessed by a system in a given state."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A Carnot engine works between a hot reservoir at 400 K and a cold reservoir at 300 K. What is its efficiency (as a percentage)?","answer":25,"explain":"η = 1 − T2/T1 = 1 − 300/400 = 0.25 = 25%."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Water's exceptionally high specific heat capacity (4186 J/kg/K) explains why:","options":["coastal towns have a milder climate than deserts at the same latitude","water boils at exactly 100°C","ice floats on water","water expands when it freezes"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water absorbs/releases large amounts of heat with small temperature change, moderating coastal climates."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In an isochoric process, work done by the gas is:","options":["zero, because volume doesn't change","maximum possible","equal to PΔV with ΔV large","equal to μRT ln(V2/V1)"],"answer":0,"explain":"With no volume change, W = PΔV = 0."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An electric heater supplies 100 W of heat to a system while the system does work at 75 J/s. At what rate (in W) does internal energy increase?","answer":25,"explain":"Rate of ΔU = rate of ΔQ − rate of ΔW = 100 − 75 = 25 W."}]}]},{"id":"kinetictheory11","index":11,"name":"Kinetic Theory","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 12","emoji":"💨","palette":{"from":"#38bdf8","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"kt1","title":"The Atomist's Ruins","icon":"⚛️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep beneath a cracked temple floor you find carvings of Kanada and Democritus arguing that all matter is grains too small to see. A stone door will only open once you accept — and prove — the Atomic Hypothesis.","teach":[{"title":"The Atomic Hypothesis","body":"Feynman called it the most significant scientific idea: all things are made of atoms — tiny particles in perpetual motion, attracting each other at a little distance apart but repelling when squeezed together. Kanada in India and Democritus in Greece guessed this over two thousand years ago.","visual":"⚛️"},{"title":"How close are atoms?","body":"An atom is about an angstrom (10^-10 m) across. In solids and liquids, atoms sit only about 2 angstroms apart, so interatomic forces matter. In gases the spacing swells to tens of angstroms, so molecules barely interact except when they collide.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Weighing a water molecule","body":"Liquid water has density 1000 kg/m3, but its vapour at 100C and 1 atm has density only 0.6 kg/m3. Since 1 mole (0.018 kg) of water holds 6x10^23 molecules, one molecule has mass 3x10^-26 kg, giving a volume of 3x10^-29 m3 and radius about 2 angstrom.","visual":"💧"},{"title":"Room to roam","body":"Because vapour has 1000/0.6 = 1667 times the volume of the same mass of liquid, each molecule's share of space grows by that factor, so the radius of that space grows by its cube root (about 10x) — pushing the average interatomic distance in vapour out to roughly 40 angstrom.","visual":"🌫️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Atomic Hypothesis (as Feynman restated it), atoms are particles that...","options":["move around in perpetual motion, attracting each other a little distance apart but repelling when squeezed together","sit frozen in fixed positions unless heated","only exist inside solids, never in liquids or gases","are infinitely divisible into smaller pieces"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the Atomic Hypothesis as stated in the text: perpetual motion, attraction at a distance, repulsion on squeezing."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The density of water is 1000 kg/m3 and of its vapour at 100C, 1 atm is 0.6 kg/m3. How many times larger is the volume of the vapour than the same mass of liquid water?","answer":1667,"explain":"1000/0.6 ≈ 1667, so the vapour occupies about 1667 times the volume of the same mass of liquid water."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the water density and Avogadro's number (6x10^23 per mole, molar mass 18 g), the estimated radius of a single water molecule works out to about how many angstrom?","answer":2,"explain":"The molecular volume works out to 3x10^-29 m3, giving a radius of about 2x10^-10 m = 2 angstrom."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In gases, the interatomic distances compared to solids/liquids are...","options":["tens of angstroms — much larger, since gas molecules are far apart","the same, about 2 angstroms","smaller, since gas molecules pack tighter","impossible to estimate"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text states interatomic distances in gases are in tens of angstroms, versus about 2 angstrom in solids and liquids."}]},{"id":"kt2","title":"The Pressure Vault","icon":"🎈","kind":"lesson","story":"A vault door in the ruins is sealed by a gas-filled chamber. The inscription reads: squeeze the volume, the pressure rises — but only if you keep it warm. You must master the gas laws to balance the chamber and step through.","teach":[{"title":"Boyle's Law","body":"For a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, PV = constant. Squeeze the volume down and the pressure climbs to match; this is Boyle's law, first found in 1661 and later explained by kinetic theory.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"The Ideal Gas Equation","body":"For mu moles of gas, PV = mu R T, where R = N_A k_B = 8.314 J mol^-1 K^-1 is the universal gas constant and k_B = 1.38x10^-23 J/K is the Boltzmann constant. A gas that obeys this exactly at all pressures and temperatures is called an ideal gas.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Avogadro's Hypothesis","body":"Equal volumes of all gases at the same temperature and pressure contain the same number of molecules. At standard temperature and pressure (273 K, 1 atm), 22.4 litres of any gas contains 6.02x10^23 molecules — the Avogadro number, N_A.","visual":"🔢"},{"title":"Dalton's Law of Partial Pressures","body":"In a mixture of non-reacting ideal gases, the total pressure is the sum of the partial pressures each gas would exert alone. For neon and oxygen with partial pressures in ratio 3:2, the number of molecules N1:N2 is also 3:2, since P is proportional to mu, which is proportional to N.","visual":"🎈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Boyle's law states that for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature...","options":["pressure varies inversely with volume (PV = constant)","pressure varies directly with volume","volume is proportional to temperature","pressure is independent of volume"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fixing mu and T in PV = muRT gives PV = constant, which is Boyle's law."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the value of the universal gas constant R, in J mol^-1 K^-1?","answer":8.314,"explain":"R = N_A k_B = 8.314 J mol^-1 K^-1, as given in the chapter."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At STP, 22.4 litres of any ideal gas contains how many molecules, expressed in units of 10^23?","answer":6.02,"explain":"This is the Avogadro number, N_A = 6.02x10^23."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A vessel holds neon and oxygen whose partial pressures are in the ratio 3:2. What is the ratio of the number of neon molecules to oxygen molecules, N1/N2?","answer":1.5,"explain":"Since P is proportional to mu which is proportional to N, N1/N2 = P1/P2 = 3/2 = 1.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using atomic mass of Ne = 20.2 u and molecular mass of O2 = 32.0 u with N1:N2 = 3:2, what is the mass-density ratio rho_Ne / rho_O2 (to 3 decimal places)?","answer":0.947,"explain":"rho1/rho2 = (N1/N2)(M1/M2) = (3/2)(20.2/32.0) = 0.947, as worked out in Example 12.4."}]},{"id":"kt3","title":"Bombardment of the Wall","icon":"🎱","kind":"lesson","story":"Inside the vault, countless invisible spheres hammer against a shimmering barrier. To calculate its breaking pressure, you must track a single molecule's elastic collision and add up every impact.","teach":[{"title":"Elastic collisions with the wall","body":"A molecule of mass m with velocity (vx, vy, vz) strikes a wall and rebounds elastically with velocity (-vx, vy, vz): only the x-component reverses. Its momentum changes by -2mvx, so it delivers momentum 2mvx to the wall.","visual":"🎱"},{"title":"Counting the impacts","body":"In time delta-t, all molecules with x-velocity vx within a distance vx*delta-t of the wall can strike it; about half are moving toward the wall. Summing the momentum delivered by all such molecules and dividing by area and time gives the pressure P = n m <vx^2>.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"From one direction to all","body":"Since the gas is isotropic, <vx^2> = <vy^2> = <vz^2> = (1/3)<v^2>. Substituting gives the central kinetic-theory result: P = (1/3) n m <v^2>, where n is number density and <v^2> is the mean of the squared speed.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Shape doesn't matter","body":"Although the derivation used a cube, neither the wall area A nor the time interval delta-t survives in the final formula — so the result holds for a container of any shape, consistent with Pascal's law that pressure is the same everywhere in a fluid in equilibrium.","visual":"📐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A molecule of mass m and x-velocity vx rebounds elastically off a wall with velocity -vx. What momentum does it deliver to the wall?","options":["2mvx","mvx","0","4mvx"],"answer":0,"explain":"The molecule's own momentum changes by -2mvx, so by conservation of momentum it transfers +2mvx to the wall."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Kinetic theory gives the pressure of an ideal gas as...","options":["P = (1/3) n m <v^2>","P = n m <v^2>","P = (2/3) n m <v>","P = n m <v>^2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Combining the wall-collision result with isotropy (<vx^2> = (1/3)<v^2>) gives P = (1/3) n m <v^2>."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the shape of the container not matter in the kinetic-theory derivation of pressure?","options":["Because the wall area A and time interval delta-t both cancel out of the final result","Because gases never touch curved walls","Because pressure only exists at flat walls","Because molecules stop moving near curved surfaces"],"answer":0,"explain":"A and delta-t drop out of P = (1/3) n m <v^2>, so the same pressure applies to any small patch of any container, matching Pascal's law."}]},{"id":"kt4","title":"The Speed of Heat","icon":"🌡️","kind":"lesson","story":"A thermal glyph only responds to the true meaning of temperature — not touch, not fire, but the average scramble-speed of invisible molecules. Calibrate it correctly or the passage stays frozen shut.","teach":[{"title":"Temperature is molecular motion","body":"Combining P = (1/3)nm<v^2> with PV = mu RT gives E/N = (1/2)m<v^2> = (3/2) k_B T: the average kinetic energy of a molecule is proportional to absolute temperature alone, independent of pressure, volume, or the gas's identity.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Root mean square speed","body":"The square root of <v^2> is called the rms speed, v_rms = sqrt(3 k_B T / m). For nitrogen gas at T = 300 K, v_rms works out to 516 m/s — comparable to the speed of sound in air.","visual":"💨"},{"title":"Lighter means faster","body":"Since (1/2)m*v_rms^2 = (3/2)k_B T for every ideal gas, argon and chlorine at the same temperature have equal average kinetic energy per molecule (ratio 1:1), but v_rms(Ar)/v_rms(Cl2) = sqrt(M_Cl2/M_Ar) = sqrt(70.9/39.9) = 1.33 — the lighter argon atoms move faster.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Isotope separation","body":"In uranium hexafluoride, 235UF6 (mass 349 u) and 238UF6 (mass 352 u) differ in speed by only sqrt(352/349) = 1.0044, a 0.44% difference — yet this tiny gap is enough to enrich uranium using porous-membrane diffusion.","visual":"☢️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The average kinetic energy of a molecule in an ideal gas, (3/2)k_B T, depends on...","options":["only the absolute temperature of the gas","the pressure of the gas","the volume of the container","the chemical identity of the gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The kinetic interpretation of temperature shows average molecular KE depends only on T, not on P, V, or the type of gas."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the root-mean-square speed (in m/s) of a nitrogen molecule at T = 300 K, as computed in the chapter?","answer":516,"explain":"v_rms = sqrt(3k_BT/m) works out to 516 m/s for nitrogen at 300 K."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Argon (39.9 u) and chlorine (70.9 u) are mixed at the same temperature. What is the ratio v_rms(Ar)/v_rms(Cl2)?","answer":1.33,"explain":"v_rms(Ar)/v_rms(Cl2) = sqrt(M_Cl2/M_Ar) = sqrt(70.9/39.9) = 1.33, as found in Example 12.5."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"235UF6 (349 u) and 238UF6 (352 u) diffuse at slightly different speeds. What is the percentage difference in their speeds?","answer":0.44,"explain":"v349/v352 = sqrt(352/349) = 1.0044, a 0.44% difference, as found in Example 12.6."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the same temperature, the average kinetic energy per molecule of argon compared to chlorine is...","options":["exactly equal, in the ratio 1:1","twice as large for argon","half as large for argon","impossible to compare"],"answer":0,"explain":"Average KE per molecule depends only on T, so it is 1:1 for argon and chlorine at the same temperature, even though their rms speeds differ."}]},{"id":"kt5","title":"The Equal Engine","icon":"🎯","kind":"lesson","story":"An ancient dumbbell-shaped engine only runs when energy is shared out fairly among every way it can move — sliding, spinning, and vibrating. Balance its chambers to restart it.","teach":[{"title":"Degrees of freedom","body":"A molecule free to move in space needs three coordinates to locate it, so it has three translational degrees of freedom. A rigid diatomic molecule (like O2) can also rotate about two independent axes normal to the bond, adding two rotational degrees of freedom — five in total.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"The law of equipartition of energy","body":"First proved by Maxwell: in thermal equilibrium at temperature T, energy is shared equally among every quadratic term (mode) in the total energy expression, each mode carrying average energy (1/2)k_B T. This applies to translational, rotational, and vibrational modes alike.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Vibration counts double","body":"A vibrational mode contributes two quadratic terms — kinetic energy (1/2)m(dy/dt)^2 and potential energy (1/2)ky^2 — so each vibrational frequency contributes 2 x (1/2)k_B T = k_B T to the total energy, twice what a translational or rotational degree of freedom gives.","visual":"🎵"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The law of equipartition of energy states that in thermal equilibrium...","options":["energy is shared equally among all degrees of freedom, each getting average energy (1/2)k_B T","all the energy goes into translational motion only","heavier molecules get more energy than lighter ones","rotational energy is always twice translational energy"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is Maxwell's law of equipartition: every quadratic energy term averages to (1/2)k_B T."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A rigid diatomic molecule (3 translational + 2 rotational degrees of freedom) has how many total degrees of freedom?","answer":5,"explain":"3 translational plus 2 rotational degrees of freedom gives 5 total, as described for a rigid diatomic like O2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many quadratic energy terms (kinetic + potential) does a single vibrational mode contribute to a molecule's total energy?","answer":2,"explain":"A vibrational mode has both a kinetic term and a potential term, unlike translation or rotation which contribute only one term each."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Per the law of equipartition, each degree of freedom contributes how many units of k_B T to the average energy (as a decimal)?","answer":0.5,"explain":"Each quadratic term (degree of freedom) contributes (1/2)k_B T = 0.5 k_B T on average."}]},{"id":"kt6","title":"Calibrating the Furnace","icon":"🔥","kind":"lesson","story":"The forge-keeper's furnace will only heat a gas correctly if you feed it the right amount of energy per degree. Get the molar specific heats wrong and the flame gutters out.","teach":[{"title":"Monatomic gases","body":"With only 3 translational degrees of freedom, a mole of monatomic gas has internal energy U = (3/2)RT, giving Cv = (3/2)R = 12.5 J/mol/K, Cp = Cv + R = (5/2)R = 20.8 J/mol/K, and gamma = Cp/Cv = 5/3 = 1.67.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Diatomic gases","body":"A rigid diatomic molecule has 5 degrees of freedom (3 translational + 2 rotational), so U = (5/2)RT, giving Cv = (5/2)R = 20.8 J/mol/K, Cp = (7/2)R = 29.1 J/mol/K, and gamma = 7/5 = 1.40. Adding a vibrational mode raises Cv to (7/2)R.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Cp - Cv = R, always","body":"For any ideal gas — monatomic, diatomic, or polyatomic — the relation Cp - Cv = R holds exactly, since extra heat at constant pressure must also do work expanding the gas.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Specific heat of solids","body":"Modelling each atom in a solid as vibrating in 3 dimensions (each dimension giving 2 quadratic terms, kBT) gives average energy 3kBT per atom, so U = 3RT per mole and predicted molar specific heat C = 3R = 24.9 J/mol/K — matching most solids at room temperature (carbon is an exception).","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Heating a cylinder of helium","body":"A 44.8-litre cylinder of helium at STP holds 2 mol (since 1 mol occupies 22.4 L). Being monatomic, Cv = (3/2)R. To raise its temperature by 15.0C at fixed volume: heat = 2 x 1.5R x 15.0 = 45R = 45 x 8.31 = 374 J.","visual":"🎈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the molar specific heat at constant volume, Cv, for a monatomic ideal gas, in J/mol/K?","answer":12.5,"explain":"Cv = (3/2)R = (3/2)(8.31) = 12.5 J/mol/K for a monatomic gas."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is gamma = Cp/Cv for a rigid diatomic ideal gas?","answer":1.4,"explain":"For a rigid diatomic gas, Cv = (5/2)R and Cp = (7/2)R, so gamma = 7/5 = 1.40."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For any ideal gas (monatomic, diatomic, or polyatomic), Cp - Cv equals...","options":["R, the universal gas constant","zero","2R","it depends on the gas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Cp - Cv = R holds for every ideal gas regardless of how many degrees of freedom it has."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 44.8-litre cylinder of helium (2 mol) at STP is heated by 15.0C at constant volume (Cv = 1.5R, R = 8.31 J/mol/K). How much heat (in J) is required?","answer":374,"explain":"Heat = 2 mol x 1.5R x 15.0C = 45R = 45 x 8.31 = 374 J, as worked out in Example 12.8."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using the equipartition prediction C = 3R for a solid (R = 8.31 J/mol/K), what is the predicted molar specific heat, in J/mol/K?","answer":24.93,"explain":"C = 3R = 3 x 8.31 = 24.93 J/mol/K, matching the Dulong-and-Petit-like result in the chapter."}]},{"id":"kt7","title":"The Foggy Maze","icon":"🌫️","kind":"lesson","story":"A hall fills with drifting molecular fog. Though every particle races along at hundreds of metres per second, the fog itself creeps forward achingly slowly — because no molecule travels far before it collides with another.","teach":[{"title":"Why gas diffuses slowly","body":"Molecules move at speeds near that of sound, yet gas leaking in a kitchen takes a long time to spread across a room. Because molecules have finite size, they collide constantly, and their paths keep getting deflected — so real progress across a room is slow.","visual":"🌫️"},{"title":"Defining mean free path","body":"Treating molecules as spheres of diameter d, a molecule sweeps out a collision volume as it moves, giving mean free path l = 1/(sqrt(2) n pi d^2), where n is the number density of molecules — the average distance travelled between two successive collisions.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Mean free path of air","body":"For air at STP, with n = 2.7x10^25 per m3 and molecular diameter d = 2x10^-10 m, the collision time is tau = 6.1x10^-10 s and the mean free path is l = 2.9x10^-7 m — about 1500 times the molecular diameter.","visual":"💨"},{"title":"Water vapour at 373 K","body":"For water vapour at 373 K, the lower number density (n = 2x10^25 per m3, since n scales inversely with T) gives a mean free path of about 4x10^-7 m — roughly 100 times the interatomic distance of about 40 angstrom found earlier.","visual":"♨️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The mean free path of a gas molecule is defined as...","options":["the average distance a molecule travels between two successive collisions","the total distance a molecule travels in one second","the diameter of a single molecule","the distance between the container walls"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mean free path l is the average distance covered by a molecule between successive collisions."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For air molecules at STP (n = 2.7x10^25 /m3, d = 2x10^-10 m), the mean free path is about how many x10^-7 m?","answer":2.9,"explain":"The chapter computes l = 2.9x10^-7 m for air molecules at STP."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For those same air molecules at STP, the collision time tau is about how many x10^-10 s?","answer":6.1,"explain":"tau = 1/(n pi <v> d^2) works out to 6.1x10^-10 s for air at STP."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For a water molecule in water vapour at 373 K (n about 2x10^25 /m3), the mean free path is about how many x10^-7 m?","answer":4,"explain":"Example 12.9 gives a mean free path of about 4x10^-7 m for water vapour at 373 K."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The mean free path l = 1/(sqrt(2) n pi d^2) depends on the number density n and diameter d of the molecules as...","options":["inversely on n and on d^2","directly on both n and d","only on d, not on n","only on n, not on d"],"answer":0,"explain":"l is inversely proportional to n (more molecules per volume means more collisions) and to d^2 (bigger molecules collide more often)."}]},{"id":"kt-boss","title":"Duel with the Sorting Demon","icon":"👿","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the ruins waits a demon who claims it can sort every fast molecule from every slow one without doing any work — defying everything you've learned about gases. Only mastery of the full kinetic theory can prove it wrong and pass.","boss":{"name":"The Sorting Demon","taunt":"I need no energy to separate your fast molecules from your slow ones — prove to me you even understand what 'fast' and 'slow' mean down here!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything you've learned","body":"The ideal gas equation PV = mu RT = k_B N T, the kinetic pressure formula P = (1/3) n m <v^2>, the kinetic meaning of temperature (3/2)k_B T, the law of equipartition of energy, the specific heats it predicts, and the mean free path between collisions — all fit together into one consistent picture of a gas as molecules in ceaseless random motion.","visual":"💨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The equation PV = mu RT, relating pressure, volume, and absolute temperature of mu moles of gas, is known as...","options":["the ideal gas equation","Newton's second law","the law of equipartition","Dalton's law"],"answer":0,"explain":"PV = muRT = k_B NT is the ideal gas equation given in the chapter's summary."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the value of the Boltzmann constant k_B, in units of 10^-23 J/K?","answer":1.38,"explain":"k_B = R/N_A = 1.38x10^-23 J/K."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Kinetic theory derives the pressure of an ideal gas as...","options":["P = (1/3) n m <v^2>","P = n k_B / T","P = (3/2) n m <v>","P = 2 n m <v^2>"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the core kinetic-theory pressure formula derived from molecules colliding with the container walls."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the root-mean-square speed (in m/s) of a nitrogen molecule at T = 300 K?","answer":516,"explain":"v_rms = sqrt(3k_BT/m) = 516 m/s for nitrogen at 300 K, as computed in the chapter."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The law of equipartition of energy assigns average energy (1/2)k_B T to...","options":["every quadratic (translational, rotational, or vibrational) term in a molecule's energy","only the translational motion of a molecule","only monatomic gases","only the potential energy of a solid"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every independent quadratic term in the energy expression — translational, rotational, or vibrational — gets (1/2)k_B T on average."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is gamma = Cp/Cv for a monatomic ideal gas?","answer":1.67,"explain":"gamma = (5/2)R / (3/2)R = 5/3 = 1.67 for a monatomic gas."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the mean free path of air molecules at STP, in units of 10^-7 m?","answer":2.9,"explain":"The chapter computes l = 2.9x10^-7 m for air molecules at STP, about 1500 times the molecular diameter."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Avogadro's hypothesis — that equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain the same number of molecules — follows from the fact that...","options":["the Boltzmann constant k_B in PV = NkT is the same for every gas","all gases have the same molecular mass","all gases are monatomic","pressure is unrelated to the number of molecules"],"answer":0,"explain":"Since k_B is universal (the same for all gases), if P, V, and T are the same, N must be the same too — this is Avogadro's hypothesis, justified by kinetic theory."}]}]},{"id":"oscillations11","index":12,"name":"Oscillations","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 13","emoji":"🌊","palette":{"from":"#a78bfa","to":"#4c1d95"},"lessons":[{"id":"os1","title":"Periodic and Oscillatory Motion","icon":"🔁","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Rhythm Ruins, where every stone tile swings like a temple pendulum. The guardian will only lower the bridge for someone who can tell true periodic motion apart from mere repetition. Learn the rules of the cycle, or wait here forever.","teach":[{"title":"Periodic Motion","body":"A motion that repeats itself at regular intervals of time is called periodic motion. Planets orbiting the sun and a ball bouncing between your palm and the ground are both examples.","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Oscillatory (Vibratory) Motion","body":"Oscillatory motion is a to-and-fro motion about a mean position, like a swing, a wall-clock pendulum, or a boat tossing on a river. There is no sharp difference between 'oscillation' (low frequency) and 'vibration' (high frequency) — they describe the same kind of motion.","visual":"↔️"},{"title":"Equilibrium and Restoring Force","body":"At the equilibrium position, no net external force acts on the body, so if left there at rest it stays forever. Displace it slightly and a force appears that pushes it back toward equilibrium — this is what causes oscillations.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Oscillatory Motion Is Always Periodic — Not Vice Versa","body":"Every oscillatory motion is periodic, but every periodic motion need not be oscillatory. Uniform circular motion is periodic, since it repeats at regular intervals, but it is not oscillatory because there is no to-and-fro motion about a mean position.","visual":"🔄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement correctly relates periodic and oscillatory motion?","options":["Every oscillatory motion is periodic, but not every periodic motion is oscillatory","Every periodic motion is oscillatory, but not every oscillatory motion is periodic","Periodic and oscillatory motion always mean exactly the same thing","Neither periodic nor oscillatory motion ever repeats"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oscillation requires to-and-fro motion about a mean position, which is a stricter condition than simply repeating — so all oscillatory motion is periodic, but not all periodic motion oscillates."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A ball placed in a bowl, displaced slightly to one side, then released, oscillates because...","options":["a restoring force pushes it back toward the equilibrium point at the bottom of the bowl","gravity pulls it sideways out of the bowl","the bowl itself is vibrating","there is no force acting on the ball at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Displacing the ball from equilibrium brings a restoring force into play that tries to bring it back, producing oscillation about the bottom of the bowl."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is periodic but NOT oscillatory motion?","options":["Uniform circular motion","A pendulum swinging back and forth","A mass bobbing up and down on a spring","A tuning fork vibrating"],"answer":0,"explain":"Uniform circular motion repeats at regular intervals (periodic) but never moves to-and-fro about a mean position, so it is not oscillatory."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"An average human heart beats 75 times in a minute. What is its beat frequency in Hz?","answer":1.25,"explain":"Frequency = 75 beats / 60 s = 1.25 s⁻¹ = 1.25 Hz."}]},{"id":"os2","title":"Period, Frequency and Displacement","icon":"⏱️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Ruins, a chamber of shattered clocks ticks in a thousand mismatched rhythms. To reset the master clock and open the vault, you must measure exact periods and frequencies — starting with your own heartbeat as you sneak past the guardians.","teach":[{"title":"Period T","body":"The smallest interval of time after which a periodic motion repeats itself is called its period, denoted T, with SI unit second. Very fast or slow periodic motions use convenient units, like microseconds for a quartz crystal or years for Halley's comet (76 years).","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"Frequency ν","body":"The frequency ν is the reciprocal of the period: ν = 1/T. Its unit is named hertz (Hz), after Heinrich Rudolph Hertz, where 1 hertz = 1 oscillation per second = 1 s⁻¹. Frequency need not be an integer.","visual":"📡"},{"title":"Displacement, More Generally","body":"Displacement need not mean only position — it refers to the change with time of any physical property. For a spring-block system it's distance from equilibrium; for a pendulum it's the angle from the vertical; it can even be a voltage in an AC circuit or a pressure variation in a sound wave.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Sinusoidal Functions and Fourier","body":"One of the simplest periodic functions is f(t) = A cos ωt, with period T = 2π/ω. The French mathematician Fourier proved that any periodic function can be expressed as a superposition of sine and cosine functions of different periods with suitable coefficients.","visual":"🌊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A heart beats with a frequency of 1.25 Hz. What is its time period in seconds?","answer":0.8,"explain":"T = 1/ν = 1/1.25 s⁻¹ = 0.8 s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The SI unit of frequency is the...","options":["hertz (Hz)","second (s)","radian per second","newton (N)"],"answer":0,"explain":"Frequency is measured in hertz, where 1 Hz = 1 oscillation per second."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For an oscillating simple pendulum, which quantity is used as the displacement variable?","options":["The angle θ the string makes with the vertical","The tension in the string","The horizontal distance to the wall","The speed of the bob"],"answer":0,"explain":"The angular displacement θ from the vertical, as a function of time, describes the pendulum's motion."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of the following functions of time is periodic?","options":["sin ωt + cos ωt","e⁻ωt","log(ωt)","1 + ωt + ω²t²"],"answer":0,"explain":"sin ωt + cos ωt can be written as √2 sin(ωt + π/4), a sinusoid with period 2π/ω. The other functions never repeat their values."}]},{"id":"os3","title":"Simple Harmonic Motion","icon":"🎵","kind":"lesson","story":"A control pedestal glows with the equation of a dancing particle: x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ). Only by naming its amplitude, phase, and angular frequency can you calibrate the pedestal and reveal the next passage.","teach":[{"title":"Defining SHM","body":"A particle oscillating between +A and −A is in simple harmonic motion (SHM) if its displacement from the origin varies as x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ), where A, ω and φ are constants. SHM is not just any periodic motion — its displacement must be a sinusoidal function of time.","visual":"🎵"},{"title":"Amplitude A","body":"The amplitude A of SHM is the magnitude of the maximum displacement of the particle; it can always be taken as positive. As cos varies between +1 and −1, the displacement varies between the extremes +A and −A.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Phase and Phase Constant","body":"The time-dependent quantity (ωt + φ) is called the phase of the motion, and its value at t = 0, namely φ, is the phase constant (or phase angle). Given the amplitude, φ can be found from the displacement at t = 0.","visual":"🕰️"},{"title":"Angular Frequency ω","body":"The quantity ω is called the angular frequency of SHM, related to the period by ω = 2π/T, with SI unit radians per second. Since frequency is 1/T, ω is simply 2π times the frequency of oscillation.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the SHM equation x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ), what is A called?","options":["amplitude","phase constant","angular frequency","period"],"answer":0,"explain":"A is the magnitude of the maximum displacement — the amplitude."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The quantity (ωt + φ) in the SHM equation is called...","options":["the phase of the motion","the amplitude","the frequency","the period"],"answer":0,"explain":"(ωt + φ) is the time-dependent phase; its value at t = 0 is the phase constant φ."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A body oscillates as x = 5 cos(2πt + π/4) (SI units). What is its amplitude in metres?","answer":5,"explain":"The amplitude is the coefficient of the cosine term: A = 5 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same body, x = 5 cos(2πt + π/4), the angular frequency is ω = 2π rad/s. What is its time period in seconds?","answer":1,"explain":"T = 2π/ω = 2π/(2π) = 1 s."}]},{"id":"os4","title":"SHM and Uniform Circular Motion","icon":"⭕","kind":"lesson","story":"The chamber ahead is lit by a spinning wheel casting a shadow on the far wall, and the shadow swings back and forth exactly like a pendulum. Figure out how uniform circular motion and SHM are secretly the same trick of geometry, and the shadow-gate will open.","teach":[{"title":"The Reference Circle","body":"If a particle P moves uniformly on a circle of radius A, the projection of P onto a diameter of that circle executes simple harmonic motion. This can be seen by watching a ball on a string swung in a horizontal circle, viewed edge-on.","visual":"⭕"},{"title":"x-projection Gives the SHM Equation","body":"If OP makes angle φ with the x-axis at t = 0 and P moves with angular speed ω, then at time t the projection P′ on the x-axis is at x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) — exactly the defining equation of SHM.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"y-projection Is Also SHM","body":"Projecting the same motion onto the y-axis gives y(t) = A sin(ωt + φ), an SHM of the same amplitude as the x-projection but differing from it by a phase of π/2.","visual":"🔵"},{"title":"Circular Motion ≠ SHM Force","body":"Despite this neat geometric connection, the force acting on a particle in linear SHM is very different from the centripetal force that keeps a particle in uniform circular motion.","visual":"⚠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The projection of a particle in uniform circular motion onto a diameter of the circle executes...","options":["simple harmonic motion","uniform circular motion","projectile motion","non-periodic motion"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the reference-circle connection: x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) is exactly the SHM equation."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In one reference-circle example, OP makes an initial angle of 45° with the x-axis. Expressed as a multiple of π radians, what is this angle (e.g. answer 0.25 for π/4)?","answer":0.25,"explain":"45° = π/4 rad = 0.25π rad."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The projection of uniform circular motion on the y-axis differs in phase from the projection on the x-axis by...","options":["π/2","π","2π","0"],"answer":0,"explain":"y(t) = A sin(ωt + φ) differs from x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) by a phase of π/2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A reference particle completes one revolution every T = 4 s. What is its angular frequency ω in rad/s (use π ≈ 3.14, round to 2 decimals)?","answer":1.57,"explain":"ω = 2π/T = 2π/4 = π/2 ≈ 1.57 rad/s."}]},{"id":"os5","title":"Velocity and Acceleration in SHM","icon":"💨","kind":"lesson","story":"A fast-moving dart trap fires in rhythm with an unseen oscillator. To dash through unharmed, you must predict exactly how fast, and how hard, the mechanism moves at each instant using the velocity and acceleration laws of SHM.","teach":[{"title":"Velocity in SHM","body":"Differentiating displacement x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ) gives velocity v(t) = −ωA sin(ωt + φ). Speed is maximum at the mean position (x = 0) and zero at the extremes of motion (x = ±A).","visual":"💨"},{"title":"Acceleration in SHM","body":"Differentiating velocity gives acceleration a(t) = −ω²A cos(ωt + φ) = −ω²x(t). Acceleration is proportional to displacement but opposite in sign — it always points back toward the mean position.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Phase Relationships","body":"Displacement, velocity and acceleration all vary sinusoidally with the same period T, but they differ in phase: velocity leads displacement by π/2, and acceleration differs from displacement by π.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Maximum Values","body":"The velocity amplitude is vₘ = ωA and the acceleration amplitude is aₘ = ω²A — the largest speed and largest acceleration the oscillator ever reaches.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A body oscillates as x = 5 cos(2πt + π/4) m. What is its displacement in metres at t = 1.5 s? (round to 3 decimals)","answer":-3.535,"explain":"x(1.5) = 5 cos(2π×1.5 + π/4) = 5 cos(3π + π/4) = −5×0.707 = −3.535 m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same body, what is its speed in m/s at t = 1.5 s (round to nearest whole number)?","answer":22,"explain":"v(1.5) = −(5)(2π) sin(3π + π/4) = 10π × 0.707 ≈ 22 m/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same body, what is the magnitude of its acceleration in m/s² at t = 1.5 s?","answer":140,"explain":"a(1.5) = −(2π)² × (−3.535) ≈ 140 m/s²."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where in its motion is the speed of a particle in SHM maximum?","options":["At the mean position (x = 0)","At the extreme positions (x = ±A)","Speed is constant everywhere","Speed cannot be determined"],"answer":0,"explain":"v(t) = −ωA sin(ωt+φ) is largest in magnitude when the sine term is ±1, which happens at x = 0."}]},{"id":"os6","title":"Force Law for SHM","icon":"🔧","kind":"lesson","story":"At the Forge of Restoring Force, a blacksmith challenges you to tune a spring-loaded blade so it always snaps back to true. Master the force law F = −kx and you'll earn the forge's blessing.","teach":[{"title":"The Force Law F = −kx","body":"Combining Newton's second law with the SHM acceleration a(t) = −ω²x(t) gives F(t) = ma(t) = −mω²x(t) = −kx(t), where k = mω². This force law is an equivalent way to define SHM.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Restoring Force","body":"Like acceleration, this force is always directed toward the mean position, which is why it is called the restoring force in SHM.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Linear Oscillator","body":"A particle oscillating under a force linearly proportional to x is called a linear harmonic oscillator, with ω = √(k/m). If extra terms proportional to x² or x³ appear, the system becomes a non-linear oscillator.","visual":"⚙️"},{"title":"Two Springs Example","body":"A block of mass m between two identical springs (each of constant k) attached to walls feels F = −2kx when displaced, since both springs act together to restore it. Its period of oscillation is T = 2π√(m/2k).","visual":"🌀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a particle in SHM, the restoring force F(t) is related to displacement x(t) by...","options":["F(t) = −k x(t)","F(t) = +k x(t)","F(t) = −k x(t)²","F(t) is constant, independent of x"],"answer":0,"explain":"F(t) = ma(t) = −mω²x(t) = −kx(t), where k = mω²."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A mass of 3 kg is attached to a spring of constant 1200 N/m. What is the angular frequency ω in rad/s?","answer":20,"explain":"ω = √(k/m) = √(1200/3) = √400 = 20 rad/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two identical springs, each of constant k, act on a mass from opposite sides. The net restoring force is F = −2kx. What is the coefficient of k in the effective spring constant (i.e., enter 2 for '2k')?","answer":2,"explain":"Both springs contribute a restoring force of −kx each, adding to a net force of −2kx, so the effective constant is 2k."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A system with a force containing extra terms proportional to x² or x³, in addition to the linear −kx term, is called a...","options":["non-linear oscillator","linear oscillator","damped oscillator","forced oscillator"],"answer":0,"explain":"Real-world restoring forces can have small non-linear terms, making the oscillator non-linear."}]},{"id":"os7","title":"Energy in SHM","icon":"⚡","kind":"lesson","story":"The Energy Vault trades kinetic sparks for potential glow as a pendulum swings across the room. To claim the treasure inside, prove you understand how energy shifts between motion and position while the total stays locked.","teach":[{"title":"Kinetic Energy in SHM","body":"K = ½mv² = ½kA²sin²(ωt + φ). Kinetic energy is zero at the extreme positions (where v = 0) and maximum at the mean position, where speed is greatest.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Potential Energy in SHM","body":"The spring force F = −kx is conservative, with potential energy U = ½kx² = ½kA²cos²(ωt + φ). U is zero at the mean position and maximum at the extreme displacements.","visual":"🔋"},{"title":"Total Energy Is Constant","body":"Adding K and U, the trigonometric terms combine to give E = K + U = ½kA², a constant independent of time — as expected for motion under a conservative force.","visual":"♾️"},{"title":"Energy Trade-off","body":"As the particle moves between the mean position and the extremes, kinetic energy increases at the expense of potential energy, or vice versa, while the sum always stays fixed at ½kA².","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1 kg block on a spring of constant 50 N/m is pulled to A = 10 cm (0.1 m) from equilibrium and released from rest. What is the total mechanical energy in joules?","answer":0.25,"explain":"E = ½kA² = ½(50)(0.1)² = 0.25 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same block-spring system, what is the potential energy in joules when the block is 5 cm (0.05 m) from the mean position? (round to 4 decimals)","answer":0.0625,"explain":"U = ½kx² = ½(50)(0.05)² = 0.0625 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same system, what is the kinetic energy in joules at that same point, 5 cm from the mean position? (round to 2 decimals)","answer":0.19,"explain":"K = E − U = 0.25 J − 0.0625 J ≈ 0.19 J."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the extreme positions (x = ±A) of SHM, the energy of the oscillator is...","options":["entirely potential energy","entirely kinetic energy","half kinetic, half potential","zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Velocity is zero at the extremes, so kinetic energy vanishes and all the energy is potential."}]},{"id":"os8","title":"The Simple Pendulum","icon":"🔔","kind":"lesson","story":"Legend says Galileo himself once measured a swinging chandelier here using nothing but his own pulse. Build and time your own simple pendulum to earn the key to the final chamber.","teach":[{"title":"Simple Pendulum Setup","body":"A simple pendulum consists of a small bob of mass m tied to an inextensible, massless string of length L, fixed at the other end, oscillating in a plane about the vertical line through the support.","visual":"🔔"},{"title":"Restoring Torque","body":"Gravity's tangential component, mg sinθ, provides a restoring torque τ = −L(mg sinθ) about the support, which tends to reduce the angular displacement θ.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Small-Angle Approximation","body":"For small θ (in radians), sinθ ≈ θ, which turns the pendulum's equation of motion into the same form as SHM's a = −ω²x, but for angular displacement — with ω = √(mgL/I).","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Period Formula","body":"Since the string is massless, the moment of inertia of a point bob is I = mL², giving the well-known formula for the period of a simple pendulum: T = 2π√(L/g), independent of the bob's mass and (for small angles) its amplitude.","visual":"⏳"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the length in metres of a simple pendulum whose time period is exactly 2 s, given g = 9.8 m/s²?","answer":1,"explain":"T = 2π√(L/g) ⟹ L = gT²/4π² = 9.8×4/(4π²) ≈ 1 m."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The period T = 2π√(L/g) of a simple pendulum (for small angles) depends on...","options":["the length L and gravitational acceleration g only","the mass of the bob only","the amplitude of swing only","both the mass and the amplitude"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mass cancels out of the formula, and for small angles the period does not depend on amplitude either."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The small-angle approximation sinθ ≈ θ, used to derive SHM for a pendulum, stays fairly accurate up to about...","options":["20 degrees","90 degrees","5 degrees only","it is exact at any angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text's table shows sinθ is nearly equal to θ (in radians) for angles as large as 20 degrees."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which component of the force on the pendulum bob provides the restoring torque?","options":["mg sinθ, the tangential component","mg cosθ, the radial component","the full weight mg","the string tension T"],"answer":0,"explain":"The radial component T − mg cosθ supplies centripetal force but no torque about the support; only the tangential mg sinθ produces the restoring torque."}]},{"id":"os-boss","title":"Chronos, the Phase Reaper","icon":"⏳","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Rhythm Ruins waits Chronos, the Phase Reaper — a being woven from every oscillation in the temple, daring you to prove you've truly mastered periodic motion before the final gate opens.","boss":{"name":"Chronos, the Phase Reaper","taunt":"Your rhythm is off, mortal — every phase, every force, every joule of energy will betray you before this clock strikes zero!"},"teach":[{"title":"Everything Is Connected","body":"Periodic motion, SHM, the reference circle, velocity and acceleration laws, the force law F = −kx, energy conservation, and the simple pendulum are all one connected picture of oscillation. Recall them all to defeat Chronos.","visual":"⏳"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement correctly relates periodic and oscillatory motion?","options":["Every oscillatory motion is periodic, but not every periodic motion is oscillatory","Every periodic motion is oscillatory, but not every oscillatory motion is periodic","Periodic and oscillatory motion are always identical","Neither ever repeats"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oscillation requires to-and-fro motion about a mean position, a stricter condition than merely repeating."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A heart beats 75 times per minute. What is its period in seconds?","answer":0.8,"explain":"Frequency = 75/60 = 1.25 Hz, so T = 1/1.25 = 0.8 s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which equation correctly defines simple harmonic motion?","options":["x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ)","x(t) = At²","x(t) = A/t","x(t) = A ω t"],"answer":0,"explain":"SHM requires displacement to be a sinusoidal function of time, x(t) = A cos(ωt + φ)."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A reference particle completes one revolution every T = 30 s. What is its angular frequency ω in rad/s (round to 2 decimals)?","answer":0.21,"explain":"ω = 2π/T = 2π/30 ≈ 0.21 rad/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A 1 kg mass sits on a spring of constant 50 N/m. What is the angular frequency ω in rad/s (round to 2 decimals)?","answer":7.07,"explain":"ω = √(k/m) = √(50/1) ≈ 7.07 rad/s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A spring of constant 1200 N/m holds a mass pulled to amplitude A = 2 cm (0.02 m). What is the total mechanical energy in joules?","answer":0.24,"explain":"E = ½kA² = ½(1200)(0.02)² = 0.24 J."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"On the Moon, g = 1.7 m/s². What would be the period, in seconds, of a 1 m long simple pendulum there? (round to 2 decimals)","answer":4.82,"explain":"T = 2π√(L/g) = 2π√(1/1.7) ≈ 4.82 s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At the mean position of SHM (x = 0), the acceleration of the particle is...","options":["zero","maximum","equal to ω²A","undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"a(t) = −ω²x(t), so at x = 0 the acceleration is zero — this is also where speed is maximum."}]}]},{"id":"waves11","index":13,"name":"Waves","subtitle":"Physics Chapter 14","emoji":"🌊","palette":{"from":"#22d3ee","to":"#164e63"},"lessons":[{"id":"wv1","title":"Ripples of Disturbance","icon":"💧","kind":"lesson","story":"You crouch at the edge of a still pond and drop a single pebble in. Rings race outward across the surface, yet a floating leaf near you only bobs up and down — it never drifts toward the shore. Something is travelling here, but it isn't the water.","teach":[{"title":"What Is a Wave?","body":"A wave is a disturbance that propagates through a medium without the medium itself flowing along with it. Cork or leaves on a disturbed pond bob up and down in place; they are not carried outward by the expanding ripple.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Three Kinds of Waves","body":"Mechanical waves (sound, water, waves on a string, seismic waves) need a material medium to travel. Electromagnetic waves (light, radio, X-rays) need no medium and all travel through vacuum at c = 299,792,458 m/s. Matter waves are associated with particles like electrons and belong to quantum mechanics.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"Energy Travels, Matter Doesn't","body":"A wave transports energy and information from one point to another, but the medium as a whole does not move with it. Wind is a real bulk flow of air; a sound wave is not — it is only compressions and rarefactions passing through still air.","visual":"🔋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When a pebble is dropped into a still pond, a cork floating nearby...","options":["Bobs up and down in place, not carried outward by the ripple","Flows outward together with the expanding circle of ripples","Sinks immediately because of the disturbance","Only moves if the pond is very deep"],"answer":0,"explain":"The disturbance propagates outward, but the water (and the cork) does not travel with it — only the pattern moves."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT a mechanical wave?","options":["Light travelling from a star through space","Sound travelling through air","A pulse travelling along a stretched string","A seismic wave travelling through rock"],"answer":0,"explain":"Light is an electromagnetic wave and needs no medium; the other three all require a material medium."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is transported by a wave from one point of a medium to another?","options":["Energy and information (the pattern of disturbance)","The medium's particles, permanently","Nothing measurable","Only heat"],"answer":0,"explain":"Waves carry energy and information encoded in the disturbance pattern, without net transport of matter."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the chapter, what is the speed of light in vacuum, c, in m/s?","answer":299792458,"explain":"The text states c = 299,792,458 m/s — the same speed for all electromagnetic waves in vacuum."}]},{"id":"wv2","title":"Sideways or Straight Ahead?","icon":"〰️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deeper in the ruins you find two relics: a taut cable that hums when plucked, and a long pipe of trapped air with a piston at one end. Both carry disturbances — but in very different ways. You must learn to tell a sideways shiver from a forward-and-back push.","teach":[{"title":"Transverse Waves","body":"In a transverse wave, particles of the medium oscillate perpendicular to the direction the wave travels — like a jerk sent along a stretched string. Because this requires the medium to resist shearing, transverse waves propagate only in solids (and, in a limited sense, on liquid surfaces), not through the bulk of fluids.","visual":"〰️"},{"title":"Longitudinal Waves","body":"In a longitudinal wave, particles oscillate along the same direction as the wave's propagation — like the push-pull of a piston in an air-filled pipe, generating alternating compressions and rarefactions. Because this only needs the medium to resist compression (bulk modulus), longitudinal waves can travel through solids, liquids, and gases alike.","visual":"🔊"},{"title":"Water Waves Mix Both","body":"Waves on the surface of water are a combination of transverse and longitudinal particle motion — particles move up-and-down as well as back-and-forth in loops. Capillary waves (ripples) are restored by surface tension; gravity waves are restored by gravity.","visual":"🌊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"One end of a long, stretched spring is displaced sideways, producing a kink that travels along the spring's length. What kind of wave motion is this?","options":["Transverse and longitudinal, combined","Purely transverse","Purely longitudinal","Not a wave at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The kink's sideways displacement is transverse while its travel along the spring involves the spring's own longitudinal coupling — both effects appear together."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Waves are produced in a liquid-filled cylinder by moving its piston back and forth along the cylinder's axis. What kind of wave is this?","options":["Longitudinal","Transverse","Both transverse and longitudinal","Neither"],"answer":0,"explain":"The piston pushes the liquid along the same direction the disturbance travels, so it is purely longitudinal."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Waves produced by a motorboat sailing across open water are best described as","options":["A combination of transverse and longitudinal motion","Purely transverse","Purely longitudinal","Not a wave at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water wave particles move in loops — both up-and-down and back-and-forth — so the motion combines both types."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ultrasonic waves in air, produced by a vibrating quartz crystal, are","options":["Longitudinal","Transverse","A combination of transverse and longitudinal","Not classifiable"],"answer":0,"explain":"Sound of any frequency in a gas is longitudinal, since gases cannot sustain shearing stress."}]},{"id":"wv3","title":"Reading the Waveform","icon":"📈","kind":"lesson","story":"An ancient scroll shows a single curling equation: y(x,t) = a sin(kx − ωt + φ). The atlas whispers that this one line encodes the entire shape and motion of a travelling wave — if you know how to read it.","teach":[{"title":"The Travelling Wave Equation","body":"A sinusoidal wave moving in the positive x-direction is written y(x,t) = a sin(kx − ωt + φ). Freeze time and it gives the wave's shape in space; freeze position and it gives the simple harmonic motion of one particle over time.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Amplitude and Phase","body":"The amplitude a is the maximum displacement of a particle from equilibrium, always taken positive. The full argument (kx − ωt + φ) is called the phase of the wave, and φ itself — the phase at x = 0, t = 0 — is the initial phase angle.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"Wavelength and Wave Number","body":"The wavelength λ is the minimum distance between two points of the same phase — for example, the distance between consecutive crests. It is related to the angular wave number k by k = 2π/λ, measured in rad/m.","visual":"〰️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a wave written as y(x,t) = a sin(kx − ωt + φ), what does 'a' represent?","options":["The amplitude — maximum displacement from equilibrium","The wavelength","The angular frequency","The phase constant"],"answer":0,"explain":"a is taken as a positive constant equal to the maximum displacement of the medium's constituents."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The wavelength of a wave is defined as","options":["The minimum distance between two points having the same phase","The maximum displacement of a particle","The time for one complete oscillation","The number of oscillations per second"],"answer":0,"explain":"By definition, λ is the least distance separating two points of identical phase, such as two consecutive crests."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A wave on a string is y(x,t) = 0.005 sin(80.0x − 3.0t), in SI units. What is its amplitude, in mm?","answer":5,"explain":"Comparing with y = a sin(kx − ωt), a = 0.005 m = 5 mm."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the same wave, k = 80.0 rad/m. What is its wavelength in cm, using λ = 2π/k? (round to 2 decimal places)","answer":7.85,"explain":"λ = 2π/80.0 = 0.0785 m = 7.85 cm."}]},{"id":"wv4","title":"The Rhythm of a Point","icon":"⏱️","kind":"lesson","story":"You pin your attention on a single bead threaded on the vibrating string, ignoring the wave's shape entirely. The bead swings up, down, up, down — a private rhythm hidden inside the travelling pattern.","teach":[{"title":"Period and Angular Frequency","body":"The period T is the time any one element of the medium takes to complete one full oscillation, related to angular frequency by T = 2π/ω.","visual":"⏱️"},{"title":"Frequency","body":"The frequency ν is the number of oscillations per second, ν = 1/T = ω/2π, measured in hertz (Hz).","visual":"🔁"},{"title":"Longitudinal Displacement, s(x,t)","body":"For a longitudinal wave, the same sinusoidal form is used but the symbol s(x,t) replaces y(x,t), since the particles' displacement is along the direction the wave propagates rather than perpendicular to it.","visual":"↔️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For the wave y(x,t) = 0.005 sin(80.0x − 3.0t), ω = 3.0 rad/s. What is its period T in seconds, using T = 2π/ω? (round to 2 decimal places)","answer":2.09,"explain":"T = 2π/3.0 ≈ 2.09 s."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"For that same wave, what is the frequency ν in Hz (ν = 1/T)? (round to 2 decimal places)","answer":0.48,"explain":"ν = 1/2.09 ≈ 0.48 Hz."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a longitudinal wave, which symbol does the text use instead of y(x,t), for displacement along the direction of propagation?","options":["s(x,t)","d(x,t)","L(x,t)","p(x,t)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text writes s(x, t) = a sin(kx − ωt + φ) for longitudinal displacement."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If you fix your attention on one particular location on a string carrying a travelling wave, that point's displacement over time is","options":["Simple harmonic motion","Uniform circular motion","No motion at all","Completely random motion"],"answer":0,"explain":"At a fixed x, y varies sinusoidally with time — each particle undergoes SHM as the wave passes."}]},{"id":"wv5","title":"How Fast Does the Pattern Move?","icon":"🏃","kind":"lesson","story":"Two snapshots of the same wave, taken a heartbeat apart, are pinned side by side. The crest has shifted — you measure how far it moved and in how long, and the wave's true speed reveals itself.","teach":[{"title":"Tracking a Fixed Phase Point","body":"To find a wave's speed, track any fixed point of the wave pattern, such as a crest, as it shifts by Δx in a small time Δt. The speed of that point — and of the whole wave pattern — is v = Δx/Δt = ω/k.","visual":"🏃"},{"title":"The General Relation v = λν","body":"Since v = ω/k, and ω = 2πν, k = 2π/λ, it follows that v = λν = λ/T. In the time for one full oscillation, the wave pattern advances by exactly one wavelength — true for any progressive wave.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Speed Belongs to the Medium","body":"The speed of a mechanical wave is fixed by the inertial and elastic properties of the medium, not by the wave's own wavelength or frequency. The source decides ν; the medium then fixes λ = v/ν.","visual":"🧵"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The relation v = λν tells us that","options":["In the time for one oscillation, the wave pattern travels exactly one wavelength","Wavelength and frequency are always numerically equal","Speed increases whenever frequency decreases","Frequency has no effect on wavelength"],"answer":0,"explain":"v = λν directly follows from v = ω/k combined with T = 2π/ω and λ = 2π/k."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"For a mechanical wave in a given medium, the wave speed v is set by","options":["The inertial and elastic properties of the medium","The amplitude of the wave","The frequency chosen by the source","The initial phase angle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The medium (e.g. tension & mass density, or bulk modulus & density) fixes v; the source only sets the frequency."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A sound wave has speed v = 340 m/s and frequency ν = 1000 Hz. What is its wavelength in metres?","answer":0.34,"explain":"λ = v/ν = 340/1000 = 0.34 m."}]},{"id":"wv6","title":"Tension and the Travelling Kink","icon":"🎻","kind":"lesson","story":"A guardian offers you a challenge: pluck the guarded wire correctly and its disturbance must race to the far end within a measured time. You'll need tension, mass, and length to predict exactly how fast the kink will travel.","teach":[{"title":"Restoring Force Meets Inertia","body":"On a stretched string, the tension T supplies the restoring force and the linear mass density μ = m/L supplies the inertia. Dimensional analysis combines these into the speed of a transverse wave, v = √(T/μ), and the exact derivation confirms the undetermined constant equals exactly 1.","visual":"🎸"},{"title":"Speed Depends Only on the String","body":"This speed v depends only on the string's own tension and mass density — not on the wavelength or frequency of whatever disturbance is sent down it.","visual":"🚫"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A steel wire 0.72 m long has a mass of 5.0×10⁻³ kg. What is its linear mass density μ, in units of 10⁻³ kg/m? (round to 1 decimal place)","answer":6.9,"explain":"μ = m/L = 5.0×10⁻³/0.72 ≈ 6.9×10⁻³ kg/m."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"That same wire (μ = 6.9×10⁻³ kg/m) is under a tension of 60 N. What is the speed of transverse waves on it, in m/s? (round to nearest whole number)","answer":93,"explain":"v = √(T/μ) = √(60/6.9×10⁻³) ≈ 93 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The speed of a transverse wave on a stretched string depends on","options":["Tension T and linear mass density μ of the string only","The wavelength of the wave only","The amplitude of the wave only","The colour of the string"],"answer":0,"explain":"v = √(T/μ) — speed is a property of the string, independent of the wave's own wavelength or amplitude."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A string of mass 2.50 kg under tension 200 N has length 20.0 m. How many seconds does a transverse jerk take to travel from one end to the other? (Find μ, then v = √(T/μ), then time = L/v)","answer":0.5,"explain":"μ = 2.50/20.0 = 0.125 kg/m; v = √(200/0.125) = 40 m/s; time = 20.0/40 = 0.5 s."}]},{"id":"wv7","title":"Compress, Release, Repeat","icon":"🔊","kind":"lesson","story":"In the sound chamber, air itself becomes your instrument — squeezed into compressions, stretched into rarefactions. Newton once tried to predict how fast this hum should travel... and got it wrong. You'll see exactly where, and how Laplace fixed it.","teach":[{"title":"Bulk Modulus Sets the Pace","body":"For longitudinal (sound) waves, the speed is v = √(B/ρ), where B is the bulk modulus of the medium and ρ its density. In a solid bar under only longitudinal strain, Young's modulus Y replaces B: v = √(Y/ρ).","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Newton's Isothermal Guess","body":"Newton assumed the pressure changes accompanying sound in a gas happen slowly enough to stay at constant temperature (isothermal), giving B = P and v = √(P/ρ). For air at STP this predicts only about 280 m/s — about 15% too low.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Laplace's Correction","body":"Laplace realised the compressions and rarefactions of sound happen too fast for heat to flow — the process is adiabatic, not isothermal, giving B = γP and v = √(γP/ρ). With γ = 7/5 for air, this correctly predicts 331.3 m/s, matching experiment.","visual":"✅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"One mole of air (mass 29.0×10⁻³ kg) occupies 22.4 litres at STP. What is the density of air at STP, in kg/m³? (round to 2 decimal places)","answer":1.29,"explain":"ρ = 29.0×10⁻³ kg / 22.4×10⁻³ m³ ≈ 1.29 kg/m³."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Using Newton's isothermal formula, the predicted speed of sound in air at STP is about how many m/s? (round to nearest 10)","answer":280,"explain":"Newton's formula v = √(P/ρ) gives about 280 m/s, roughly 15% below the measured value."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Laplace's adiabatic correction (v = √(γP/ρ), γ = 7/5 for air) gives a speed of sound in air at STP of about how many m/s? (round to 1 decimal place)","answer":331.3,"explain":"The adiabatic formula gives 331.3 m/s, matching the measured speed of sound."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why did Laplace correct Newton's formula for the speed of sound?","options":["Because pressure variations in sound propagate too fast for heat to flow, making the process adiabatic rather than isothermal","Because Newton forgot to include the density of air","Because sound waves are transverse, not longitudinal","Because temperature never affects the speed of sound"],"answer":0,"explain":"The rapid compressions and rarefactions of sound leave no time for heat exchange, so the correct bulk modulus is the adiabatic one, B = γP."}]},{"id":"wv8","title":"When Waves Meet","icon":"➕","kind":"lesson","story":"Two pulses race toward each other from opposite ends of a rope — one crest-up, one crest-down. You brace for a collision, but instead they pass calmly through one another, briefly vanishing to nothing at the exact instant they overlap.","teach":[{"title":"The Principle of Superposition","body":"When two or more waves overlap in a region, the net displacement is simply the algebraic sum of the individual displacements: y = y1 + y2 + ... + yn. Each wave behaves exactly as if the others were not present, and each pulse retains its own identity after they cross.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Constructive Interference","body":"Two harmonic waves of equal amplitude a and the same frequency, exactly in phase (φ = 0), combine into a single wave of amplitude 2a — the largest possible resultant.","visual":"🔊"},{"title":"Destructive Interference","body":"If the same two waves are exactly out of phase (φ = π), their amplitudes cancel completely and the resultant displacement is zero everywhere, at all times.","visual":"🔇"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two wave pulses of equal and opposite shape move toward each other on a string and momentarily overlap. What happens to them afterward?","options":["Each pulse continues on unaffected, retaining its original shape","Both pulses are destroyed permanently","They merge into a single pulse forever","They reverse direction"],"answer":0,"explain":"By the principle of superposition, each pulse moves as if the other were not present, so both survive the crossing intact."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two harmonic waves of equal amplitude a and the same frequency, with phase difference φ = 0, are superposed. What is the amplitude of the resultant wave?","options":["2a","a","0","a/2"],"answer":0,"explain":"In-phase waves add constructively to the maximum possible amplitude, 2a."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two harmonic waves of equal amplitude a and the same frequency, with phase difference φ = π, are superposed. What is the resultant displacement, at all times?","options":["Zero everywhere","2a everywhere","a everywhere","Undefined"],"answer":0,"explain":"Completely out-of-phase waves cancel exactly — this is destructive interference."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"The resultant amplitude of two superposed equal waves is A(φ) = 2a·cos(φ/2). If a = 0.02 m and φ = 0, what is the resultant amplitude A, in metres?","answer":0.04,"explain":"A = 2(0.02)cos(0) = 0.04 m."}]},{"id":"wv9","title":"Bouncing Off the Wall — Standing Waves","icon":"🎵","kind":"lesson","story":"The corridor narrows into a sealed stone tube. Every sound you make bounces back and collides with itself, weaving a pattern that never moves forward or backward — frozen loops of silence and sound, fixed in place.","teach":[{"title":"Reflection at a Rigid Boundary","body":"A wave reflecting off a rigid, fixed boundary suffers a phase change of π (180°); the net displacement at the wall stays zero at all times — like an echo bouncing back reversed.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Reflection at an Open Boundary","body":"At an open (free) boundary, such as the open end of an organ pipe, the reflected wave has no phase change, and the boundary point can move up to twice the amplitude of the incident wave.","visual":"🚪"},{"title":"Standing Waves","body":"A wave travelling right plus its own reflection travelling left superpose into y(x,t) = 2a sin(kx) cos(ωt) — a pattern that does not travel, with fixed nodes (zero amplitude) and antinodes (maximum amplitude), spaced λ/2 apart.","visual":"🎵"},{"title":"Normal Modes of a Fixed String","body":"For a string of length L fixed at both ends, only wavelengths λ = 2L/n (n = 1, 2, 3, ...) satisfy the boundary conditions, giving natural frequencies ν = nv/2L. n = 1 is the fundamental mode or first harmonic.","visual":"🎻"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A travelling wave reflecting off a RIGID boundary undergoes what phase change?","options":["π (180°), so displacement at the boundary stays zero","No phase change at all","π/2 (90°)","2π (360°), i.e. no net effect"],"answer":0,"explain":"A rigid wall forces zero displacement at all times, which only happens if incident and reflected waves differ in phase by π."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At an OPEN boundary, such as the open end of an organ pipe, a reflected wave","options":["Suffers no phase change, and the boundary can move up to twice the incident amplitude","Suffers a phase change of π","Is completely absorbed","Reverses its frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"A free boundary is not constrained to zero displacement, so the reflected wave returns in phase with the incident wave."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a standing wave pattern y(x,t) = 2a sin(kx) cos(ωt), the points where the amplitude is always zero are called","options":["Nodes","Antinodes","Crests","Troughs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nodes occur where sin(kx) = 0; antinodes occur where sin(kx) = ±1, the points of maximum amplitude."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A pipe 30.0 cm long, open at both ends, has speed of sound 330 m/s. Its first-harmonic frequency is v/2L. A source of 1.1 kHz will resonate with which harmonic number n of this open pipe?","answer":2,"explain":"ν1 = 330/0.6 = 550 Hz; νn = 550n; for νn = 1100 Hz, n = 2 — the second harmonic."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"If instead one end of that same 30 cm pipe is closed, its fundamental frequency becomes v/4L. What is that frequency, in Hz?","answer":275,"explain":"ν1 = 330/(4×0.30) = 275 Hz — and since 1100 Hz corresponds to the 4th (even) harmonic, which isn't allowed in a closed pipe, no resonance occurs with the source once the end is closed."}]},{"id":"wv10","title":"The Waxing and Waning Hum","icon":"🥁","kind":"lesson","story":"Two nearly-identical tuning forks ring out together. The pitch you hear barely wavers, but the loudness throbs — swelling, fading, swelling again — a slow drumbeat hidden inside the sound itself.","teach":[{"title":"What Are Beats?","body":"When two harmonic sound waves of close but unequal frequencies are heard together, we hear one averaged tone whose loudness periodically waxes and wanes. This throbbing is called beats, and musicians use it to tune instruments together until the beats disappear.","visual":"🎶"},{"title":"Beat Frequency","body":"The beat (waxing-and-waning) frequency equals the difference between the two source frequencies: νbeat = ν1 − ν2.","visual":"🥁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Two sitar strings A and B produce beats of frequency 5 Hz. When the tension in string B is increased slightly, the beat frequency DECREASES to 3 Hz. What does this tell us?","options":["The original frequency of B was lower than that of A","The original frequency of B was higher than that of A","A and B originally had exactly the same frequency","Tension has no effect on frequency"],"answer":0,"explain":"Increasing tension raises B's frequency. Since this reduced the beat frequency, B must have started below A, moving closer to it."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In that same scenario, if the frequency of string A is 427 Hz and the original beat frequency was 5 Hz, what was the original frequency of string B, in Hz?","answer":422,"explain":"Since νB < νA and νA − νB = 5 Hz, νB = 427 − 5 = 422 Hz."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two harmonic waves of frequency 11 Hz and 9 Hz are superposed. What beat frequency, in Hz, will be heard?","answer":2,"explain":"νbeat = |11 − 9| = 2 Hz."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The beat frequency produced by two waves of frequencies ν1 and ν2 is given by","options":["νbeat = ν1 − ν2 (the difference of the two frequencies)","νbeat = ν1 + ν2","νbeat = ν1 × ν2","νbeat = ν1 / ν2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Beats arise at a frequency equal to the difference of the two close source frequencies."}]},{"id":"wv-boss","title":"The Final Resonance","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"Every corridor of this last Physics chapter — strings, springs, air columns, colliding pulses — has led here. A vast standing wave guards the exit, its nodes and antinodes locked in place, waiting to see if you truly understand what makes a wave a wave.","boss":{"name":"Resonantia, Warden of the Final Frequency","taunt":"Every wave you have ever ridden through this book — string, spring, air, echo — collapses into one truth right here: master the pattern, or be lost in the noise forever."},"teach":[{"title":"The Chapter, in One Breath","body":"Waves carry energy through a medium via oscillations — transverse (perpendicular) or longitudinal (parallel) to their direction of travel — described by y(x,t) = a sin(kx − ωt + φ), moving at v = λν, and combining by the principle of superposition into interference, standing waves, and beats.","visual":"🌊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A wave is fundamentally a disturbance that transports...","options":["Energy and information, not matter","Matter, but not energy","Nothing measurable","Only sound, specifically"],"answer":0,"explain":"The medium as a whole does not travel with the wave — only the pattern (energy and information) does."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sound waves travelling through air are classified as","options":["Longitudinal mechanical waves","Transverse mechanical waves","Electromagnetic waves","Matter waves"],"answer":0,"explain":"Air can only sustain compressional (longitudinal) disturbances, since gases cannot resist shearing stress."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A wave has wavelength 0.5 m and speed 340 m/s (the speed of sound in air used throughout this chapter). What is its frequency in Hz?","answer":680,"explain":"ν = v/λ = 340/0.5 = 680 Hz."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"A steel wire has linear mass density 6.9×10⁻³ kg/m under tension 60 N. What is the wave speed on it, in m/s? (round to nearest whole number)","answer":93,"explain":"v = √(T/μ) = √(60/6.9×10⁻³) ≈ 93 m/s."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which boundary condition produces a phase change of π upon reflection?","options":["A rigid (fixed) boundary","An open (free) boundary","No boundary at all","Any boundary, always"],"answer":0,"explain":"A rigid wall must stay at zero displacement, forcing the reflected wave to differ from the incident wave by phase π."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A string fixed at both ends, of length L, vibrating in its fundamental mode (first harmonic), has a wavelength of","options":["2L","L","L/2","4L"],"answer":0,"explain":"For n = 1 in λ = 2L/n, the fundamental wavelength is λ = 2L."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A pipe open at BOTH ends supports which harmonics?","options":["All harmonics — 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, ...","Only odd harmonics","Only even harmonics","No harmonics at all"],"answer":0,"explain":"Both ends are antinodes, so νn = nv/2L for every positive integer n — all harmonics are allowed."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Two sitar strings A and B, playing the note 'Ga', produce beats of frequency 6 Hz. When the tension in string A is slightly REDUCED, the beat frequency decreases to 3 Hz. If the frequency of A is 324 Hz, what is the frequency of B, in Hz?","answer":318,"explain":"Reducing tension lowers νA. Since this reduced the beat frequency, A must have started above B, so νB = 324 − 6 = 318 Hz."}]}]}];